3100:
** μᾰθητ-εύω**,
**to be pupil**, τινι **to** one, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.832c.}}
**II** _transitive_, **make a disciple of, instruct**, πάντα τὰ ἔθνη {{NT|NT.Matt.28.19, cf. NT.Act.14.21}}:—_passive_, {{NT|NT.Matt.13.52.}}

3105:
** μαίνομαι**,
_future_ μᾰνοῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.109, μᾰνήσομαι “Anthologia Graeca” 11.216 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus), 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.118 (neither found in _Attic dialect_)}}: _perfect_ with _present_ sense μέμηνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 977, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 879, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristophanes Byzantinus Philosophus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.586f}}; _Doric dialect_ μέμηνα uncertain in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 68}}; also in _passive_ form μεμάνημαι [ᾰ] {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.31:}}_aorist_ _passive_ ἐμάνην, _participle_ μᾰνείς, _infinitive_ μᾰνῆναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.30, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1295}}: also _aorist_ _middle_ ἐμηνάμην {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“CPHerm.” 7.18 (3rd c.AD)}}; _poetry_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἐμήναο probably in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus 1.61}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ μήνατο {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 20.34}}; _participle_ μηνάμενος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.35 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus):—on the _active_ forms, see below 11.— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} uses only _present_ and _imperfect_:—**rage, be furious**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”}} frequently of martial rage, μαίνεσθαι ἐάσομεν οὖλον Ἄρηα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.717, compare 6.101, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.350, etc.; χεῖρες ἄαπτοι μαίνονται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.245}}; μαίνεται ἐγχείη ἀπὸ λοιγὸν ἀμῦναι{{Refs|[prev. work] 75; δόρυ μαίνεται ἐν παλάμῃσιν 8.111}}; **rage with anger**, πατὴρ.. φρεσὶ μαίνεται οὐκ ἀγαθῇσι {{Refs|[prev. work] 360}}; ἐνὶ φρεσὶ μ. ἦτορ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 413; φρεσὶ μαινομένῃσιν 24.114; μαινομένᾳ κραδίᾳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 781, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 432 (both Lyric poetry); μανείσᾳ πραπίδι [prev. author] “Ba.” 999 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὁ μανείς **the madman**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 726; μ. καὶ παραπαίω 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 173e, etc.; αἱ τῶν μεμηνότων αἰσθήσεις Aristocles Epigrammaticus cited in 4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “Praeparatio Evangelica” 14.20}}; **to be mad** with wine, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.406, 21.298; μεμηνότες ὑπὸ τοῦ ποτοῦ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 18.2}}; of Bacchic frenzy, μαινόμενος Διώνυσος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.132; [Θυιάδες] μαινόμεναι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1152 (Lyric poetry); Διονύσῳ μαίνεσθαι 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 2.7.5; ἐπὶ τῷ Δ. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 223}}; ὑπὸ τοῦ θεοῦ μ. **to be inspired by.., driven mad by**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.79}}, compare μάντι; τὸ μαίνεσθαι **madness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1537}}; πλεῖν ἢ μαίνομαι I **am beside myself** with joy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 103, 751}}; of madness in animals, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.641c, +others}}; later simply, ={ὀργίζομαι}, μαινόμενος ὅτι.. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 41.11 (3rd c.BC)}}: frequently with words of manner, ὁ δὲ μαίνεται οὐκέτ᾽ ἀνεκτῶς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.355; τάδε μαίνεται 5.185: with _accusative_ _cognate_, μεμηνότ᾽ οὐ σμικρὰν νόσον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Pr.” [same place]; μ. μανίας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 793; μ. μανίαν ἐρρωμένην 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 22: with _dative_, μ. γόοισι φρήν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 967 (Lyric poetry); τόλμῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.24}}; πόνοις **at** or **because of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 562 (Lyric poetry); τοῖς εὑρήμασιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 465}}; ἐπί τινι (i.e. φιλοτιμίᾳ) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 535 (but ἐπί τινι}}, of love, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.31); ἀμφί τινι 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.33; εἰς τὴν ποιητικήν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.109; κατά τινος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Abd.” 1; ὑφ᾽ ἡδονῆς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1153.}}
**2** of things, **rage, riot**, especially of fire, ὡς ὅτ᾽.. ὀλοὸν πῦρ οὔρεσι μαίνηται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.606, compare 3rd-4th c.AD: Tryphiodorus Epicus 230}}; μαινόμενος οἶνος a **hot, strong** wine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 773d}}; of feelings, ἐλπὶς μαινομένη Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.77; ἔρις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 935 (Lyric poetry); ἄχεα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 957 (Lyric poetry); μαινομένᾳ ξὺν ὁρμᾷ [prev. author] “Ant.” 135 (Lyric poetry); σὺν μ. δόξᾳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 887 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** ἄμπελος μαινομένη, of a vine that is never done bearing fruit, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 846a38, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.18.4.}}
**4** μαινόμενα ἕλκη **malignant** ulcers, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 15.14.}}
**II** _aorist 1_ _active_ ἔμηνα, in causal sense, **madden**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 520 (troch., probably in “IA” 580 (Lyric poetry)), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 561}}; **enrage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.8:}}_present_ μαίνω first in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Hymni” 71.6. (Cf. μέμονα.)}}

3107:
** μᾰκᾰρ-ιος**, α, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 803c: collateral form. of μάκαρ}}, mostly used in Prose, but also in Poets, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus, and frequently in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}},
**1** mostly of men, **blessed, happy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.46, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 86, etc.; σοφοί τε καὶ μ. ἄνδρες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 335e; μ. τε καὶ εὐδαίμων [prev. work] 354a: distinguished from εὐδαίμων by 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1101a7, 19}}: frequently in phrases such as μ. ὅστις.. νοῦν ἔχει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 114, compare “Mon.” 357, 614, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.3}} W.: _superlative_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Piet.” 104: in addresses, ὦ μακάριε}} **my good sir, my dear sir**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 309c, “R.” 432d, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 219: with _genitive_, ὦ μ. τῆς τύχης}} **O happy you** for.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 186, compare “V.” 1512, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 303c; ἰὼ χελῶναι μακάριαι τοῦ δέρματος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1292}}; ὦ μ. σὺ τά τε ἄλλα καὶ αὐτὸ τοῦτο ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.39.}}
**2** **prosperous**, οἱ μ., opposed to οἱ ἐνδεεῖς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1157b21, +others; κινδυνεύω σοι δοκεῖν μ. τις εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 71a; τοὺς μ. καλουμένους ὁρῶ πονοῦντας ἡμῖν ἐμφερῆ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Kith.fragment” 1.6; μακαριωτάτην.. πόλιν Καπύην 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.91.6; ἐπιγονή “SIG” 695.48 (Magn.Mae., 2nd c.BC).}}
**3** of the dead, like{μακαρίτης}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 947e, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 488.9; μακαρίας μνήμης “BCH” 25.89 (from Bithynia), “Sammelb.” 4753.2, etc.}}
**II** of states, qualities, etc., μ. λέχος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1208; - ωτέρα πόλις [prev. author] “Tr.” 365; -ώταται τύχαι [prev. work] 328 (Lyric poetry)}}; βίος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 238, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 561d; τοῖς θεοῖς ἅπας ὁ βίος μ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1178b26; μ. διάθεσις 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 18; μ. ἐστιν ἡ τραγῳδία ποίημα 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 191.1}}; τὸ μακάριον **bliss**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1099 b2.}}
**III** _adverb_ -ίως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 909, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 629, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1324a24, etc.}}: _superlative_ -ώτατα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 733e.}}

3111:
** μάκελλον**, τό,
**enclosure,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 42(1).102.107, +others (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}:— also **μάκελλος**, ὁ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 137}}; **μάκελος**, ὁ, and _plural_ **μακέλα**, **μάκελλα**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II** **μάκελλος**, ὁ, = Latin _macellum, market,_ {{NT+1st c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 5(2).268.45 (Mantinea, 1st c.BC), 5(1).149 (from Sparta), NT.1Cor.10.25, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 61.18, “PLond,unpublished” 2487.43 (4th c.AD)}}:—hence **μακελλάριος**, ὁ, = Latin _macellarius_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 134}}; = **laniator, lanio,** {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}: _neuter_ μακελλάριον, τό, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1000 (6th c.AD)}}.

3112:
** μακράν**,
_Ionic dialect_ **μακρήν**, _accusative_ _feminine_ of μακρός used as _adverb_, **far**, μ. ἀνωτέρω θακῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 314}}; μ. λελειμμένος left **far** behind,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 857; οὐδέπω μακρὰν πτέσθαι σθένοντες 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 16; ἀπελθεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 438 (Lyric poetry); ἱέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.17; ἔστ᾽ οὐ μ. ἄπωθεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1184}}; τοὔργον οὐ μ. λέγεις the business you speak of is not **far** to seek, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 26}}: with _genitive_, **far from**, βαρβάρου χθονός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 629; κἂν ᾖ τοῦ γένους μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 192; τῶν πολεμίων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.50.8; οὐ μ. ἀπό τινος [prev. author] 3.45.2: in _comparative_, ἀποσκίδνασθαι μακροτέραν}} **to a greater distance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.98; πορεύεσθαι μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.11: _superlative_, ὅτι μακροτάτην}} **as far as possible**, with _genitive_ loci, {{Refs|[prev. work] 7.8.20.}}
**2** μακρὰν λέγειν speak **at length**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 713, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1259; μ. τείνειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1296, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1040; ἐκτείνειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 916.}}
**II** of Time, **long**, μ. ζῆν, ἀναμένειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 323, 1389 (Lyric poetry)}}; οὐ μ. **shortly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 850, etc.; so οὐκ ἐς μακρήν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.108, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 925, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 454, etc.; εὐθύς, οὐκ εἰς μὰκράν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.36}} (but, not **at length**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 25).}}

3114:
** μακρο-θῡμέω**,
**to be long-suffering**, ἐπί τινι towards one, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Sir.18.11, +others, NT.Matt.18.26; πρός τινα NT.1Thes.5.14.}}
**2** **to be slow** to help, {{NT|NT.Luke.18.7.}}
**3** **persevere**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.593 f.}}
**4** with _accusative_, **bear patiently**, {{LXX|LXX.Bar.4.25: _imperative_ μακροθύμει}} **bear up**! {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Astrampsychus Onirocriticus “Oracula” 90.7.}}

3115:
** μακρο-θῡμία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, **long-suffering, patience**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 549, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.4.10, NT.Col.1.11, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 6.1.5, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.1.}}
**2** **forbearance**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Pro.25.15, Aristeas 188. NT.Eph.4.2.}}

3117:
** μακρός**, ά, όν [ᾰ
by nature], **long**, whether of Space or Time,
**I** of Space,
**I.1** in length, **long**, δόρυ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.140}}; νέες, νῆες μ. **ships of war**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.21, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.41, etc. (_collective_ in _sing._, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 380); πλοῖα μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.30, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.14}}; ἐπὶ τὰ -ότερα measured by **the longer sides**, i. e. **length-wise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.50}}; τὰ μ. τείχη the **long** walls of Athens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.71, etc.; ἐν τῷ μακρῷ σκέλει τῷ πὸτ τῷ Ποτειδανίῳ “SIG” 247 iii 11 (Delph., 4th c.BC)}}; ἡ μακρά (i.e. γραμμή), line traced by δικασταί to indicate the heavier penalty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 106}}; ὁ μ. δρόμος the **long-distance** torch-race, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 1068.9 (Patmos, 3rd-2nd c.BC), +others, “OGI” 339.83 (Sestos, 2nd c.BC).}}
**I.2** in height, **tall, high**, μ. Ὄλυμπος, οὔρεα, δένδρεα, κίων, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.193, 13.18, 9.541, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.127, etc.}}; of a man, μακρότερον καὶ πάσσονα θῆκεν ἰδέσθαι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|8.20, compare 18.195; μ. πύκτης “PLond.” 3.1158.6 (3rd c.AD).}}
**I.2.b** reversely, **deep**, φρείατα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.197; φάραγξ 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 8.17.}}
**I.3** in distance, **long, far, remote**, κέλευθος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.358; οἶμος 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 290; ναυτιλίαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1; στόλος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 490}}; μ. ἐπιβοήθειαι **long** marches to aid, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.47}}; **remote**, ἀποικία {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 814}}; τὰ μακρότατα the **remotest parts**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.32: frequently in _neuter_ _sing._ and _plural_ as}} _adverb_, μακρὰ βιβάς, βιβάσθων, with **long** strides, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.213, 13.809}}; μακρὰ ῥίψαις, δισκήσαις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.45, “I.” 2.35; - ότερον σφενδονᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.16}}; μακρὸν ἀῧσαι, βοᾶν, to shout **so as to be heard afar**, i. e. **loudly**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.81, 2.224; μακρὰ μεμυκώς 18.580; μακρὸν ἠχεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 329a; κλάειν σε μακρὰ κελεύσας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 433 (see. κλαίω and below see)}}; later by analogy, μακρὰ χαίρειν φράσας τῷ ναυπηγῷ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nav.” 2, compare “Apol.” 3, +others, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 46.3}}; compare μακράν.
**I.4** generally, **large in size** or **degree, great**, ἤπειρος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 75; ὄλβος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.26; πλοῦτος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 130; τιμήματα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1278a23, compare 1297b4 (_superlative_); οὐσία [prev. work] 1290b16, 1321a11; μακροτέρα ἀρετά 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).13; ἐλπίσαντες μακρότερα μὲν τῆς δυνάμεως, ἐλάσσω δὲ τῆς βουλήσεως 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.39}}; μ. τραπεζῖται, perhaps **big** bankers, **Cat.Codex Astr.**{{Refs|7.222.}}
**I.5** _dative_ μακρῷ, to strengthen _comparative_ and _superlative_, **by far**, μ. πρῶτος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.34; ἄριστος μ. [prev. author] 9.71; ἀσθενεστέρα μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 514, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 66e; μ. μάλιστα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.171, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 30, etc.; κάκιστα δὴ μ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 895}}: also with Verbs implying comparison, ἀριστεύει μ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 890 (Lyric poetry), compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.2.}}
**II** of Time, **long** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”), ἤματα, νύξ, 10.470, 11.373}}; αἰών see reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.75; μ. χρόνος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, etc.}}; οὐ μ. χρόνου for no **long** time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1078, etc.; διὰ μ. χρόνου 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 741 (troch.); ἐν χρόνῳ μ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 88, etc.; δι᾽ αἰῶνος μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 582 (Lyric poetry); τὸν μ. βίον [prev. author] “Pr.” 449; τοῦ μ. βίου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 473}}; μηνὶ -ότερος **by** a month, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32}}; μακρῷ{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|(compare 1.5) πρότερον 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.958}}; μ. ἐέλδωρ a **long-cherished** wish, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.54}}; μ. γόοι, ὀδύρματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 375, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 297.}}
**II.2** **long, tedious**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.33, etc.; λόγοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1335, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.60, etc.}}; μακρὰν ἔοικε λέξειν (i.e. ῥῆσιν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 382; οὐδὲ εἷς Ὅμηρον εἴρηκεν μακρόν 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 97.7}}; μακρόν [ἐστι] with _infinitive_, Latin _longum est_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).56; μ. ἂν εἴη γράφειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 7.1}}. _adverb_ - ρῶς, λέγεσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 268}}: _comparative_ -ότερον, ποιεῖς you are taking **too long,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 48.4 (3rd c.BC),compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Philippides Comicus 21.}}
**II.3** Grammars, **long** in quantity, φωνήεντα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1458a11, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 15}}; μακρά (i.e. συλλαβή), ἡ, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 92.12; ἡ φύσει μ. [prev. author] “Adv.” 179.16}}: _comparative_, φωνήεντι μακροτέρῳ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1458a1}}; also μακρά (i.e. προσῳδία), ἡ, **mark of long quantity**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.113, 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus “Supp.” 674.7; ᾱ μακρόν 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 112. 6.}}
**III** _neuter_ with Preps. in _adverbial_ sense, διὰ μακροῦ (i.e. χρόνου) after **a long time, long delayed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 320, “Ph.” 1069}}; οὐ διὰ μακροῦ not **long after**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.15, 91, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.151b (also of place, οὐ διὰ μ. τῆς Ῥώμης 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.56); διὰ μακρῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 420.1; διὰ μακρᾶς 3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 69.1}}; but διὰ μακρῶν **at great length**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 449b, etc.; διὰ μακροτέρων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.106}}; μικρῷ διὰ μ. at somewhat **greater length**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1279b11.}}
**III.2** ὄλβος οὐκ ἐς μακρὸν ἔρχεται for no **long time**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.105}}; ἐς τὰ μακρότατα to the **utmost**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.31; see at {μακράν} 11.}}]
**III.3** ἐπὶ μακρόν **far, a long way**, πορεύεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.47}}; of Time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 255; ὅσον ἐπὶ μακρότατον οἷοί τε ἐγενόμεθα ἐξικέσθαι ἀκοῇ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.16, compare 2.34 (ἐπὶ omitted 1.171 codices)}}; τοσόνδε ἐπὶ μ. ἐπυθόμην {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.29}}; ἐπὶ μακρότερον **yet more**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.41.}}
**IV** regular _comparative_ and _superlative_, see above: irregular _comparative_ μάσσων, _superlative_ μήκιστος, (see entry).
**V** _adverb_ -ρῶς **at great length**, opposed to συντόμως, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1416b4}}; **slowly**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.51.2}}; μ. ἔχειν τοῖν σκέλοιν have **long** legs, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “de gymnastica” 31}}; of pronunciation, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 15; μ. ἐκφέρειν συλλαβήν 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 13.1.68: but the}} _adverb_ is usually expressed by _neuter_ μακρόν or μακρά, compare above {{Refs|1.3}}; μακρὰ κλάειν to howl **loudly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 211; οἰμώξει μ. [prev. author] “Av.” 1207, “Pl.” 111; ὀτοτύζεσθαι μ. [prev. author] “Lys.” 520}}; τί μακρὰ δεῖ λέγει; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 33.5; also by μακράν (see. entry); or by _neuter_ with a _preposition_, see above 111}}: for _comparative_ and _superlative_ of the _adverb_, see at {μακροτέρως, μακροτάτω}: _neuter_ _plural_ -ότερα as _adverb_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 250c, +others}} — Fem. μακρά not to be confused with μάκρα (which see). (Cf. Avest. **mas**-'long', Latin _ma?~Xcer._)

3118:
** μακρο-χρόνιος**, ον,
**lasting a long time, lingering**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 3.7; πυρετός 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(2).739 (_superlative_)}}; τὸ μ. **long duration**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 83.}}
**2** **dwelling a long time**, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.20.12, +others}}
**3** **long-lived,** {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Eph.6.3; βοῦς 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Vita Pythagorae - Porphyrii Opuscula” 24 (_superlative_).}}

3119:
** μᾰλᾰκ-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, (μαλακός) **softness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus}}“Aër.” 20: hence, of persons, **moral weakness**, opposed to καρτερία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1150a31, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.11, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.122, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.8, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 11.22, etc.; τῇ σαυτοῦ ζυγομάχει μ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 201.5.}}
**2** ={κιναιδεία}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.306, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “C. Gracchus” 4, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 58.4.}}
**3** **weakliness, sickness**, {{LXX+NT+5th c.BC+|LXX.Gen.42.4, NT.Matt.4.23, Pseudo-Herodotus Historicus “Vitae Homeri” 36, “POxy.” 1151.27 (5th c.AD)}}; μ. σώματος, opposed to ψυχῆς, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.30 K.}}
**II** **calmness of the sea, malacia ac tranquillitas**, {{Refs|Caes. “BG” 3.15.3.}}
** μᾰλᾰκ-ια**, τά,
**cephalopod mollusca**, i.e. **water-animals of soft substance, without external shells**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 523b2, “PA” 654a10, +others, Diocl.fragment 132.}}
**II** see at {μαλάχιον}.

3120:
** μᾰλᾰκός**, ή, όν,
**soft**:
**I** of things subject to touch, εὐνή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9. 618; κώεα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.38; τάπης μαλακοῦ ἐρίοιο 4.124; χιτών 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.42, “PSI” 4.364.5 (3rd c.BC)}}; πέπλοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.796}}; νειὸς μ. **fresh-ploughed** fallow, {{Refs|18.541}}; λειμῶνες μ. **soft grassy** meadows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.72, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.349; πόας ἄνθος 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 54; τάπητες.. -ώτεροι ὕπνω 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.125}}; of the skin or flesh, μ. παρειαί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 783 (Lyric poetry); χρώς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1403 (_anapaest meter_); σώματα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.1}}; πρόβατα μ. **soft-fleeced**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.52}}; τόποι πεδινοὶ καὶ μ., opposed to hard, rugged ground, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 607a10}}; οἱ κρημνοὶ οἱ μ.{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 615b31; μ. πέτρα “SIG” 970.8 (3rd c.BC), “PPetr.” 2p.6 (3rd c.BC)}}; μ. τέφρα a **slow** fire, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 89.36; so μ. πῦρ 1st c.AD: Andromachus Poeta Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.26; μ. ἀνθρακιά 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.76}}; ὕδωρ μ., of **marsh** water, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 192.8 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 59d (compare μαλθακός)}}; of soil, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 111b.}} _adverb_, μαλακῶς ἐνεύδειν, εὑδέμεναι, to sleep **softly**, i.e. **on soft bedding**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.350, 24.255; μαλακώτατα καθεύδειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.24}}; καθίζου μ. sit **softly**, i.e. **on a cushion**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 785; ὑποστορεῖτε μ. τῷ κυνί 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 90, compare 108}}; but ὡς μ. ἐσθίεις what **dainty** food you have! {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 2.10.}}
**I.2** μαλακά (i.e. σκεύη), τά, **household utensils**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Per.fragment” 3, 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 19.}}
**II** of things not subject to touch, **gentle**, θάνατος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.202; ὕπνος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.2; κῶμα 14.359}}; μ. ἔπεα **soft, fair** words, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.582, 6.337; λόγοι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.56; ἐπαοιδαί 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.51; παρηγορίαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 95 (_anapaest meter_); αὖραι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 20.18}}; μ. βλέμμα **tender, youthful** looks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1022; μαλακὰ φρονέων ἐσλοῖς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.95}}; μ. οἶνος **mild**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 873b34}}; μ. [φωνή] **soft**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aud.” 803a8 (_comparative_)}}; of scent, **faint, delicate**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.7.4}}; of climate, **mild**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 6.8.1.}} _adverb_ μαλακῶς, αὐλεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 803a20; ἐὰν τὰ σκληρὰ μ. λέγηται [prev. author] “Rh.” 1408b9.}}
**II.2** **light, mild**, - ώτεραι ζημίαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45.}}
**III** of persons or modes of life, **soft, mild, gentle**, μαλακώτερος ἀμφαφάασθαι **easier** to handle, of a fallen hero, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.373; ἐκ τῶν μ. χώρων μ. ἄνδρας γίνεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.122; - ώτερον ἐκ σκληροτέρου τὸ τῆς ψυχῆς ἦθος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 666b; ἀρνίου -ώτερος 4th-3rd c.BC: Philippides Comicus 29; - ώτερον τὸ ἦθος τὸ τῶν θηλειῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 608a25; ἀρρένων καὶ μ. ἠθῶν καὶ πράξεων 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.92 K.}}
**III.2** in bad sense, **soft**, μ. ἐν τῇ ξυναγωγῇ τοῦ πολέμου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.18; μ. ἦν περὶ τοῦ μισθοῦ [prev. author] 8.29; πρὸς τὸ πονεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.2}}. _adverb_ - κῶς, ξυμμαχεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.78}}; -ωτέρως ἀνθήπτετο attacked him **somewhat feebly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.50; μ. φιλεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.10.}}
**III.2.b** **faint-hearted, cowardly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.16 (_comparative_), etc.}}
**III.2.c** **morally weak, lacking in self-control**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.153 (_comparative_)}}; ἀντίκειται τῷ μ. ὁ καρτερικός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1150a33}}: with _infinitive_, μαλακὸς καρτερεῖν πρὸς ἡδονάς τε καὶ λύπας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 556c; τὸ τρυφῶν καὶ μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1455 (Lyric poetry)}}; μ. οὐδὲν ἐνδιδόναι not to give in **from weakness** or **want of spirit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.51, 105, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 488}}; τὰ μ. **indulgences**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 288, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.28.}}
**III.2.d** ={παθητικός}, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.54.11 (3rd c.BC), NT.1Cor.6.9, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 113.22, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.173.}}
**III.2.e** of music, **soft, effeminate**, μ. ἁρμονίαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 398e, 411a, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1290a28}}; **tuned to a low pitch**, opposed to σύντονος, χρῶμα μ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Cleonides Musicus “Introductio Harmonica - Musici Scriptores Graeci” 7, etc.}}
**III.2.f** of style, **feeble**, τὸ -ώτερον καὶ ταπεινότερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.197 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**III.2.g** of reasoning, **weak, loose**, λόγος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.4 (_comparative_), compare 5.149 (_comparative_)}}; λόγος λίαν μ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1090b8.}} _adverb_ -κῶς, συλλογίζεσθαι to reason **loosely**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1396b1 (_comparative_)}}; ἀποδεικνύειν -ώτερον {{Refs|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1025b13.}}
**III.3** **weakly, sickly**, -κῶς ἔχειν to be **ill**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 58, Pseudo-Herodotus Historicus “Vitae Homeri” 34, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 9.1; - κῶς διάκειται “PCair.Zen.” 263.3 (3rd c.BC).}}
**IV** _adverb_ -κῶς, see supr. I, II, III.

3122:
Included with: ** μάλα** [μᾰλᾰ;
but {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} sometimes uses the _ultima _ long before λ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.214, 4.379}}; before μ, {{Refs|10.172]}} _adverb_ **very, exceedingly**, prefixed or subjoined to Adjectives, Verbs, and Adverbs:
**1** strengthening the word with which it stands,
**1.a** with _adjectives_, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus most frequently, μάλα πολλά}} **very** many, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.1}}; μ. πᾶσα, μ. πάντα, **every one**, all **together**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.741, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.306, etc.}}; μάλ᾽ ἀσκηθής **all** unhurt, {{Refs|5.26}}; ἀβληχρὸς μ. τοῖος **quite** gentle, {{Refs|11.135}}; σαρδάνιον μ. τοῖον a **quite** sardonic smile,{{Refs|20.302; μάλα μυρίοι 15.556, 16.121, etc.}}; ἐμέο πρότερος μ. **actually** before me, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.124}}; later, μ. φιλόσοφοι, πλάτανος μάλ᾽ ἀμφιλαφής, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 126b, “Phdr.” 230b, etc.: strengthened, μ. δὴ πρεσβύτης}} **very** old, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.1; μ. γέ τινες ὀλίγοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 531e.}}
**1.b** with Advs., πάγχυ μ., μ. πάγχυ, **quite** utterly, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.165, 14.143; πάνυ μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 80c}}; εὖ μ. **right** well, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.190, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 92d, etc.; μάλα.. εὖ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.175, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 156a}}; μάλ᾽ αὐτίκα (see. αὐτίκα); μάλ᾽ αἰεί for ever and aye, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.557, 23.717}}; ἄχρι μ. κνέφαος until **quite** dark, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.370}}; μάλ᾽ ὧδε **just** in this way, {{Refs|6.258}}; μ. διαμπερές **right** through, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.362}}; μ. μόλις (see. μόλις); to express repeated action, μάλ᾽ αὖθις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1345, “Ch.” 654; μάλ᾽ αὖ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1410, etc.}}; μ. alone, ἔα, ἔα μ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 870; οἲ μ. καὶ τόδ᾽ ἀλγῶ [prev. author] “Pers.” 1045 (Lyric poetry); εἶα μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 460s q. (so ἄλλος πύργος.., καὶ ἕτερος μ. ἐπὶ τούτῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.181, compare 7.186): frequently after καί, αὗταί σ᾽ ὁδηγήσουσι καὶ μάλ᾽ ἀσμένως 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 728, compare “Ch.” 879: with _negative_, μάλ᾽ οὐ, μάλ᾽ οὔ πως, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.241, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.103; οὐ μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.93, 2.37, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 676 (Lyric poetry).}}
**1.c** with Verbs, μήτ᾽ ἄρ με μάλ᾽ αἴνεε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.249; μ. τ᾽ ἔκλυον αὐτοῦ 1.218}}; ἡ δὲ μάλ᾽ ἡνιόχευεν drove **carefully**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.319}}; μ... προπέμπει **in earnest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 915 (Lyric poetry), compare “Eu.” 368 (Lyric poetry); καὶ μ. δοκοῦντας φρονίμους εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.36; μ. πολιορκούμενοι [prev. author] “HG” 7.1.25.}}
**2** strengthening an assertion, νῦν σε μ. χρὴ αἰχμητὴν ἔμεναι, i.e. now **or never**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.492}}; τῷ κε μάλ᾽ ἤ κεν μεῖνε.. then **doubtless** he would have stayed, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.733}}; σοὶ δὲ μάλ᾽ ἕψομ᾽ ἐγώ yes **indeed**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.108}}; ἀλλὰ μάλ᾽ ὤφελλες why **plainly**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.472: frequently with another word, as ἦ μ. δή}}.. now **in very** truth, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.422, etc.; ἦ δή που μ. 21.583; frequently ἦ μ. (without δή) 3.204, etc.}}
**3** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus sometimes after εἰ, as εἰ μ. μιν χόλος ἵκοι}} if wrath come on him **ever so much**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.399, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.485, +others; καὶ εἰ μ. τις πολεμίζοι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.318}}; also μ. περ with _participle_, μ. περ μεμαώς though desiring **never so much**, {{Refs|13.317, compare 14.58, 17.710, +others}}; καὶ μ. περ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.217, compare 17.571; καὶ εὐρέα περ μάλ᾽ ἐόντα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.385.}}
**4** in _Attic dialect_ frequently in answers, **yes, certainly**, μ. γε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 555d, 564e, etc.; μ. τοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.46; καὶ μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 258c; καὶ μ. γε [prev. author] “Tht.” 148c, etc.; καὶ μ. δή [prev. work] 177a}}; καὶ μ. ἐπαύσατο **certainly** it stopped, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 189a, etc.}}
**II** _comparative_ **μᾶλλον** [ᾱ by nature, Hdn.Gr.{{Refs|2.932], _Ionic dialect_}}**μάλιον** [ᾰ] only in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 12.6, cf. Choerob.in “Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 2.240}}; late _Doric dialect_ **μαλλότερον** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|Pempelus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.25.52}}:—**more, rather**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.231, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.351, +others: mostly followed by ἤ, but in Prose also by a _genitive_, μ. τοῦ ξυμφέροντος}} **more than** is expedient, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.1; μ. τοῦ δέοντος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 487b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.8 (sometimes expressed by μᾶλλον alone, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 63d); οὐπώποτ᾽ ἔργου μ. εἱλόμην λόγους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 394}}; παντὸς μ. most assuredly, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 715d (see. below 8); in [prev. author] “Ap.” 36d, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὅτι μ. πρέπει οὕτως, ὡς.., ὡς is probably ={ἤ} (see. ὡς)}}]: denoting a constant increase, **more and more**, sometimes doubled, μ. μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1406, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1001 (Lyric poetry), 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 29}}: in positive sense, **exceedingly**, κηρόθι μ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.300, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.370, +others:}} —Usage:
**II.1** frequently strengthened by other words, πολὺ μ. {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.700, etc.; ἔτι μ. 14.97, +others; μ. ἔτ᾽ ἢ τὸ πάροιθεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.322; καὶ μ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.470; καὶ μ. ἔτι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.22; ἔτι καὶ μ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.57; ἔτι καὶ πολὺ μ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.386, 429, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 428; ἐπὶ μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.104; ἔτι ἐπὶ μ. [prev. author] 1.94; πολλῷ μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 80e, NT.1Cor.12.22: also modified, μᾶλλόν τι}} somewhat **more**, μ. τι περιημέκτεε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.114, compare 50, etc.; μ. ἤδη προσδεχομένου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.71.}}
**II.2** sometimes with a second _comparative_, ῥηΐτεροι μ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.243, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 673, “Supp.” 279, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1210, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 222, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 79e, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.14 (assuming variant), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 116b24.}}
**II.3** μᾶλλον δέ **much more**.., or **rather**.., to correct a statement already made, ὁ δεσπότης πέπραγεν εὐτυχέστατα, μ. δ᾽ ὁ Πλοῦτος αὐτός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 634; πολλοί, μ. δὲ πάντες 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.65, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 173e; so ἢ μ. 1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 20, 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 25.16}}; οὐχὶ μ. ἤ.. not so, **but rather** so.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.87.}}
**II.4** μ. δὲ καὶ ἡσυχαίτερα **more** or less **violent**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.82.}}
**II.5** οὐδὲν μ. none **the more**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 79; οὐδέν τι μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 87d}}; μηδέν τι μ. ἤ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 280.}}
**II.6** μ. ἤ.. followed by οὐ in comparisons, where preference implies rejection or denial, πόλιν ὅλην διαφθεῖραι μᾶλλον ἤ οὐ τοὺς αἰτίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.36}}: preceded by another _negative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.118, 5.94, 7.16. γ, etc.: by an _interrogative_ which conveys a _negative_ force, τίδεῖ.. μᾶλλον, ἤ οὐ..; 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.15.}}
**II.7** τὸ μ. καὶ ἧττον, a form of argument, **a fortiori**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1397b12.}}
**II.8** παντὸς μ., see at {πᾶς} {{Refs|III.4.}}
**III** _superlative_ **μάλιστα** **most of all, above all**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.; πᾶσι, μάλιστα δ᾽ ἐμοί 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.353}}; μ. μὲν.., ἔπειτα or ἔπειτα δέ.., **first and above all**.., next.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 647, compare “Ph.” 1285}}; μ. μὲν.., δεύτερον δὲ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.20}}; μ. μὲν.., εἰ δὲ μὴ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.22, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.40, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 590d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.25, etc.; τοῦτο δ᾽ ἐστὶ μ. μὲν θάνατος, εἰ δὲ μή, πάντα τὰ ὄντα ἀφελέσθαι [prev. author] 21.152}}; μάλιστα μὲν.., μᾶλλον μέντοι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 180b}}; μάλιστα.., εἰ μὴ δ᾽.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 617}}; δοκέων μιν μ. ταύτης ἂν πείθεσθαι **certainly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.53}}; τί μ.; what **precisely**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 448d, compare “Men.” 80b, “Smp.” 218c: with _genitive_ _partitive_, μ. πάντων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 327a, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.86}}; τὸ μ. πάντων the **supreme** reality, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.5.11}}; τὸ μ. εἶναι the **highest degree** of being, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.2.7.}}
**III.1** strengthened, ὡς μ. **certainly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 294, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 460a, etc.; ὅσον μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 524; ὅσα ἐδύνατο μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.185; ὡς δύναμαι μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 367b; ὡς οἷόν τε μ. [prev. author] “Grg.” 510b; εἰς ὅσον ἀνθρώπῳ δυνατὸν μ. [prev. author] “Phdr.” 277a; ὅτι μ. δύνασαι [prev. author] “Sph.” 239b; μακρῷ μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.171; πολλῷ μ. 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.42.3; παντὸς μ. 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.35, etc.}}
**III.2** with the _Article_, ἐς τὰ μ. **in the highest degree**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.20, 2.76, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.104, 8.6, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.212: without ἐς, φίλοι τὰ μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.147, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.92, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.62; τά γε μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 794d}}; εἰ τὰ μ. ἦσαν ἀληθεῖς if they were **ever so** true, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.95}}; εἰ τὰ μ. μὴ τινές, ἀλλὰ πάντες.. if (**to put an extreme case**) not some, but all.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 20.2; εἰ.. δοκοίη τὰ μ. [prev. author] 18.21}}; ἀνὴρ δόκιμος ὁμοῖα τῷ μ. as famous as he that is **most** [famous], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.118, compare 3.8; τοῖς μάλισθ᾽ ὁμοίως 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 2.24.}}
**III.2.b** ἐν τοῖς μ. **especially, as much as any**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.90, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 173b, etc.}}: with a _superlative_, ἐν τοῖς μ. ὠμότατος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 14.40; φιλτάτη καὶ ἀναγκαιοτάτη ἐν τοῖς μ. 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 4.}}
**III.3** added to a _superlative_ (see. μάλα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|11.2, πλεῖστον), ἔχθιστος μ., μ. φίλτατος, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.220, 24.334; μ. κῃ ἐμφερέστατα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.76; μ. φίλτατος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1421: to a _comparative_ (?), μ. δὴ ὀκνηρότεροι ἐγένοντο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55.}}
**III.4** μάλιστα for μᾶλλον, μ. τῆς κόρης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” [1594]; μ. ἤ ἐμοί 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.91.}}
**III.5** with numerals, **in round numbers, about**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.29, 92, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.31, etc.}}; πεντήκοντα μάλιστα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|is 49 in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.118; ἑκατοστὸς μ. 99t h, [prev. author] 8.68}}; ἐς μέσον μάλιστά κῃ **about** the middle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.191, compare 76; ἥμισυ μ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93}}; μ. σφᾶς μεσοῦν δειπνοῦντας that they were **about** the middle of supper, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 175c; κου μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.22; μ. πως 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.41.13.}}
**III.6** in answers, **most certainly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 827, etc.; μ. γε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 994, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 253; μ. πάντων [prev. author] “Av.” 1531; πάντων μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 453d; see above 1.4}}. (Orig. perhaps **fiercely**, compare μαλερός.)

