**1** strengthening the word with which it stands,
**1.a** with _adjectives_, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus most frequently, μάλα πολλά}} **very** many, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.1}}; μ. πᾶσα, μ. πάντα, **every one**, all **together**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.741, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.306, etc.}}; μάλ᾽ ἀσκηθής **all** unhurt, {{Refs|5.26}}; ἀβληχρὸς μ. τοῖος **quite** gentle, {{Refs|11.135}}; σαρδάνιον μ. τοῖον a **quite** sardonic smile,{{Refs|20.302; μάλα μυρίοι 15.556, 16.121, etc.}}; ἐμέο πρότερος μ. **actually** before me, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.124}}; later, μ. φιλόσοφοι, πλάτανος μάλ᾽ ἀμφιλαφής, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 126b, “Phdr.” 230b, etc.: strengthened, μ. δὴ πρεσβύτης}} **very** old, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.1; μ. γέ τινες ὀλίγοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 531e.}}
**1.b** with Advs., πάγχυ μ., μ. πάγχυ, **quite** utterly, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.165, 14.143; πάνυ μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 80c}}; εὖ μ. **right** well, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.190, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 92d, etc.; μάλα.. εὖ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.175, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 156a}}; μάλ᾽ αὐτίκα (see. αὐτίκα); μάλ᾽ αἰεί for ever and aye, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.557, 23.717}}; ἄχρι μ. κνέφαος until **quite** dark, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.370}}; μάλ᾽ ὧδε **just** in this way, {{Refs|6.258}}; μ. διαμπερές **right** through, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.362}}; μ. μόλις (see. μόλις); to express repeated action, μάλ᾽ αὖθις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1345, “Ch.” 654; μάλ᾽ αὖ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1410, etc.}}; μ. alone, ἔα, ἔα μ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 870; οἲ μ. καὶ τόδ᾽ ἀλγῶ [prev. author] “Pers.” 1045 (Lyric poetry); εἶα μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 460s q. (so ἄλλος πύργος.., καὶ ἕτερος μ. ἐπὶ τούτῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.181, compare 7.186): frequently after καί, αὗταί σ᾽ ὁδηγήσουσι καὶ μάλ᾽ ἀσμένως 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 728, compare “Ch.” 879: with _negative_, μάλ᾽ οὐ, μάλ᾽ οὔ πως, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.241, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.103; οὐ μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.93, 2.37, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 676 (Lyric poetry).}}
**1.c** with Verbs, μήτ᾽ ἄρ με μάλ᾽ αἴνεε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.249; μ. τ᾽ ἔκλυον αὐτοῦ 1.218}}; ἡ δὲ μάλ᾽ ἡνιόχευεν drove **carefully**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.319}}; μ... προπέμπει **in earnest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 915 (Lyric poetry), compare “Eu.” 368 (Lyric poetry); καὶ μ. δοκοῦντας φρονίμους εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.36; μ. πολιορκούμενοι [prev. author] “HG” 7.1.25.}}
**2** strengthening an assertion, νῦν σε μ. χρὴ αἰχμητὴν ἔμεναι, i.e. now **or never**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.492}}; τῷ κε μάλ᾽ ἤ κεν μεῖνε.. then **doubtless** he would have stayed, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.733}}; σοὶ δὲ μάλ᾽ ἕψομ᾽ ἐγώ yes **indeed**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.108}}; ἀλλὰ μάλ᾽ ὤφελλες why **plainly**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.472: frequently with another word, as ἦ μ. δή}}.. now **in very** truth, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.422, etc.; ἦ δή που μ. 21.583; frequently ἦ μ. (without δή) 3.204, etc.}}
**3** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus sometimes after εἰ, as εἰ μ. μιν χόλος ἵκοι}} if wrath come on him **ever so much**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.399, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.485, +others; καὶ εἰ μ. τις πολεμίζοι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.318}}; also μ. περ with _participle_, μ. περ μεμαώς though desiring **never so much**, {{Refs|13.317, compare 14.58, 17.710, +others}}; καὶ μ. περ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.217, compare 17.571; καὶ εὐρέα περ μάλ᾽ ἐόντα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.385.}}
**4** in _Attic dialect_ frequently in answers, **yes, certainly**, μ. γε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 555d, 564e, etc.; μ. τοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.46; καὶ μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 258c; καὶ μ. γε [prev. author] “Tht.” 148c, etc.; καὶ μ. δή [prev. work] 177a}}; καὶ μ. ἐπαύσατο **certainly** it stopped, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 189a, etc.}}

3125:
** μάμμ-η**, ἡ,
child's word for **mother**, ὦ μάμμη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 70, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 28; Σισύφου ὦ μ. “Anthologia Graeca” 11.67 (Myrinus Epigrammaticus), compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 176.}}
**II** **mother's breast**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.16.43.}}
**III** later, **grandmother,** {{LXX+1st c.BC+|POxy. 1644.12 (1st c.BC), 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.301, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agis” 4, LXX.4Mac.16.9, “SIG” 844 B 5 (Chaeronea, 3rd c.AD), etc.}}

3128:
**Μανασσῆς**, -ῆ, ὁ (Heb. מְנַשֶּׁה),
**Manasseh**;
1. (a) the first-born son of Joseph;
(b) **the tribe which bore his name**: Rev.7:6.
2. **King of Judah**: Mat.1:10.†
(From Abbott-Smith. LSJ has no entry)

3129:
** μανθάνω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.80, etc.}}: _future_ μᾰθήσομαι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 35, 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 1.31, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 926, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 667, “OC” 1527, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 53e, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ μᾰθεῦμαι uncertain in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.60; late μαθήσω 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.450, “Sect.Intr.” 9 (codex L)}}: _aorist_ ἔμᾰθον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.18, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ ἔμμαθον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.226, μάθον (see. below)}}: _perfect_ μεμάθηκα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 74, 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 10, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 17.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1148, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 9c, etc.:}}_pluperfect_ ἐμεμαθήκη{{Refs|[prev. work] 14c}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐμεμαθήκει {{Refs|[prev. author] “Men.” 86a}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ μεμαθήκεσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 10}}:—_passive_, used by good writers only in _present_, as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 87b, “Men.” 88b.— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses only the _Epic dialect_}}_aorist_ forms μάθον, ἔμμαθες, ἔμμαθε:—**learn**, especially **by study** (but also, **by practice**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 147, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1103a32, “Metaphorical” 1049b31, 980b24}}; **by experience**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 251 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Anthologia Graeca” 0.81a40), thrice in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἔργα κακά 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.226, 18.362}}: with _infinitive_, μάθον ἔμμεναι ἐσθλός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.444: frequently later, οὐδὲ μαθόντες γινώσκουσιν 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 17; ἀεὶ γὰρ ἡβᾷ τοῖς γέρουσιν εὖ μαθεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 584}}; ταλάντου τοῦτ᾽ ἔμαθεν **for** a talent, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 876}}; οἱ μανθάνοντες **learners, pupils**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.17}}; μ. τὰ Ὁμήρου ἔπη **learn by heart**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 3.5; μ. βέμβικα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1461}}; μ. τί τινος **learn** from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.80, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 701, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 575, etc.: with Preps., ἐσθλῶν ἄπ᾽ ἐσθλὰ μαθήσεαι}} Thgn.[same place]; μ. καθ᾽ Ὅμηρον Xenoph.[same place]; τι ἔκ τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 352; ἐκ ποίων ὀνομάτων μεμαθηκὼς ἦν τὰ πράγματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 438a; παρά τινος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 859, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1012, etc.}}; παρά τινος ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.67; πρός τινος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 12}}; πρὶν μάθοιμ᾽ εἰ.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 961}}: with _infinitive_, **learn to**.., or **how to**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” [same place], 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.284, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1068 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 667; μ. σεαυτὸν αἰς χύνεσθαι 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 244.}}
**II** **acquire a habit of**, and in past tenses, **to be accustomed to**.., with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 17.9, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 10; τοὺς μεμαθηκότας ἀριστᾶν [prev. author] “Acut.” 28}}; τὸ μεμαθηκός **that which is usual**, πρότερον ἢ ὕστερον τοῦ μ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mul.” 2.128}}; ἀργαὶ μανθάνουσι **acquire a habit** of idleness, {{NT|NT.1Tim.5.13.}}
**III** **perceive, remark, notice**, τὸ πλῆθος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.208; ἀλλήλους μ. ὁπόσοι εἴησαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.1.}}
**III.2** frequently with _participle_, μ. τὴν νέα ἐμβαλοῦσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.88; ἔμαθον ταῦτα πρησσόμενα [prev. author] 1.160; ἵνα μάθῃ σοφιστὴς ὤν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 62; μὴ μάθῃ μ᾽ ἥκοντα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 13, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1113}}; μάνθανε ὤν **know that** you are, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1342; διαβεβλημένος οὐ μανθάνεις 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.1; εἰ μὴ μανθάνετε κακὰ σπεύδοντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.39.}}
**III.3** with ὅτι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 394b}}; with ὡς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.34, etc.}}
**IV** **understand** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|cf (5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 277e), ὡς μάθω σαφέστερον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 767; ὄψ᾽ ἐμάθεθ᾽ ἡμᾶς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1345; τὰλεγόμενα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.15, etc.: with _dative_ _person_, εἴ μοι μανθάνεις}} if you **take** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 394c: frequently in Dialogue, μανθάνεις}}; **d'ye see**? Answ., πάνυ μανθάνω **perfectly**! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 195, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 84d. “Tht.” 174b; εἶεν, μανθάνω [prev. author] “R.” 372e, compare “Phd.” 117b, etc.}}
**V** τί μαθών..; frequently in questions, literal **under what persuasion, with what idea**? hence ironically for **why on earth**? τί δὴ μ. φαίνεις ἄνευ θρυαλλίδο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 826, compare “Nu.” 402, 1506, “Lys.” 599, “Pl.” 908}}; τί τοῦτο μ. προσέγραψε; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.127}} (sometimes with variant{τί παθών}; what possessed you to.. ? compare πάσχω); imitated in later Greek, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Fragmenta” 67}}; also indirectly, **because** (with a sense of disapprobation), ὅ τι μαθόντες τοὺς ξένους μὲν λέγετε ποιητὰς σοφούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 357; ὅ τι μαθὼν.. οὐχ ἡσυχίαν ἦγον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36b; σοὶ εἰς κεφαλήν, ὅ τι μαθών μου καὶ τῶν ἄλλων καταψεύδη τοιοῦτο πρᾶγμα [prev. author] “Euthd.” 283e, compare 299a}}: even with _neuter_ subject, ὅμως δ᾽ ἂν κακὰ ἦν, ὅ τι μαθόντα χαίρειν ποιεῖ {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prt.” 353d.}}

3130:
** μᾰνία**,
**A** _Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, (μαίνομαι) **madness**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.112, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 7.5, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 958 (Lyric poetry), etc.; πολλὴν καταγνῶναι μ. τινῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.133; μέχρι μανίας ἡ σφοδρὰ ἡδονὴ κατέχουσα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 45e; μανίη νοῦσος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.75: frequently in _plural_, Legal Solonis cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 46.14, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1231, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 879, 1057 (both _anapaest meter_), etc.}}
**A.II** **enthusiasm, inspired frenzy**, μ. Διονύσου πάρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 305; ἀπὸ Μουσῶν κατοκωχή τε καὶ μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 245a}}; θεία μ., opposed to σωφροσύνη ἀνθρωπίνη,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 256b, compare “Prt.” 323b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.16; τῆς φιλοσόφου μ. τε καὶ βακχείας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 218b.}}
**A.III** **passion**, ἐρωτικὴ μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 265b; μανίην μανεὶς ἀρίστην “Anacreontea” 59.2: frequently in _plural_, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.39, “N.” 11.48, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 835; ἐγγὺς μανιῶν ἐλαύνει [prev. author] “Heracl.” 904 (Lyric poetry)}}; μανίη τινός **mad desire for**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Hermesianax Elegiacus 7.85.}}

3131:
** μάννᾰ**, ἡ, μάννα λιβάνου
frankincense **powder** or **granules**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.68.6}}; but μ. λιβανωτοῦ **gum** of λίβανος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 35}}; μ. alone, of the powder, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 36, “Epid.” 2.2.18, 2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 7.21.8, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.722}}; ={λιβάνου τὸ λεπτόν}, {{Refs|“Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 108.}}
**II** **μάννα**, τό, = Hebrew **mān, manna**, [{{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Exo.16.35 (variant{μάν}), LXX.Num.11.6, +others, compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 3.1.6.}}]

3132:
** μαντ-εύομαι**,
_future_ -εύσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.154, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.38, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1367, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.46, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐμαντευσάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21a, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.107; poetry μαντευσάμην 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.31}}: _perfect_ μεμάντευμαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “P.” 4.163}}:—_passive_, see infr. IV: (μάντις):—**divine, prophesy**, τί μοι θάνατον μαντεύεα; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.420}}; τί νύ μοι μαντεύεαι αἰπὺν ὄλεθρον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|16.859; ἐτεὸν μ. 2.300: _absolutely_, οὐ γὰρ ἀπείρητος μαντεύομαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.170, compare 9.510, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.107}}; τινι **to** one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.178, etc.}}: with _dative_ modi, **draw divinations from** or **by means of**.., ῥάβδοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.67: with _accusative_ _cognate_, μαντεῖα μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 716 (assuming variant).}}
**2** generally, **presage, forebode, surmise**, of presentiment, opposed to knowledge, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 411b, “R.” 349a, +others; μαντευσόμεσθα τἀνδρὸς ὡς ὀλωλότος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Ag.” [same place]; ἔστι γὰρ ὃ μαντεύονταί τι πάντες φύσει κοινὸν δίκαιον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1373b7; μ. τὸ συμβησόμενον ἐκ τῶν εἰκότων [prev. author] “GA” 765a27.}}
**3** of animals, **get scent of**, κύων ἄρτον μ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.45.}}
**II** **consult an oracle, seek divinations**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.31, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.46, 4.172, etc.; ἐν Δελφοῖσι [prev. author] 6.76; ἐπὶ κασταλίᾳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” [same place]; περί τινος [prev. author] “O.” 6.38, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.36; ὑπέρ τινος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 431; ὁ γὰρ θεὸς μαντευομένῳ μοὔχρησεν ἐν Δελφοῖς ποτε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 159, compare “Av.” 593}}; ταῦτα καὶ μ. this is **the question I ask the oracle**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 346; ἐτόλμησε τοῦτο μαντεύσασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21a}}; μαντείαν μ. παρὰ τῷ θεῷ Aeschin. [prev. cited]
**III** later, of the god, **give an oracle**, ταῦθ᾽ ὁρῶ τὸν Δία ὑμῖν μαντευόμενον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.253, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 19, “Sol.” 9.}}
**IV** in later Greek, _active_ **μαντεύω**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 75, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Indica” 11.5, 2nd c.AD(?): Xenophon Ephesius Scriptor Eroticus 5.4, 4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 10.5, 14.34, 23.23: but}}_aorist_ _passive_, ἐμαντεύθη **an oracle was given**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.114:}}_perfect_ _participle_, τὰ μεμαντευμένα **the words of the oracles**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 45}}; τὰ ὑπὸ τοῦ θεοῦ μαντευσθέντα (sic) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).122.81 (Epid., 4th c.BC),compare 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 7.1.}}

3133:
** μᾰραίνω**,
_future_ μᾰρᾰνῶ {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 262, “epigram Gr.” 854 (from Delos)}}: _aorist 1_ ἐμάρᾱνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 140, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1328, etc.:}} —_middle_, _aorist_ ἐμᾰρηνάμην (see. below):—_passive_, _future_ μᾰρανθήσομαι {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Jam.1.11, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.691}}: _aorist_ ἐμᾰράνθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.212, 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 1231, etc.}}: _perfect_ μεμάρασμαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.99, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 25, μεμάραμαι}} (to be read -αμμ-) variant in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus [same place], 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 31}}: _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _pluperfect_ μεμάραντο {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 9.371}}:—**quench** fire, ἀνθρακιήν {{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium”}}[same place]:—_passive_, **die away, go slowly out**, of fire, φλὸξ ἐμαράνθη Il.[same place]; πυρκαϊὴ ἐμαραίνετο {{Refs|23.228, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 5.4 (Stat. Flacc.): distinguished from σβέννυσθαι}} as that which goes out of itself, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 305a11}}; of rays of light, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 862.}}
**II** later, in various senses, ὄψεις μ. **quench** the orbs of sight, S.[same place]; especially **waste, wither**, [νόσος] μαραίνει με {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 597 (Lyric poetry); γῆρας ἁμὲ μαραῖνον ταριχεύει 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 54; κάλλος ἢ χρόνος ἀνάλωσεν ἢ νόσος ἐμάρανε 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.6; μάραινε [αὐτὸν] διώγμασι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 139; πίνος πλευρὰν μ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1260; πάνθ᾽ ὁ μέγας χρόνος μ. [prev. author] “Aj.” 714 (Lyric poetry), 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 240; ἀδικία φθείρει [τὴν ψυχὴν] καὶ μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 609d}}:—_middle_, νέους ἐμαρήνατο δαίμων {{Refs|“IG” 5(1).1355 (from Abia)}}:—_passive_, **waste away**, καμάτοισι (variant{ὑπὸ νούσοις}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus [156.3]; νόσῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 203; τὸ σῶμα οὐκ ἐμαραίνετο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 270e}}; but also of a tumour, **disappear**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.84}}; αἷμα.. μαραίνεται χερός blood **dies away from** my hand, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 280}}; of a river, **dry up**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.24; μ. ἡ κίνησις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 901a26}}; of a musical sound, **die away**,{{Refs|[prev. work] 921b15; τὸ νοεῖν μ. [prev. author] “de An.” 408b24}}; of winds and waves, **abate**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 15, “Mar.” 37}}; of wine, **lose its strength**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.692d; κῦδος μαρανθέν 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 1231}}, compare 1127; μ. ἀκμή, δύναμις, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 2, “Caes.” 3; τῶν ἐπιθυμιῶν καὶ ὀργῶν μεμαρασμένων 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 3.26}}. (Perh. cf. Latin _morbus_; _signification_ {{Refs|11 may be the earlier in origin.)}}

3135:
** μαργᾰρ-ίτης** [ῑ], ου, ὁ,
**pearl**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 36, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 10.13}}: -ῖτις λίθος, ἡ, Androsth. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.93b}}; -ῖτις alone, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Isidorus Characenus Geographus 20: -ὶς λίθος, ἡ, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.53}}; -ίς alone, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.30.}}
**II** -ίτης χερσαῖος, an unidentified precious stone, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 15.8.}}

3139:
** μάρμᾰρος**, ὁ,
a **crystalline rock**, which sparkles (μαρμαίρει) in the light, μάρμαρος ὀκριόεις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.380, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.499, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 663 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1172 (Lyric poetry)}}: as _adjective_, πέτρος μ. ὀκριόεις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.735, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1401, etc.}}
**II** later, **marble**, μάρμαρον ἢ λίθον λευκήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.185, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 9: also _feminine_, μαρμάρου.. τῆς Πεντελικῆς μέταλλα 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.1.23; μ. λίθος [prev. author] 14.1.35: hence}}
**II.2** **work in marble**, i.e. **tombstone**, τυκτὴ μ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.211.}}
**II.3** **chips made by cutting marble** (_masculine_), {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.660c, 954a, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.79.}}

3140:
** μαρτῠρ-έω**,
_future_ -ήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.21}}:—_passive_, _future_ μαρτυρηθήσομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.13, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.40}}; μαρτυρήσομαι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus (see.below 9), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.37:}}_aorist_ ἐμαρτυρήθην: _perfect_ μεμαρτύρημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.16}}, used in _active_ sense, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.43.3}}:—**bear witness, give evidence**:—Constr.:
**1** _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 4.7, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).48; μαρτυροῦντι πιστεύειν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.7; ἐξέστω καὶ τοῖς δούλοις μαρτυρεῖν “PLille” 29.20 (3rd c.BC),compare “SIG” 953.19 (Calymna, 2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**2** with _dative_ _person_, **bear witness to** or **in favour of** another, **confirm** what he says, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 594, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.94, etc.}}; μαρτυρέει μοι τῇ γνώμῃ, ὅτι.. **bears witness to** my opinion, that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.18, compare 4.29; μαρτυρεῖς σαυτῷ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 532}}; especially **bear favourable witness to, give a good report of** a person, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 22.657 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; πάντες ἐμαρτύρουν αὐτῷ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Eu. Luc.” 4.22. b. with _dative_ _of things_, μ. τῇ διαθήκῃ “POxy.” 494.33 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**3** with _accusative_ _of things_, **testify to** a thing, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 102, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.108, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 515, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 244d; μ. τινί τι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.21, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 797 (Lyric poetry).}}
**4** μ. περί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21a; ὑπέρ τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.54.}}
**5** with _infinitive_, **testify that** a thing is, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 34, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1265, etc.}}; τίς σοι μαρτυρήσει ταῦτ᾽ ἐμοῦ κλύει; **that** he heard.. ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 422, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 977; ὁ κληθεὶς μαρτυρείτω ἀληθῆ μαρτυρεῖν “PHal.” 1.225 (3rd c.BC)}}: rarely with _participle_, μαρτυρεῖτέ [μοι].. ῥινηλατούσῃ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1184; μ. τισὶ παραγινομέναις 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.46.}}
**6** μ. τινὶ ὡς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 494, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 523c; σώματα.. ὡς ἔστιν, αὐτὴ ἡ αἴσθησις.. μ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.6U.}}; μ. ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.25.}}
**7** μ. τινὶ τῆς συμμαχίας **testify to, acknowledge the value of** his alliance, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 13.5.3.}}
**8** with _accusative_ _cognate_, μαρτυρίαν μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.25, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 399b}}; μ. ἀκοήν **give** hearsay **evidence**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.4}}; μ. ψεῦδος, ψεύδη, **bear** false **witness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Amipsias Comicus 13, 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 32.16; τὰ ψευδῆ 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.4; τἀληθῆ 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.46}}:—_passive_, μαρτυρίαι μαρτυρηθεῖσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.1; μεμαρτύρηταί τι περί τινος 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.16, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.66.}}
**9** _impersonal_ in _passive_, παρ᾽ ἄλλου ποιητοῦ μαρτυρεῖται **testimony is borne** by.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 344d}}; οἶδα.. μαρτυρήσεσθαί μοι ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.8.10, compare “Ap.” 26}}; μεμαρτύρηται ὑμῖν **testimony has been given** before you, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.55, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 9.5.}}
**10** _passive_, μαρτυρεῖταί μοι σοφία **is ascribed** to me, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.26}}; μαρτυροῦμαι ἐμπειρίαν I **have** it **ascribed to me**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.58a, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sacr.” 10; καλοκἀγαθίαν μαρτυρούμενος 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 15.10.5}}; μαρτυροῦμαι ἐπί τινι I **bear a character** for.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.25 f}}; ἄνδρας μαρτυρουμένους men **whose character is approved by testimony,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Acts.6.3; τεχνίτας.. μαρτυρηθέντας ὑπό τινος “SIG” 799.28 (Cyzicus, 1st c.AD)}}; δι᾽ ὅλης οἰκουμένης μαρτυρούμενον θεόν {{Refs|“Sammelb.” 1070 (from Abydos).}}
**II** Astrology texts, **to be in aspect with**, with _dative_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 123; μ. τὴν μοῖραν “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 7.226}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC(?): Nechepso Astrologus cited in 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 279.23.}}

3141:
** μαρτῠρ-ία**, ἡ,
**testimony**, Διονύσου μαρτυρίῃσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.325, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 282 (_plural_): frequently both _sing._ and _plural_, μ. τινός}} his **evidence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.7; μ. παρέχεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 179b; εἰς μ. κληθῆναι [prev. author] “Lg.” 937a}}; μαρτυριῶν ἀπέχεσθαι to refuse to give **evidence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1316; ἐμβάλλεσθαι μ. ψευδῆ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.31}}; γράφειν μ. τινί **serve** him **with a subpoena**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.45; μ. ἔχειν παρά τινων ἔκ τινων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1338a36}}: in non-legal sense, **commendation**, πάσης μ. ἐπιτήδειον (in sense of ἄξιον) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“SIG” 1073.17 (Olympia, 2nd c.AD)}}: in _plural_, **demonstrations of favour,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|POxy. 41.18 (3rd-4th c.AD).}}
**II** Astrology texts, **aspect**, Vett. Val.5.5, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.532, 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 1.124, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Paraphrasis Ptolemaei Tetrabiblou” 255.}}

3142:
** μαρτύριον** [ῠ], τό,
**testimony, proof**, πρῶτον καὶ μέγιστον μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.22, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).10, etc.: frequently in _plural_, μαρτύρια θέσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.55, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1095 (Lyric poetry), “Eu.” 485, 797; μετ᾽ ἀειμνήστου μ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.33}}; μαρτύριον δέ.., followed by γάρ, **here is a proof, namely**.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 8}}, etc.; μέγα τόδε μ.,.. γάρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.120.}}
**II** τὰ μ. **the tables of the Decalogue**, {{LXX|LXX.Lev.16.13, +others}}
**III** **shrine of a martyr**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 15.15 (a) Z., “POxy.” 941.4 (6th c.AD), etc.}}

3143:
** μαρτύρομαι** [ῡ],
_future_ μαρτῠροῦμαι (δια-) {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Psa.80(81).9 (codex 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor)}}: _aorist_ ἐμαρτῡράμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 47d, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 2.47}}:—**call to witness, invoke**, with _accusative_ _person_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.29, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 813, etc.}}; especially of the gods, Ἄρτεμιν, γαῖαν καὶ θεούς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1451, “Ph.” 626}}; δαίμονας, ὡς.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Med.” 619}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ὑμᾶς δ᾽ ἀκούειν ταῦτ᾽ ἐγὼ μαρτύρομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 643, etc.: with}}_participle_, μαρτύρομαι τυπτόμενος I **call you to witness that**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1031, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 858 (troch.).}}
**2** with _accusative_ _of things_, **call** one **to witness** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.44, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 528, “Pl.” 932.}}
**3** **protest, asseverate**, μ. ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Nu.” 1222, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.80; ταῦτα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus [same place]: _absolutely_, μαρτύρομαι}} I **protest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 926, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.15.}}
**4** with _accusative_ _person_ et _genitive_ _of things_, οἱ δὲ τῆς τῶν θεῶν.. παραγωγῆς τὸν Ὅμηρον μ. ὅτι.. **cite** Homer **as a witness** of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 364d; ἐμαυτὸν μ. τῆς φιλοτιμίας 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus}} [same place]; μ. τινὶ ὅτι.. {{NT|NT.Gal.5.3}}; τοὺς ἀποστάντας μ. τῆς ἐπιορκίας **remind** them of.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.129.}}

3144:
** μάρτῠς**, ὁ, ἡ,
Cretan dialect, Epid. **μαῖτυς** {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 1.13, +others, “IG” 42(1).42}}, Cretan dialect also **μαίτυρς** {{Refs|“GDI” 4998}} see ii; _genitive_ μάρτῠρος, _accusative_ -ῠρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 371, etc.}}, formed from **μάρτυρ** (which see), except _accusative_ μάρτῠν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 84. 4, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 1034, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.49a}}; _dative_ _plural_ μάρτῠσι (but μάρτυρσι probably in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 51)}}:—**witness** (not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus), 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus [same place], “hymnus ad Mercurium” 372, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1226, etc.; ἄμμιν μάρτυς ἔστω Ζεύς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.167, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 664; ἁμέραι δ᾽ ἐπίλοιποι μ. σοφώτατοι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.34; τούτων μάρτυρας καλῶ θεούς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1248, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 491; μάρτυρα θέσθαι τινά [prev. author] “Supp.” 261; μ. θεοὺς ποιεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.87, etc.; μάρτυρι χρῆσθαί τινι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1375b30}}; μάρτυρας παρέχεσθαι produce **witnesses**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 471e, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.51, etc.; μάρτυρες παρίστανται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.16}}; μάρτυρα παράγεσθαι, μάρτυρας ἐπάγεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 836c, “R.” 364c; δικάζει ταῦτα μαρτύρων ὅπο 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 934; μαρτύρων ἐναντίον 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.28, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 448; ἐν μάρτυσι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 175e}}; τί δεῖται μάρτυρο; {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 340a.}}
**II** **martyr,** {{NT|NT.Rev. 2.13, etc.}}
**III** Astrology texts, **in aspect**, μάρτυρες ἀλλήλων {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 4.451. (Cf. μάρτυρ, μάρτυρος.)}}

3147:
** μαστ-ίζω**,
{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 2.645}}, _Doric dialect_ **μαστ-ίσδω** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.108}}: _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ μάστιξα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.768:}} —_passive_, _aorist_ ἐμαστίχθην variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.114}}; _participle_ μαστιχθείς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.348 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor)}}: (μάστιξ):—**whip, flog**, μάστιξεν δ᾽ ἵππους Il.[same place], etc.; τυ.. ὑπὸ.. ὤμους μαστίσδοιεν (see. above) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus [same place]: with}}_infinitive_, μάστιξεν δ᾽ ἐλάαν **whipped** them on or forward, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.366, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.82, etc.: _metaphorically_, ἵνα.. σε πολλοὶ μαστίξωσι λόγοις “epigram Gr.” 303.5 (from Smyrna).}}
**2** **stimulate** the bowels, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|7th c.AD: Stephanus Medicus “in Hp.” 2.311 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator:}} — _passive_ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work]312 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}—_Epic dialect_ word, used twice in Comedy texts, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 72, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 133.5, also in LXX.Num. 22.25, LXX.Wis.5.11 (_passive_)}}; and in late Prose, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 42, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pr.Im.” 24, etc.}}; the _Attic dialect_ form being μαστιγόω.

3148:
** μάστ-ιξ**, ῑγος, ἡ,
**whip, scourge**, mostly for driving horses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.748, etc.; μ. λιγυρῇ 11.532}}; ἵππου μ. horse-**whip**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.3}}; λιγυρὰ μ. διπλῆ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 242 (_anapaest meter_)}}; later, **scourge**, ὑπὸ μαστίγων διαβαίνειν to cross under **the lash**, of soldiers flogged on, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.56, compare 103; τοξεύειν ὑπὸ μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.25; τῇ μ. κνάπτειν 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 275}}; μάστιγ᾽ ἔχων **whip** in hand, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 933, 5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 36.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **scourge, plague**, μάστιξ Διός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.37, 13.812; μάστιγι θείᾳ.. ἐλαύνομαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 682; πληγεὶς θεοῦ μάστιγι [prev. author] “Th.” 608}}; διπλῇ μ., τὴν Ἄρης φιλεῖ, i.e. fire and sword, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 642}}; μ. [Θεοῦ], of sickness, {{NT|NT.Mark.5.34, compare 3.10 (_plural_)}}; but μάστιξ Πειθοῦς the **lash** of eloquence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.219:}} — _Ionic dialect_ μάστις (which see): μάστιγξ is not found.

3149:
** μαστός**, ὁ,
_Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_ **μαζός**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (except in 3.133, 5.18, where codices give μαστός}}; twice in codices of Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 531, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 701)}}; _Doric dialect_ **μασδός** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.16, 48}}; later **μασθός** {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX.Isa.32.12 (codex A), +others, 3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.934, NT.Rev.1.13 (variant), “IG” 3.238}} **b,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|PMag.Lond. 121.208, etc., also in codices of 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 545:}} —usage contradicts the statement of Grammars that μαζός is **the man's breast**, μαστός **the woman's:— breast**, δεξιτερὸν κατὰ μαζόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.393}}; of men's breasts, βάλε δουρὶ στέρνον ὑπὲρ μαζοῖο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.528; βάλε στῆθος παρὰ μαζόν 8.121, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.82, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.17, 4.3.6.}}
**2** more frequently of **a woman's breast**, μαζὸν ἀνέσχε, of Hecuba pleading with Hector, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.80; εἴ ποτέ τοι λαθικηδέα μαζὸν ἐπέσχον [prev. work] 83}}; γυναῖκά τε θήσατο μαζόν sucked her **breast**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|24.58; πάϊς δέ οἱ ἦν ἐπὶ μαζῷ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.448; σὺ δέ μ᾽ ἔτρεφες.. τῷ σῷ ἐπὶ μαζῷ 19.483; so φαίνουσαι τοὺς μαζούς 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.85; τοὺς μ. ἀποταμοῦσα [prev. author] 4.202; ἐπὶ τοῦ μαστοῦ ἔφυ φῦμα [prev. author] 3.133; προὔκειτο μαστῶν περονίς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 925}}; προσέσχε μαζόν, of the mother, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 531; μαστὸν ἀμφέχασκε, of the child, [prev. work] 545, compare 897; μαστῶν ἀποστάς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 776; πῶλον ἀφέλξων σῶν ἀπὸ μαστῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 142 (_anapaest meter_), etc.}}
**2.b** rarely of animals, **udder**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyc.” 55 (Lyric poetry), 207, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Jovem” 48.}}
**2.c** generally, of the **breasts** of all mammalia, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 521b21, “PA” 688a18f, “GA” 752b23.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **any round, breast-shaped object**:
**II.1** **round hill, knoll**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.6, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 48.}}
**II.2** **round piece of wool** fastened to the edge of nets, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 2.6, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 5.29.}}
**II.3** at Paphos, **breast-shaped cup**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus Cyren. cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.487b, compare “IG” 7.3498 (from Oropus), 11(4).1307.21 (from Delos).}}

3152:
** μάται-ος** [μᾰ], α, ον
{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 331, “Th.” 442, “Ag.” 422 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; also ος, ον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1151, “Ch.” 82, “Eu.” 337 (all Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 780, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 628, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 231b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.18}}: (μάτη):—
**I** **vain, empty, idle**:
**I.1** of words, acts, etc., μάταια νομίζομεν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 141, compare 487, 492, etc.}}; μ. λόγοι **idle** tales or words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10}}.; μ. ἔπεα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 11; δόξαι φέρουσαι χάριν μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 422 (Lyric poetry)}}; μ. ὑλάγματα, ποιφύγματα,{{Refs|[prev. work] 1672, “Th.” 281}}; μ. εὐχή E.[same place]; μάταια βάζειν τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 119; μ. τι δρᾶσαί τινα [prev. author] “Cyc.” 662 (Lyric poetry); μ. ἂν εἴη πόνος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 40d}}; μ. ἡδονή S.[same place]; δοξοσοφία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sph.” [same place]; ὄρεξις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1094a21}}; τὰ μ. ἀναλώματα **useless** expenses, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 58.20 (3rd c.AD)}}; but also, μ. ἔπος a word **of offence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.120.}}
**I.2** of persons, **empty, foolish**, ματαιότεροι νόον {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1025, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.173, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 863, 888 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 338, 5th-6th c.BC: Amipsias Comicus 9 (_superlative_)}}; φῦλον ματαιότατον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.21}}; **worthless**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1339 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** **rash, irreverent, profane**, frequently in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, μ. γλῶσσα “Pr.” [same place], “Ag.” 1662 (troch.); φρονήματα “Th.” 438}}; αὐτουργίαι μ., of matricide and the like, “Eu.” [same place]; χαρὰ μ. **mad** merriment, {{Refs|“Th.” 442; μ. ἀνοσίων τε κνωδάλων “Supp.” 762}}; τὸ μὴ μ. **seriousness, gravity**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 198; ψαύειν ματαίαις χερσί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 565.}}
**III** _adverb_ -ως **idly, without ground**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 940, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 39.2, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 908.4; ὀχλεῖν τοῖς ἀνθρώποις 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 6.1}}; μ. ἐρεῖν **to no purpose**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 331d; ταλαιπωρῆσαι Polystr. p.31 W.}}

3154:
** μᾰταιόω**,
**bring to naught**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 258, “Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}
**2** ματαιοῦσιν ἑαυτοῖς ὅρασιν **invent** a vision of their own, {{LXX|LXX.Jer.23.16.}}
**II** more frequently in _passive_, **to be brought to naught**, οὐ ματαιωθήσεται τὰ ῥήματα{{LXX|LXX.Judg.6.4.}}
**II.2** **become foolish, act foolishly**, ἐματαιώθην σφόδρα{{LXX+NT|LXX.1Ch.21.8, cf. NT.Rom.1.21}}; μεματαίωταί σοι thou **hast done foolishly**, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.13.13.}}

3155:
** μάτην**,
_Doric dialect_ **μάτᾱν** [μᾰ], _adverb_ **in vain,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Hymnus in Cererem 308, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.83, etc.; μὴ πόνει μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 44; βλέποντες ἔβλεπον μ. [prev. work] 447; μ. ἐρεῖν [prev. work] 1007}}; with a Noun, τὸ μ. ἄχθος the **fruitless** burden, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 165 (Lyric poetry)}}; Διὸς μ. ἄκοιτις his bedfellow **to no good end**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1149: as predicate, μ. ὁ μόχθος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 521.}}
**2** **at random, without reason**, οὔ σε μ. τιμῶσι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 523; ὅρα μὴ μ. κόμπος ὁ λόγος ᾖ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.103, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 127; οὐ γὰρ δίκαιον.. τοὺς κακοὺς μ. χρηστοὺς νομίζειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 609; ἵνα μὴ μ. θαρρήσῃς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 189d}}; ὁ νοσῶν μ., i.e. he that is mad, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 635 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 95 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**3** **idly, falsely**, λέγοντες εἴτ᾽ ἀληθές, εἴτ᾽ ἄρ᾽ οὖν μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 345; λόγῳ μάτην θνῄσκοντες [prev. author] “El.” 63, compare 1298}}; μ. βέβακεν, of a dream, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 423 (Lyric poetry).—Originally _accusative_ of μάτη, and we find εἰς μάτην in 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Trag.” 28,241, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 33(51).3; ἐπὶ μ. “POxy.” 530.8 (2nd c.AD).}}

3162:
** μάχαιρ-α** [μᾰ], ας
(later -ης, _dative_ -ῃ, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 16.14 (2nd c.BC), NT.Luke.21.24, etc.)}}, ἡ, **large knife** or **dirk**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.844, 18.597, 19.252; μ. ἥ οἱ πὰρ ξίφεος μέγα κουλεὸν αἰὲν ἄωρτο 3.271}}; **carving-knife**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.49, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.61, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 489, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 353a, etc.; κοπίδες μ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 242}}; **sacrificial knife**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 948, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 91, “Michel” 832.52 (Samos, 4th c.BC):}} —ἡ Δελφικὴ μ. a knife adapted to various purposes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1252b2, compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}} see at {Δελφικὴ μ}.; proverbial, of greedy persons, because Delphian sacrificers claimed a share for the knife, {{Refs|“App.Prov.” 1.94.}}
**2** as a weapon, **short sword, dagger**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.59, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.75, 7.225, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.87, etc.; an assassin's weapon, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.69}}; used by jugglers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 294e (_plural_), etc.}}; later, **sabre**, opposed to the straight sword (ξίφος), {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 12.11, compare “HG” 3.3.7, “Cyr.” 1.2.13, NT.Matt.26.52, etc.}}; οἱ ἐπὶ τῆς μ., of a bodyguard, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.30.7}}; but, ἐπὶ μ. τασσόμενοι possessing power of life and death (**jus gladii), Cat.Codex Astr.**{{Refs|8(4).173}}; μ. ἱππική cavalry **sabre,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 11(2).161 B 99 (Delos, 3rd c.BC).}}
**3** μ. κουρίδες, **shears** or **scissors**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 37}}; κεκαρμένος μοιχὸν μιᾶ μ., i.e. with one **blade**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 849, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.32 (where διπλῇ is _falsa lectio_), 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}} see entry {μιᾷ μαχαίρ; μ. κουρικαί} {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Dio” 9.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, διὰ μαχαιρῶν καὶ πυρός {{NT+3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Zenobius Paroemiographus 3.19, compare 3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 1.10; μ. τοῦ πνεύματος NT.Eph.6.17, cf. LXX.Isa.49.2.}}
**II** name of a precious stone, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 847a5, Pseudo-Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fluv.” 10.5.}}
**III** part of the liver, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 180.}}

3163:
** μᾰχ-η**, ἡ,
(μάχομαι) **battle, combat**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, usually of armies, μ. καὶ φύλοπις 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.789; ἐπὶ ἶσα μ. τέτατο πτόλεμός τε 12.436; μ. ἐνοπή τε 12.35; μάχαι τ᾽ ἀνδροκτασίαι τε 7.237}}; sometimes of **single combat**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 158, 232, 263}}; μ. καὶ δηϊοτής{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 290; ναῶν ἐν μάχαις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.34; μάχαις καὶ ναυμαχίαις 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.26; μάχη δορός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 439 (Lyric poetry), etc.: with Verbs, μάχην μάχεσθαι}} fight **a battle**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.414, etc.; θήσονται μ. 24.402; μάχας εἰσήλυθον 2.798; ἀρτύνθη μ. 11.216; μ. ἤγειραν 17.261; μ. ὀρνύμεν, ὤτρυνον, 9.353, 12.277; συμφερόμεσθα μάχῃ 11.736; πειρᾶτο μάχας 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.43}}; ἀντιάζειν τινὶ μάχαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 67; σὺν γυναιξὶ τὰς μ. ποιούμενος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 302, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.29; μάχην συνάψαι ἐμβολαῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 336}}; μ. συμβάλλειν τινί engage **battle** with.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 837}}; διὰ μάχης τινὶ ἀπικέσθαι, ἔρχεσθαι, ἥκειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.169, 6.9, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 475; διὰ μάχης ἐκβαλεῖν τινα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1303a35 (so ἐξέπεσον διὰ μάχης [prev. work] 34)}}; εἰς μάχην πρός τινα ἐλθεῖν, μολεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 636 (troch.), “Ph.” 694; ἐς μάχην ἐπεξιέναι τινί 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.23; μάχης γενομένης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 869c}}; μάχῃ κρατῆσαι conquer **in battle**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 612, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.193 (with variant{μάχην}); νενικήκαμεν τὴν μεγάλην μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.53; Μιλτιάδης ὁ τὴν ἐν Μαραθῶνι μ. τοὺς βαρβάρους νικήσας 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.181}}]; μάχη τινός **battle with** an enemy, Αἴαντος δ᾽ ἀλέεινε μ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.542, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 361; μ. ὑπέρ τινος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.42; περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 919b}}: _plural_, **strifes**, ἔρις τε.. πόλεμοί τε μάχαι τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.177; μάχας ἐν λόγοις ποιεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 88a, etc.}}: generally, **contention, strife**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Ep.” 352c, etc.; μάχης ἐάν τις ἄρξηται “SIG” 1109.72 (2nd c.AD)}}; μ. νομικαί {{NT|NT.Tit.3.9.}}
**2** ={ἀγών}, **contest**, as for a prize in the games, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.58 (but ἄεθλα, opposed to μάχαι πολέμου, [prev. author] “O.” 2.44).}}
**3** **struggle**, μηχανή τε πολλὴ καὶ μ. ἦν περί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.38.}}
**II** **mode of fighting, way of battle**, ἡ μ. σφέων ἦν ἀπ᾽ ἵππων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.79; ἐπίστασθαι τὴν μ. τινῶν [prev. author] 7.9.ά, compare 7.85, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.7.}}
**III** **field of battle**, interpolated in {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 2.2.6, 5.5.4.}}
**IV** in Logic, **contradiction, inconsistency**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 52.1, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.392.}}

3164:
** μάχομαι** [ᾰ],
_Ionic dialect_ _present_ _optative_ μαχέοιτο [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.272, μαχέοιντο [prev. work] 344 (variant{μαχέονται})}}]; _participle_ μαχεόμενος variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.104 (elsewhere μαχόμενος, 9.75, +others)}}; _Epic dialect_ μαχειόμενος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.471, μαχεούμενος 11.403, 24.113}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ μαχέσκετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.140}}: _future_ μαχήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|23.621, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.209}}; μαχέσομαι, δια-μαχεσόμεθα (as variant) {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.48}}; also in late Prose, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 11.8.3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.215f; μαχοῦμαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 837, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1076, etc., μαχεῖται even in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.26, but μαχέονται 2.366; _Epic dialect_ μαχέσσομαι variant for{μαχήσομαι} 1.298}}]: _aorist_ ἐμαχεσάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.18, etc.; _optative_ μαχέσαιο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.329}}; _infinitive_ μαχέσασθαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|17.178, also μαχέσσασθαι 15.633; _optative_ μαχεσσαίμεσθα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.74}}; _participle_ μαχεσσαμένω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.304; later μαχήσασθαι 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.27.1, (ἀνα-) 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.93}}: _perfect_ μεμάχημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.43, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.41, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.54}}: late _aorist_ ἐμαχέσθην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.970f, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.4.9:}}_future_ μαχεσθήσομαι only {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Scholia recentia 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 672}}:—**fight**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.; ὑσμῖνι μ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.863; πολεμίζειν ἠδὲ μ. [prev. work] 452, etc.}}; μάχην μ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 5.5: in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} mostly of armies and persons fighting as parts of armies, but sometimes of single combat, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.91, 433, 7.51, 111, 279, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.31, 39}}; between men and beasts, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.633, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.15; between beasts, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.824}}:—Constr.: with _dative_ _person_, **fight with**, i. e. **against**, one, ἀνδράσιν ἶφι μ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.151, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1253, etc.}}; μ. ἀντία, ἐναντίον τινός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.88, 97; ἐπί τινι 5.124, etc.; πρός τινα 17.471, but πρὸς δαίμονα}} **against** heaven's will, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 98 (in _Attic dialect_ Prose, mostly with _dative_ or πρός, μ. τοῖς πολεμίοις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.47; πρὸς ἀλλήλους 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.116)}}; μ. σὺν σοί,.. θεά, **with thy help**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.390}}; μετὰ πρώτοισι μ. among the foremost, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.575}}; μετὰ Βοιωτῶν μ. with them, in their ranks,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|13.700; πρός τινας μετ᾽ ἀλλήλων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.53, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 179a}}; κατὰ σφέας γὰρ μαχέονται **will fight** by themselves, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.366 (but κατ᾽ ἕνα μ.}} **fight** in single combat, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.104)}}; μ. πρό τινος before him: hence, _metaphorically_, for him, in his defence, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.156, 8.56, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.33, etc.; ὑπὲρ τοῦ νόμου 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 44; πάτρας ὕπερ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1002; Ἕλλησιν ὑπὲρ Ἑλλήνων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 239b; περὶ δαιτί 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.245}}; but later usually περί τινος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 740, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 163, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95; ἀμφί τινι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.70, 91; ἀμφὶ νέκυι 16.565; εἵνεκά τινος 2.377: with _dative_ instrum., τόξοισι, πελέκεσσι μ., 7.140, 15.711 (χερσὶ μαχέσσασθαι}}, of boxers, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.39)}}; μ. ἀπ᾽ ἵππου **fight** from horseback, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.63}}; τὸ μήπω μεμαχημένον **the force that had** not yet **come into action**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.43.}}
**2** with _accusative_, **fight against**, only _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.23.}}
**II** generally, **quarrel, wrangle**, ἔριδι μ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.8; μ. ἐπέεσσιν [prev. work] 304, etc.}}; τινι {{Refs|5.875, 13.118}}; τῷ παιδὶ μ. **make a scene** with.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 23.8}}; **dispute, argue**, περί τι μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 342d, etc.}}
**III** **contend for the mastery** in games, etc., πὺξ μ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.621}}; **measure oneself with** or **against**, τινι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.272; παγκράτιον μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1190, 1195; μ. Ὀλυμπιάδα 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “de gymnastica” 21.}}
**IV** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **struggle against** a force, ἀνάγκᾳ δ᾽ οὐδὲ θεοὶ μ. {{Refs 7th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 5.21; ὄμβρῳ 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 26.4; πρὸς ἡνίας μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1010; πρὸς ἐπιθυμίας ἢ ἡδονάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 191e}}; μ. τῷ λιμῷ, τῷ δίψει, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.5.}}
**V** with _infinitive_, **struggle, make an effort** to do, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 552a23.}}
**VI** of arguments, propositions, etc., **to be in contradiction** or **inconsistent**, τρία ὁμολογήματα μ. αὐτὰ αὑτοῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 155b, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.28.4; μαχόμενα 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.95 K., 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.198, +others}}

3165:
** ἐγώ**,
I: _pronoun_ of the first person:—_Epic dialect_ mostly **ἐγών** before vowels (so in _Doric dialect_, before consonants, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 85, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 81, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 748, 754), rarely in Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 932 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱών** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 51.4}}:— strengthened **ἔγωγε**, **I at least, for my part, indeed, for myself** (more frequently in _Attic dialect_ than in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}: _Doric dialect_ **ἐγώνγα** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 51, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 736, “Lys.” 986, _dative_ ἐμίνγα “IG” 22.1126.7 (Amphict. Delph.)}}: _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱώνγα** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 21}}; **ἱώνει** {{Refs|Ead. 10}}; **ἰώγα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 898}}: _Laconian dialect_ and _Tarentum dialect_ **ἐγώνη**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 255.29.}}
**II** oblique cases from a different root, _genitive_ ἐμοῦ, _enclitic_ μο; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ἐμέο, ἐμεῦ, μευ, also ἐμέθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.525, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 177 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἔμεθεν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.7; ἐμεῖο “IG” 3.1337; μεθέν 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 20}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμέος, ἐμεῦς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 144}}; _Boeotian dialect_ ἐμοῦς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 37; also ἐμῶς, ἐμίο, ἐμίω, ἐμίως 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 74.17:}} — _dative_ ἐμοί, _enclitic_ μοι (which may be compared with Sanskrit _genitive_ **me** in κλῦθί μοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.115, +others); ὅ μοι πόσις “Schwyzer” 683 (5th c.AD: Cypria)}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 99, “AJA” 29.461 (Rhodian, 5th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 733, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.30}}; _Tarentum dialect_ ἐμίνη {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13: _accusative_ ἐμέ}}, _enclitic_ μ; {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria μι “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 59,60 H.}}
**III** dual, _nominative_ and _accusative_, **νῶι**, **we two**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.34, etc.}}; _accusative_ νῶιν Zenod.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.377}}; _Attic dialect_ νώ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 278b (also 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.219, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.475); νῶε 5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 39, 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 5: _genitive_, _dative_ νῶι; νῷν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 3}}; νῶι _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 773}}; νῶιν, ={ἡμῖν}, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 1.213, etc.}}
**IV** _plural_, _nominative_ ἡμεῖς (ἡμέες _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.6, +others, rejected by 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 93.1)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.303, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 18.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.144}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμές {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 65, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 42, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168:— _genitive_ ἡμῶν (also ἥμων 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 130.23)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.112, etc.; ἡμείων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.170, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.46}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἀμμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 88, “Milet.” 3 No.152.29}}; ἄμμων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work]74, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 66}}; ἁμῶν [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus] 266, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.158}}; Cretan dialect, _Boeotian dialect_ ἁμίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 528.5, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.21:— _dative_ ἡμῖν, in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus also ἡμίν}} (ῐ) (or ἧμιν Aristarch.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.214, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3)}}; also rarely in Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 37, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 386 (uncertain)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμῐν, ἄμμῐ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.384, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 80, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.155, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 156 (Lyric poetry), “Milet.” 3 No.152; ἄμμεσιν 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 100}}; _Doric dialect_ also ἁμίν or ἇμιν, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 77, 78, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 347 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1081}}; with ῑ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 821, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.145:— _accusative_ ἡμᾶς (also ἧμᾰς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.372)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.211, “SIG” 273.25 (Milet., 4th c.BC)}}; ἥμεας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.294 (cf. Hdn.Gr. 2.140)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.59, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 115}}, Theocr.8.25; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 1 (Abu Simbel, 6th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 173, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 759 codices, “Lys.” 95.}}—On these dialectic varieties, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 50}} _ff._ (Cf. Sanskrit _ahám_ (ἐγών), _accusative_ _plural_ **asmā´n**; for νώ cf. Sanskrit _nau_):—frequently in answers, as an affirmative, especially in form ἔγωγε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1248, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 149b, etc.}}; οὗτος ἐ. here am {{Refs 5th c.BC+|I, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; ὅδ᾽ ἐκεῖνος ἐ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 138 (Lyric poetry)}}; rarely with _Article_, τὸν ἐμέ **myself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 166a, “Sph.” 239b (but ὁ ἐ.}} **the Self, the Ego**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 444)}}; τίς ὢν οὗτος ὁ ἐγὼ τυγχάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1119a; τί ἐστι φίλο; ἄλλος ἐ. 4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.166 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}; τί τοῦτ᾽ ἐμο; ἡμῖν τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ; Latin _quid mea hoc refert_ ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 498, etc.}}; ἐγ; in a question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1336, +others}}; ἡμεῖς **the self**, ἔνθα δὴ ἡμεῖς μάλιστα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.1.7.}}

3166:
** μεγᾰλ-αυχέω**,
**boast, brag**, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1528 (_anapaest meter_), LXX.Eze.16.50, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.284, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.272 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus); ἐπί τινι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.13.10; ἐν ταῖς εὐπραγίαις [prev. author] 8.21.11; διά τι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.16}}:—also in _middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.104c, “R.” 395d; ἐπί τινι 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.13.}}
**II** c._accusative_, **boast of**, μονομάχιον {{Refs|[prev. author] “Gall.” 10.}}

3167:
** μεγᾰλεῖ-ος**, α, ον,
(μέγας) **magnificent, splendid**, ῥήματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.34 (_comparative_)}}; κτῆμα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 4.5.2; μ. τι διαπεπραγμένος 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Per.fragment” 1, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.6 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; πίστιν -οτάταν probably in Paean cited in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Flamininus” 16 (“Coll.Alex.” p.173 P.)}}; τὸ μ. τῶν πράξεων, τῆς ἀθανασίας, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.1.1, “SIG” 798.4 (1st c.AD)}}, cf. Vett. Val.70.1; τὰ μ. **mighty works**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Deu.11.2, +others, NT.Act.2.11}}. _adverb_ -ως **greatly**, ὠφελῶν τὴν πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 11.16, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.87.5}}; **magnificently**, μ. εἴρηκας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 291e; ὀψωνεῖν 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 192; θεοὺς μ. τιμᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 11.9}}: _comparative_ -ότερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 168c}}; -οτέρως, γαμεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.9.}}
**2** of persons, **stately, haughty**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mem.” 4.1.4.}}
**3** τὸ μ. τινός, as a title, **Highness,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|POxy. 1204.10 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**4** of style, **elevated**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 14, +others}} _adverb_ _superlative_ -ότατα, ἑρμηνεῦσαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.21.1.}}

3168:
** μεγᾰλει-ότης**, ητος, ἡ,
**majesty, magnificence**, {{NT+1st c.AD+|LXX “Je.” 40 (33). 9, NT.Luke.9.43, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.130f, 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 94; ἡ τῶν πυραμίδων μ. “OGI” 666.26 (Egypt, 1st c.AD)}}; of a person, **greatness,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Supp.epigram 3.583.6 (Olbia, 2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**II** as a title, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Majesty”, ἡ σὴ μ. 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 8.4.3, compare “CPHerm.” 52.23 (3rd c.AD)}}; ἐν ταῖς μ. γενήσεται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 70.4}}; also, = Latin _majestas_, ἡ μ. τοῦ Ῥωμαίων δήμου {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PGiss.” 40i 11 (3rd c.AD).}}

3169:
** μεγᾰλο-πρεπής**, ές,
**befitting a great man, magnificent**, δεῖπνον μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.18; δωρεὴν μεγαλοπρεπεστάτην [prev. author] 6.122; κάλλιστον ἔργον καὶ μεγαλοπρεπέστατον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1125; ταφή 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 234c; προαίρεσις 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Epitaphius” 40; πράξεις [prev. work] 1 (_comparative_)}}; δόξα {{NT|NT.2Pet.1.17, etc.}}
**2** of persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 487a, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1107b17; τὸ μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.10.5}}; of a horse, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 10.1 (_comparative_)}}: _superlative_, as honorific title, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“PGrenf.” 2.81 (a). 14 (5th c.AD), etc.}}
**3** of style, μ. λόγοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 210d; λέξις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1441b12; μεθιστάναι ἐπὶ τὸ -έστερον [prev. work] 1423b12.}}
**II** _adverb_ -πέως, _Attic dialect_ -πῶς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.128, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.17, etc.}}: _comparative_ -έστερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Vect.” 6.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 215e}}: _superlative_ -έστατα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.57.}}

3170:
** μεγᾰλύνω**,
_future_ μεγαλῠνῶ {{LXX|LXX.Gen.12.2, +others:}}_aorist_ ἐμεγάλυνα{{LXX|LXX.Eccl.2.4, +others}}:—_passive_, _future_ -υνθήσομαι{{LXX|LXX.Zech.12.11, +others}}: _aorist_ -ύνθην{{LXX|LXX.Mal.1.5, +others}}: _perfect_ _participle_ μεμεγαλυμμένος {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aquila LXX.Psa.143(144).12}}: (μέγας):—**make great** or **powerful, exalt**, τοὺς πολεμίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.98:}} —_passive_, μεγαλύνεσθαι ἐκτῶν συμβαινόντων **gain great glory** by.., {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.24, cf. NT.Phil.1.20, “POxy.” 1592.3 (3rd-4th c.AD).}}
**II** **make great by word, extol, magnify**, τὸ ὄνομά τινος {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 320; μ. τὴν ἑαυτοῦ δύναμιν παρά τινι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.81; ἑαυτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 32; μ. τὴν Λακεδαίμονα πρὸς Ἀθηναίους 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cimon” 16; τοῦ θεοῦ τὴν δύναμιν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.20: frequently in LXX}}, [same places]:—_middle_, **boast oneself**, περί τινος {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 35}}; γέννα in point of birth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 892 (Lyric poetry); οὐδὲ μεγαλύνεται ἐπὶ τῷ ἔργῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 2.17, compare “Oec.” 21.4; ταῦτ᾽ ἀκούων ἐμεγαλύνετο [prev. author] “Mem.” 3.6.3.}}
**II.2** **magnify, exaggerate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.28, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.173 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, “Ir.” p.45 W., 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus “fragment” 57.81, +others}}

3171:
Included with: ** μέγᾰς**, μεγάλη [ᾰ], μέγα [ᾰ],
_genitive_ μεγάλου, ης, ου, _dative_ μεγάλῳ, ῃ, ῳ, _accusative_ μέγᾰν, μεγάλην, μέγα [ᾰ]; dual μεγάλω, α, w; _plural_ μεγάλοι, μεγάλαι, μεγάλα, etc.: the stem μεγάλο- is never used in _singular_ _nominative_ and _accusative_ _masculine_ and _neuter_, and only once in _vocative_ _masculine_,
**A** ὦ μεγάλε Ζεῦ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 822 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**A.I** **big**, of bodily size: frequently of stature, εἶδος.. μ. ἦν ὁράασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.4; κεῖτο μ. μεγαλωστί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.776; ἠΰς τε μ. τε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9. 508}}; φῶτα μέγαν καὶ καλόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 513; καλή τε μεγάλη τε 15.418; κάρτα μεγάλη καὶ εὐειδής 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.1}}; φύσιν τίν᾽ εἶχε φράζ; Answ. μέγας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 742.}}
**A.I.b** **full-grown**, of age as shown by stature, νῦν δ᾽ ὅτε δὴ μ. εἰμί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.314; μήτε μέγαν μήτ᾽ οὖν νεαρῶν τινα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 358 (_anapaest meter_)}}; later, **elder** of two persons of the same name, Wilcken {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Chr.” 305 (3rd c.BC)}}; Σκιπίων ὁ μ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.35.9.}}
**A.I.c** of animals, μ. ἵπποι, βοῦς, σῦς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.839, 18.559, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.439; αἰετός 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).50.}}
**A.I.2** generally, **vast, high**, οὐρανός, ὄρος, πύργος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.497, 16.297, 6.386}}; **wide**, πέλαγος, λαῖτμα θαλάσσης, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.179, 5.174}}; **long**, ἠϊών, αἰγιαλός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.31, 2.210: sometimes opposed to ὀλίγος, κῦμα οὔτε μέγ᾽ οὔτ᾽ ὀ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.94}}; but usually opposed to μικρός or σμικρός, πρὸς ἑαυτὸ ἕκαστον καὶ μ. καὶ σμικρόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 3; τὸ ἄπειρον ἐκ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 987b26, etc.}}
**A.II** of quality or degree, **great, mighty**, frequently epithet of gods, ὁ μ. Ζεύς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1052 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; μεγάλα θεά, of Demeter and Persephone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 683 (Lyric poetry)}}; θεοὶ μεγάλοι, of the Cabiri, {{Refs|“IG” 12(8).71 (from Imbros), etc.}}; Μήτηρ μ., of Cybele, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 1014.83 (Erythrae, 3rd c.BC), 1138.3 (Delos, 2nd c.BC)}}; Μήτηρ θεῶν μ. {{Refs|“OGI” 540.6 (from Pessinus), etc.}}; Ἴσιδος μ. μητρὸς θεῶν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PStrassb.” 81.14 (2nd c.BC)}}; μ. ἡ Ἄρτεμις Ἐφεσίων {{NT|NT.Act.19.28}}; τίς θεὸς μ. ὡς ὁ θεὸς ἡμῶ; {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.76(77).13; ὁ μ. θεός NT.Tit.2.13}}; of men, μ. ἠδὲ κραταιός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.382; ὀλίγος καὶ μ. 7th c.BC: Callinus Epicus 1.17, etc.}}; μέγας ηὐξήθη rose **to greatness**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.5; ἤρθη μ. [prev. work]8; βασιλεὺς ὁ μ.}}, i. e. the King of Persia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.188, etc. (θεῶν β. ὁ μ., of Zeus, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.34); βασιλεὺς μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 24 (_anapaest meter_)}}; as a title of special monarchs, Ἀρδιαῖος ὁ μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 615c; ὁ μ. Ἀλέξανδρος 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.3d; ὁ μ. ἐπικληθεὶς Ἀντίοχος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.2.7, etc.; μ. φίλος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 549; πλούτῳ τε κἀνδρείᾳ μ. [prev. author] “Tr.” 674; ἐπὶ μέγα ἦλθεν ἰσχύος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.97.}}
**A.II.2** **strong**, of the elements, etc., ἄνεμος, λαῖλαψ, Ζέφυρος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.200, 12.408, 14.458}}; of properties, passions, qualities, feelings, etc., of men, θάρσος, πένθος, ποθή, etc.,{{NT+8th c.BC+|9.381, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.254, 11.471, etc.; ἀρετή 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.193, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.5; θυμός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.496, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 702; κλέος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.446; ἄχος 9.9; πυρετός NT.Luke.4.38 (incorrect _accusative_ to 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.275)}}; ἡ μ. νοῦσος **epilepsy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.6.5, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(2).341.}}
**A.II.3** of sounds, **great, loud**, ἀλαλητός, ἰαχή, πάταγος, ὀρυμαγδός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.138, 15.384, 21.9, 256}}; θόρυβοι, κωκυτός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 142 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1176; οὐκ ἔστι ὅκως τι νεῖκος ἔσται ἢ μέγα ἢ σμικρόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.62; μὴ φώνει μέγα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 574.}}
**A.II.4** generally, **great, mighty**, ὅρκος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.113}}; ὄλβος, τιμά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.56, “P.” 4.148}}; μ. λόγος, μῦθος, a **great** story, rumour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 732, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 226 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐρώτημα a **big**, i. e. **difficult**, question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 275d, “Hp.Ma.” 287b}}; **weighty, important**, τόδε μεῖζον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.291}}; μέγα ποιέεσθαί τι to esteem **of great importance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, compare 9.111; μέγα γενέσθαι εἴς τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.6; μ. ὑπάρχειν πρός τι [prev. author] “Mem.” 2.3.4; μέγα διαφέρειν εἴς τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 78o c}}; οὐκ ἂν εἴη παρὰ μέγα τὸ δικολογεῖν not of **great importance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.85 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; τὸ δὲ μέγιστον and what is **most important**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.70, compare 1.142}}; οἱ μέγιστοι καιροί the **most pressing** emergencies, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.44}}; μ. ὠνησάμενοι χρημάτων for **large** sums, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.50.3, etc.}}
**A.II.5** with a bad sense, **over-great**, μέγα εἰπεῖν to speak **big**, and so provoke divine wrath, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.288; λίην μέγα εἶπες 3.227, 16.243; μέγα ἔργον 3.261, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.64; ἔργων μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1546 (_anapaest meter_); ὠμὸν τὸ βούλευμα καὶ μ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.36}}; ἔπος μ., μ. λόγοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 423 (Lyric poetry), “Ant.” 1350 (_anapaest meter_); μ. γλῶσσα [prev. work] 127 (_anapaest meter_)}}; μηδὲν μέγ᾽ εἴπῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 386; μὴ μέγα λέγε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95b; μὴ μεγάλα λίαν λέγε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 835; μέγα φρονεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1078, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 6; μεγάλα φρονεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 988}}; μεγάλα, μεῖζον ἢ δικαίως πνεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 189, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 376 (Lyric poetry); μέγα τι παθεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.17; μὴ μέγα λέγων μεῖζον πάθῃς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1244.}}
**A.II.6** of style. **impressive**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 278}}; μεῖζον **more striking**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 103.}}
**A.II.7** of days, **long**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.714.}}
**B** _adverb_ μεγάλως [ᾰ] **greatly, mightily**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.432, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 429, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.16, 30, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.10, 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 28.1, etc.; strengthened, μάλα μ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.723; δμαθέντες μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 907 (Lyric poetry); with _adjectives_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.4, 7.190.}}
**B.II** more frequently _neuter_ _singular_ μέγα as _adverb_, **very much, exceedingly**, μ. χαῖρε **all** hail!, see l. for μάλα in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.402}}; especially with Verbs expressing strong feeling, μ. κεν κεχαροίατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.256; μ. κήδεται 2.27, etc.: with Verbs expressing power, might, μ. πάντων.. κρατέει 1.78; ὃς μ. πάντων.. ἤνασσε 10.32; πατρὸς μ. δυναμένοιο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.276, compare 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “Epigrammata” 15.1, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 950 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1358 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 141, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 366a; μ. δύνασθαι παρά τινι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.29; πλουτέειν μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32}}; or those expressing sound, **loudly**, μ. ἰάχειν, ἀῧσαι, βοῆσαι, εὔξασθαι, ἀμβῶσαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.333, 14.147, 17.334, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.239, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8 (also _plural_, μεγάλ᾽ εὔχετο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.450}}; μ. αὐδήσαντος, μ. ἤπυεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.505, 9.399): strengthened, μάλα μ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.321; μ. δ᾽ ἔβραχε φήγινος ἄξων 5.838, etc.: so in Trag. with all kinds of Verbs, μ. στένειν, σθένειν, χλίειν, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 711 (Lyric poetry), 938, “Ch.” 137}}: also in _plural_, μεγάλα.. δυστυχεῖς {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eu.” 791 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B.II.2** of Space, **far**, μέγα προθορών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.363}}; ἄνευθε μέγα **far** away,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|22.88; οὐκ ἂν μέγα τι τῆς ἀληθείας παρεξέλθοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 66b.}}
**B.II.3** with _adjectives_, as μέγ᾽ ἔξοχος, μέγα νήπιος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.480, 16.46}}; μ. νήπιε Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.85; μ. πλούσιος [prev. author] 1.32, 7.190; ὦ μέγ᾽ εὔδαιμον κόρη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 647}}: with _comparative_ and _superlative_, **by far**, μέγ᾽ ἀμείνονες, ἄριστος, φέρτατος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.405, 2.82, 16.21.}}
**C** degrees of Comparison (regular μεγαλώτερος, -ώτατος late, {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 780.1, 2):}}
**C.1** _comparative_ μείζων, ον, _genitive_ ονος, _Epic dialect_, _Attic dialect_ (also Delph., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 246 H 260 (4th c.BC))}}; _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}}, _Doric dialect_, _Aeolic dialect_ μέζων, ον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 25, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 44, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26, “IG” 7.235.16 (from Oropus), 5(2).3.18 (from Tegea), 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 62}} (also early _Attic dialect_, {{Refs|“IG” 12.22.65, but [με] ίζων [prev. work]6.93, by analogy of ὀλείζων [prev. work]76,95)}}; _dative_ _plural_ μεζόνεσσι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|Diotogenes Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.62: written μέσδων in 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 7.6, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 19}}: compare **μέττον·** μεῖζον, {{NT+5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus (uncertain); later μειζότερος NT.3JOhn.4}} (used as title, **elder,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|POxy. 943.3 (6th c.AD), etc.)}}; μειζονώτερος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 434}}:—**greater, longer, taller**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.168, 9.202, etc.}}; frequently also, **too great**, γέρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 231a}}; Μηνόφιλος μείζων M. **the elder,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Ostr.Bodl. see C 2 (2nd c.AD)}}; as title, μειζων κώμης **headman** of a village, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1626.5 (4th c.AD)}}, etc.: generally, **the higher authority,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PLond. 2.214.22 (3rd c.AD), “POxy.” 1204.17 (_plural_, 3rd c.AD)}}; οὔτε μεῖζον οὔτε ἔλαττον, a strong form of denial, **nothing whatever**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 4; οὐδαμὰ προὔφηνεν οὔτε μείζον᾽ οὔτ᾽ ἐλάττονα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 324}}. _adverb_ μειζόνως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1121, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 13.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.21, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ μεζόνως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.128, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.80, etc.}}: _neuter_ as _adverb_, μεῖζον σθένειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 456, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 216; μ. ἰσχύειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 3.28; ἐπὶ μ. ἔρχεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 259.}}
**C.2** _superlative_ μέγιστος, η, ον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.412, etc.}}: _neuter_ as _adverb_, μέγιστον ἴσχυσε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 502}}; δυνάμενος μ., with _genitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.5, 9.9}}: with another _superlative_, μέγιστον ἐχθίστη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1323: in _plural_, χαῖρ᾽ ὡς μέγιστα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 462; θάλλει μ. [prev. author] “OC” 700 (Lyric poetry); τὰ μέγιστ᾽ ἐτιμάθης [prev. author] “OT” 1203 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐς μέγιστον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 521; ἐς τὰ μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.111}}:—late _superlative_ μεγιστότατος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 1.130.49 (1st-2nd c.AD)}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _majmán_- 'greatness', Latin _magnus_, Gothic _mikils_ 'great'.)

3173:
** μέγᾰς**, μεγάλη [ᾰ], μέγα [ᾰ],
_genitive_ μεγάλου, ης, ου, _dative_ μεγάλῳ, ῃ, ῳ, _accusative_ μέγᾰν, μεγάλην, μέγα [ᾰ]; dual μεγάλω, α, w; _plural_ μεγάλοι, μεγάλαι, μεγάλα, etc.: the stem μεγάλο- is never used in _singular_ _nominative_ and _accusative_ _masculine_ and _neuter_, and only once in _vocative_ _masculine_,
**A** ὦ μεγάλε Ζεῦ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 822 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**A.I** **big**, of bodily size: frequently of stature, εἶδος.. μ. ἦν ὁράασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.4; κεῖτο μ. μεγαλωστί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.776; ἠΰς τε μ. τε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9. 508}}; φῶτα μέγαν καὶ καλόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 513; καλή τε μεγάλη τε 15.418; κάρτα μεγάλη καὶ εὐειδής 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.1}}; φύσιν τίν᾽ εἶχε φράζ; Answ. μέγας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 742.}}
**A.I.b** **full-grown**, of age as shown by stature, νῦν δ᾽ ὅτε δὴ μ. εἰμί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.314; μήτε μέγαν μήτ᾽ οὖν νεαρῶν τινα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 358 (_anapaest meter_)}}; later, **elder** of two persons of the same name, Wilcken {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Chr.” 305 (3rd c.BC)}}; Σκιπίων ὁ μ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.35.9.}}
**A.I.c** of animals, μ. ἵπποι, βοῦς, σῦς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.839, 18.559, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.439; αἰετός 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).50.}}
**A.I.2** generally, **vast, high**, οὐρανός, ὄρος, πύργος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.497, 16.297, 6.386}}; **wide**, πέλαγος, λαῖτμα θαλάσσης, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.179, 5.174}}; **long**, ἠϊών, αἰγιαλός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.31, 2.210: sometimes opposed to ὀλίγος, κῦμα οὔτε μέγ᾽ οὔτ᾽ ὀ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.94}}; but usually opposed to μικρός or σμικρός, πρὸς ἑαυτὸ ἕκαστον καὶ μ. καὶ σμικρόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 3; τὸ ἄπειρον ἐκ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 987b26, etc.}}
**A.II** of quality or degree, **great, mighty**, frequently epithet of gods, ὁ μ. Ζεύς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1052 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; μεγάλα θεά, of Demeter and Persephone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 683 (Lyric poetry)}}; θεοὶ μεγάλοι, of the Cabiri, {{Refs|“IG” 12(8).71 (from Imbros), etc.}}; Μήτηρ μ., of Cybele, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 1014.83 (Erythrae, 3rd c.BC), 1138.3 (Delos, 2nd c.BC)}}; Μήτηρ θεῶν μ. {{Refs|“OGI” 540.6 (from Pessinus), etc.}}; Ἴσιδος μ. μητρὸς θεῶν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PStrassb.” 81.14 (2nd c.BC)}}; μ. ἡ Ἄρτεμις Ἐφεσίων {{NT|NT.Act.19.28}}; τίς θεὸς μ. ὡς ὁ θεὸς ἡμῶ; {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.76(77).13; ὁ μ. θεός NT.Tit.2.13}}; of men, μ. ἠδὲ κραταιός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.382; ὀλίγος καὶ μ. 7th c.BC: Callinus Epicus 1.17, etc.}}; μέγας ηὐξήθη rose **to greatness**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.5; ἤρθη μ. [prev. work]8; βασιλεὺς ὁ μ.}}, i. e. the King of Persia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.188, etc. (θεῶν β. ὁ μ., of Zeus, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.34); βασιλεὺς μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 24 (_anapaest meter_)}}; as a title of special monarchs, Ἀρδιαῖος ὁ μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 615c; ὁ μ. Ἀλέξανδρος 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.3d; ὁ μ. ἐπικληθεὶς Ἀντίοχος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.2.7, etc.; μ. φίλος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 549; πλούτῳ τε κἀνδρείᾳ μ. [prev. author] “Tr.” 674; ἐπὶ μέγα ἦλθεν ἰσχύος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.97.}}
**A.II.2** **strong**, of the elements, etc., ἄνεμος, λαῖλαψ, Ζέφυρος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.200, 12.408, 14.458}}; of properties, passions, qualities, feelings, etc., of men, θάρσος, πένθος, ποθή, etc.,{{NT+8th c.BC+|9.381, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.254, 11.471, etc.; ἀρετή 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.193, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.5; θυμός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.496, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 702; κλέος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.446; ἄχος 9.9; πυρετός NT.Luke.4.38 (incorrect _accusative_ to 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.275)}}; ἡ μ. νοῦσος **epilepsy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.6.5, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(2).341.}}
**A.II.3** of sounds, **great, loud**, ἀλαλητός, ἰαχή, πάταγος, ὀρυμαγδός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.138, 15.384, 21.9, 256}}; θόρυβοι, κωκυτός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 142 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1176; οὐκ ἔστι ὅκως τι νεῖκος ἔσται ἢ μέγα ἢ σμικρόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.62; μὴ φώνει μέγα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 574.}}
**A.II.4** generally, **great, mighty**, ὅρκος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.113}}; ὄλβος, τιμά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.56, “P.” 4.148}}; μ. λόγος, μῦθος, a **great** story, rumour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 732, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 226 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐρώτημα a **big**, i. e. **difficult**, question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 275d, “Hp.Ma.” 287b}}; **weighty, important**, τόδε μεῖζον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.291}}; μέγα ποιέεσθαί τι to esteem **of great importance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, compare 9.111; μέγα γενέσθαι εἴς τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.6; μ. ὑπάρχειν πρός τι [prev. author] “Mem.” 2.3.4; μέγα διαφέρειν εἴς τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 78o c}}; οὐκ ἂν εἴη παρὰ μέγα τὸ δικολογεῖν not of **great importance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.85 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; τὸ δὲ μέγιστον and what is **most important**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.70, compare 1.142}}; οἱ μέγιστοι καιροί the **most pressing** emergencies, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.44}}; μ. ὠνησάμενοι χρημάτων for **large** sums, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.50.3, etc.}}
**A.II.5** with a bad sense, **over-great**, μέγα εἰπεῖν to speak **big**, and so provoke divine wrath, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.288; λίην μέγα εἶπες 3.227, 16.243; μέγα ἔργον 3.261, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.64; ἔργων μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1546 (_anapaest meter_); ὠμὸν τὸ βούλευμα καὶ μ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.36}}; ἔπος μ., μ. λόγοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 423 (Lyric poetry), “Ant.” 1350 (_anapaest meter_); μ. γλῶσσα [prev. work] 127 (_anapaest meter_)}}; μηδὲν μέγ᾽ εἴπῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 386; μὴ μέγα λέγε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95b; μὴ μεγάλα λίαν λέγε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 835; μέγα φρονεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1078, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 6; μεγάλα φρονεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 988}}; μεγάλα, μεῖζον ἢ δικαίως πνεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 189, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 376 (Lyric poetry); μέγα τι παθεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.17; μὴ μέγα λέγων μεῖζον πάθῃς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1244.}}
**A.II.6** of style. **impressive**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 278}}; μεῖζον **more striking**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 103.}}
**A.II.7** of days, **long**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.714.}}
**B** _adverb_ μεγάλως [ᾰ] **greatly, mightily**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.432, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 429, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.16, 30, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.10, 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 28.1, etc.; strengthened, μάλα μ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.723; δμαθέντες μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 907 (Lyric poetry); with _adjectives_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.4, 7.190.}}
**B.II** more frequently _neuter_ _singular_ μέγα as _adverb_, **very much, exceedingly**, μ. χαῖρε **all** hail!, see l. for μάλα in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.402}}; especially with Verbs expressing strong feeling, μ. κεν κεχαροίατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.256; μ. κήδεται 2.27, etc.: with Verbs expressing power, might, μ. πάντων.. κρατέει 1.78; ὃς μ. πάντων.. ἤνασσε 10.32; πατρὸς μ. δυναμένοιο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.276, compare 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “Epigrammata” 15.1, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 950 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1358 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 141, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 366a; μ. δύνασθαι παρά τινι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.29; πλουτέειν μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32}}; or those expressing sound, **loudly**, μ. ἰάχειν, ἀῧσαι, βοῆσαι, εὔξασθαι, ἀμβῶσαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.333, 14.147, 17.334, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.239, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8 (also _plural_, μεγάλ᾽ εὔχετο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.450}}; μ. αὐδήσαντος, μ. ἤπυεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.505, 9.399): strengthened, μάλα μ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.321; μ. δ᾽ ἔβραχε φήγινος ἄξων 5.838, etc.: so in Trag. with all kinds of Verbs, μ. στένειν, σθένειν, χλίειν, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 711 (Lyric poetry), 938, “Ch.” 137}}: also in _plural_, μεγάλα.. δυστυχεῖς {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eu.” 791 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B.II.2** of Space, **far**, μέγα προθορών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.363}}; ἄνευθε μέγα **far** away,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|22.88; οὐκ ἂν μέγα τι τῆς ἀληθείας παρεξέλθοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 66b.}}
**B.II.3** with _adjectives_, as μέγ᾽ ἔξοχος, μέγα νήπιος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.480, 16.46}}; μ. νήπιε Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.85; μ. πλούσιος [prev. author] 1.32, 7.190; ὦ μέγ᾽ εὔδαιμον κόρη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 647}}: with _comparative_ and _superlative_, **by far**, μέγ᾽ ἀμείνονες, ἄριστος, φέρτατος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.405, 2.82, 16.21.}}
**C** degrees of Comparison (regular μεγαλώτερος, -ώτατος late, {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 780.1, 2):}}
**C.1** _comparative_ μείζων, ον, _genitive_ ονος, _Epic dialect_, _Attic dialect_ (also Delph., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 246 H 260 (4th c.BC))}}; _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}}, _Doric dialect_, _Aeolic dialect_ μέζων, ον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 25, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 44, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26, “IG” 7.235.16 (from Oropus), 5(2).3.18 (from Tegea), 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 62}} (also early _Attic dialect_, {{Refs|“IG” 12.22.65, but [με] ίζων [prev. work]6.93, by analogy of ὀλείζων [prev. work]76,95)}}; _dative_ _plural_ μεζόνεσσι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|Diotogenes Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.62: written μέσδων in 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 7.6, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 19}}: compare **μέττον·** μεῖζον, {{NT+5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus (uncertain); later μειζότερος NT.3JOhn.4}} (used as title, **elder,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|POxy. 943.3 (6th c.AD), etc.)}}; μειζονώτερος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 434}}:—**greater, longer, taller**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.168, 9.202, etc.}}; frequently also, **too great**, γέρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 231a}}; Μηνόφιλος μείζων M. **the elder,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Ostr.Bodl. see C 2 (2nd c.AD)}}; as title, μειζων κώμης **headman** of a village, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1626.5 (4th c.AD)}}, etc.: generally, **the higher authority,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PLond. 2.214.22 (3rd c.AD), “POxy.” 1204.17 (_plural_, 3rd c.AD)}}; οὔτε μεῖζον οὔτε ἔλαττον, a strong form of denial, **nothing whatever**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 4; οὐδαμὰ προὔφηνεν οὔτε μείζον᾽ οὔτ᾽ ἐλάττονα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 324}}. _adverb_ μειζόνως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1121, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 13.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.21, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ μεζόνως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.128, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.80, etc.}}: _neuter_ as _adverb_, μεῖζον σθένειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 456, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 216; μ. ἰσχύειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 3.28; ἐπὶ μ. ἔρχεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 259.}}
**C.2** _superlative_ μέγιστος, η, ον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.412, etc.}}: _neuter_ as _adverb_, μέγιστον ἴσχυσε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 502}}; δυνάμενος μ., with _genitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.5, 9.9}}: with another _superlative_, μέγιστον ἐχθίστη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1323: in _plural_, χαῖρ᾽ ὡς μέγιστα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 462; θάλλει μ. [prev. author] “OC” 700 (Lyric poetry); τὰ μέγιστ᾽ ἐτιμάθης [prev. author] “OT” 1203 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐς μέγιστον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 521; ἐς τὰ μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.111}}:—late _superlative_ μεγιστότατος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 1.130.49 (1st-2nd c.AD)}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _majmán_- 'greatness', Latin _magnus_, Gothic _mikils_ 'great'.)

3174:
** μέγεθος**,
_Ionic dialect_ (not {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus)}} **μέγᾰθος** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (see. below), also 3rd c.BC: Philoxenus Epigrammaticus 2.19}}, εος, τό: (μέγας):— **greatness, magnitude**, opposed to πλῆθος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 1, etc.; πλῆθος μὲν.. ἐὰν ἀριθμητὸν ᾖ, μ. δὲ ἐὰν μετρητὸν ᾖ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1020a9.}}
**I** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always **stature**, of men and women, εἶδος ἀκιδνοτέρη μέγεθός τ᾽ εἰσάντα ἰδέσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.217, compare 6.152; ἐς μ. καὶ κάλλος ὁρώμενος 18.219, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 154c; θηλειῶν ἀρετὴ σώματος κάλλος καὶ μ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1361a7}}: then, generally, **size**, μύρμηκες μεγάθεα ἔχοντες κυνῶν ἐλάσσονα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.102; μ. λαβεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.3; ἡ ἐπίδοσις εἰς τὸ μ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 560a20}}; of sound, **loudness**, βοῆς μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.126: _accusative_ as}} _adverb_, λίθου λάμποντος μέγαθος, ={μεγάλως}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.44}}; but usually, **in size**, τεῖχος κατὰ τὸν Ἀθηνέων κύκλον.. τὸ μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.98; [δένδρεον] μέγαθος κατὰ συκέην μάλιστά κῃ [prev. author] 4.23; ὅσην δεῖ τὸ μ. τὴν πόλιν ποιεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 423b}}: also in _plural_, ποταμοὶ οὐ κατὰ τὸν Νεῖλον ἐόντες μεγάθεα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.10, compare 1.202; σμικροὶ τὰ μεγάθεα [prev. author] 3.107; κυαμιαῖοι τὰ μ. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 40; μεγέθη ἔργων καὶ διαθέσεων 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 43 G.}}
**I.2** frequently in _dative_, μεγέθει.. ἐκπρεπεστάτη **in stature**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 184; ἀνθρώπους μεγέθει μεγίστους καὶ ἥκιστα διαφόρους ἐς.. τὰ μεγέθεα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 12; πλήθεϊ μέγιστον καὶ μεγάθεϊ ὑψηλότατον, of a mountain, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.203}}; κρητῆρες μεγάθεϊ μεγάλοι{{Refs|[prev. work] 51; μεγάθεϊ μέγιστος [prev. author] 7.117; μ. περιμήκεας [prev. author] 2.108}}; σμικρός{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 74; ἐλάττω τῷ μ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 560b5.}}
**II** of quality and degree, **greatness, magnitude**, πόνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 593; τῆς παρανομίας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.15; τῆς ζημίας 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.3; τῆς κολάσεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 934b}}; **importance**, μ. ἐχούσας πράξεις {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isocrate” 6.}}
**II.2** **might, power**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 273; δαίμονος μεγέθει πάντα ἐπέχοντος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.1.}}
**II.3** **greatness, magnanimity**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 14; περί τι [prev. author] “Ant.” 24.}}
**II.4** Rhet., **loftiness, sublimity**, μ. περιτιθέναι τοῖς πράγμασιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 17, compare 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 5, 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.5, etc.; λόγων μ. 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 4.1, +others}}: in _plural_, **sublime objects**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.1, +others}}
**III** in Mathematics texts, **magnitude**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista 3; μ. ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 57d, compare 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de communi mathematica scientia” 3, etc.}}; **extension**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.4.11}}: in _plural_, **magnitudes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356c; τὰ μ. τὰ γεγραμμένα “IG” 7.3073.102 (Lebad.).}}
**III.2** Astron., **magnitude**, of stars, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.11, 1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 7 passim.}}
**IV** Grammars, **metrical length**, τὸ μέγιστον μ. τρίχρονον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 133.26, compare “Etymologicum Magnum” 419.50.}}
**IV.2** τὰ ἐν τῷ μέτρῳ μ. the recognized **lengths** of lines in a metre, {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 12.3.}}
**V** τὸ μ. τινός, as title, his **Highness,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|POxy. 2107.8 (3rd c.AD)}}; τὸ σὸν μ. {{Refs|“Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 8.10.12.1a.}}

3178:
** μέθη**, ἡ,
(for μεθύω: μέθη, compare πληθύω: πλήθᾱ) **strong drink**, καλῶς ἔχειν μέθης to be pretty well **drunk**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.20; ὑπερπλησθεὶς μέθης 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 779; μέθῃ βρεχθείς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 326; ἡ ἀπειρία τῆς μ. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.3.2; ἐσφαλμένος ὑπὸ μέθης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 396d; μανδραγόρᾳ ἢ μέθῃ συμποδίσαι τινά [prev. work] 488c}}; μ. εὐώδης παλαιός fragrant old **wine**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.82.}}
**II** **drunkenness**, μ. αἰώνιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 363d; πίνειν εἰς μέθην [prev. author] “Lg.” 775b}}; μέθῃ χρῆσθαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 674a; διὰ μέθης ποιήσασθαι.. τὴν συνουσίαν [prev. author] “Smp.” 176e; κωμάζειν μετὰ μέθης [prev. author] “Lg.” 637b; τρεῖς εἶχεν προφάσεις, μέθην, ἔρωτα, ἄγνοιαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.38}}: _plural_, **carousals**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 159, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 682e; ἐν μέθαις [prev. author] “Phdr.” 256c, cf. LXX.Jdt.13.15, NT.Rom.13.13, etc.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, ὑπὸ μέθης τοῦ φόβου ναυτιᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 639b, compare 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 831.18; μ. νηφαλίῳ κατασχεθεὶς ὥσπερ οἱ κορυβαντιῶντες 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.16, compare 2.320.}}
**III** Μέθη personified, in Art, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 2.27.3.}}

3179:
** μεθίστημι**:
causal, in _present_ and _imperfect_, _future_ and _aorist_ 1, **place in another way, change**, τοι ταῦτα μεταστήσω I **will change** thee this present, i. e. **give another instead**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.612; μ. τὰ νόμιμα πάντα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.65}}; ὄνομα, τύχην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 296, “Heracl.” 935; τὸ μέγα εἰς οὐδὲν χρόνος μ. [prev. author] “fragment” 304 (Lyric poetry); μ. νόμους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.64; ταύτην τὴν πολιτείαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 562c; ἐκ τοῦ παρόντος κόσμου τὴν πόλιν μεταστήσας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48}}; ἐς ὀλιγαρχίαν μ. [τὴν πολιτείαν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.24; ἐξ ὀλιγαρχίας ἐς τὸ δημοκρατεῖσθαι μ. τοὺς Βυζαντίους [prev. work] 4.8.27; τὰ ἐκεῖ πάντα πρὸς Λακεδαιμονίους [prev. work] 2.2.5}}; also ἐκ τῆς καθεστηκυίας ἄλλην μ. [πολιτείαν] **introduce a new** polity, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1301b8; μ. βασιλείαν ἀντὶ τυραννίδος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 319d.}}
**2** with _genitive_ _partitive_, οὐ μεθίστησι τοῦ χρώματος he **changes** [nothing] **of** his colour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 398 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** of persons, **set free**, τινὰ νόσου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 463}}; κακῶν, πόνων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1442, “IT” 991, compare 775; ὕπνου [prev. author] “Or.” 133.}}
**II.2** **remove by killing**, αὑτόν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 18.6.2: so in}}_middle_, τὸν ἄνθρωπον{{Refs|[prev. work] 18.9.5.}}
**II.3** **remove from one place to another**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.57; ὠστράκιζον καὶ μεθίστασαν ἐκ τῆς πόλεως 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1284a21; ἐς ἄλλην χθόνα μ. πόδα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 49:}} — _aorist 1_ _middle_ μεταστήσασθαι **remove from oneself** or **from one's presence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.89, 8.101, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.12, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.79}}; **banish**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.129; μ. φρουρὰς ἐκ πόλεων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.44.4.}}
**B** _passive_, with _aorist 1_ μετεστάθην [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1202 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 26.6, also}}_aorist_ 2, _perfect_, and _pluperfect_ _active_:
**B.I** of persons, **stand among** or **in the midst of**, with _dative_, ἑτάροισι μεθίστατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.514.}}
**B.I.2** **change one's position**, τυράννοις ἐκποδὼν μεθίστασο **make way** for them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 40}}; **depart**, παλαιὸν εἰς ἴχνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 538 (Lyric poetry); ἐκ τῆς τάξιος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.58; ἐκ τυραννικοῦ κύκλου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 750; ἔξω τῆς οἰκουμένης 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.165; ἐκ φωτὸς εἰς σκότος μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 518a: with _genitive_, δεῦρ᾽ Ἰωλκίας χθονός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 551; θρόνων [prev. author] “Ph.” 75; μ. φυγῇ [prev. author] “Med.” 1295: _absolutely_, μετάσταθ᾽, ἀπόβαθι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 162 (Lyric poetry), compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.69}}; ὅταν μεταστῇ [ὄλβος] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 646.6.}}
**B.I.3** with _genitive_ _of things_, **change, cease from**, κότου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 900; ξηρῶν τρόπων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1451 (Lyric poetry), compare “Pl.” 365}}; λύπης, κακῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1122, “Hel.” 856}}; μ. βίου die, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] Alcaeus21 (also μ. alone, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.4.2, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1104c}}; ἑκὼν μ. commit suicide, Vett. Val.94.9); μ. φρενῶν **change** from **one's** former mind, **change one's** mind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 944.}}
**B.I.4** **go over to** another party, **revolt**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.35, etc.; ἀπό τινος [prev. author] 8.76}}; παρά or πρός τινα, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.107, 130.}}
**B.I.5** **to be banished**, ὑπό τινων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 26.6.}}
**B.II** of things, **change, alter**, either **for the better**, τῆς τύχης εὖ μετεστεώσης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.118; ἐς τὸ λῷον.. μεθέστηκεν κέαρ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 911}}; or **for the worse**, ἐξ ἧς [πολιτείας] ἡ ὀλιγαρχία μετέστη from which oligarchy **arose by a change**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 553e, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.24, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1301a22, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.9.10}}; εἴ τι μὴ δαίμων.. μεθέστηκε στρατῷ **hath changed** for them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 158 (troch.); νέος μεθέστηκ᾽ ἐκ γέροντος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 796.}}
**B.II.2** in Medicine texts, of pains, **change position**, εἰς τὴν ἄνω χώραν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.652.}}

3180:
** μεθοδ-εία**
or **μεθοδ-ία**, ἡ, **craft, wiliness,** {{NT|NT.Eph.4.14: _plural_, μ. τοῦ διαβόλου [prev. work]6.11.}}
**II** **method of collecting taxes** or **debts** (in form μεθοδία), {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1134.9 (5th c.AD), 136.18 (6th c.AD).}}

3182:
** μεθύσκω**,
_future_ -ύσω [ῠ] {{LXX|LXX.Deu.32.42:}}_aorist 1_ ἐμέθῠσα{{LXX|LXX.2Ki.11.13}}, _Epic dialect_ -υσσα {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 3.11, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.260 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus)}}; _infinitive_ μεθύσαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor (see. below)}}:—_passive_, _future_ μεθυσθήσομαι {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Hos.14.8, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Luct.” 13, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.118}}: _aorist_ ἐμεθύσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 117, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 167, etc.}}; _Aeolic dialect_ _infinitive_ μεθύσθην {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 35}}: _perfect_ μεμέθυσμαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Hedylus Epigrammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.176d:}} —Causal of μεθύω, **make drunk, intoxicate**, Διόνυσος οἶδε τὸ μεθύσαι μόνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 214; μ. ἑαυτὴν οἴνῳ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Syr.D.” 22: _metaphorically_, πάνθ᾽ ὅσα δι᾽ ἡδονῆς μεθύσκοντα παράφρονας ποιεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 649d; τὴν αἴσθησιν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Odoribus” 46; Ἀθηνᾶ μεθύσασα ὕπνῳ τοὺς βαρβάρους Vett. Val. 347.26.}}
**2** **give to drink**, θηλὴ μεθύσκει με μητρῴη {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 89.9}}; **moisten**, βωμοὺς ἐν γάλακτι, τέφρην, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.99 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus), 11.8.}}
**II** _passive_, ={μεθύω}, **drink freely, get drunk**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus [same place], 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.133, etc.}}; ὀδμῇ, οἴνῳ,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 202; πίνων οὐ μεθύσκεται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.11: in}}_aorist_ ἐμεθύσθην, **to be drunk**, ἀνὴρ ὁκόταν μεθυσθῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus [same place]; ἅπαξ μεθυσθείς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 167, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1252; ἀνθρώπους οἵους μεθυσθέντας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.19: with _genitive_, νέκταρος}} **with** nectar, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 203b: _metaphorically_, ὅταν πόλις [ἐλευθερίας] μεθυσθῇ [prev. author] “R.” 562d: with _dative_, ταῖς ἐξουσίαις}} **with** power, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.74:—in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Steril.” 218 μεθυσκέτω is corrupt for μεθυσκέσθω.}}
**II.2** **to be filled** with food, μ. σίτῳ {{LXX|LXX.Hos.14.8}}; compare μεθύει· πεπλήρωται, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

3183:
** μέθῠσος**,
**drunk with wine**, properly only _feminine_, μέθυσος Ἀμαζών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus 34 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; μεθύση γραῦς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 555, compare “V.” 1402, 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 129, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 6.25.}}
**2** later also, of men, μεθύσους τοὺς ἐμπόρους ποιεῖ {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 67.1, compare “Comica Adespota” 384, LXX.Pro.23.21, NT.1Cor.5.11, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 5, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 55, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 3.195}}; **drunken, intemperate**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cebes Philosophus 34, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Caesares” 330c.}}

3184:
** μεθύω** [ῠ],
(μέθυ), only _present_ and _imperfect_: _future_ and _aorist_ _active_ belong to μεθύσκω (μεθύσας is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 28.211}}; μεθύσαντας is _falsa lectio_ for -τες in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.239a)}}_aorist_ being supplied by _passive_ of μεθύσκω:— **to be drunken with wine**, νευστάζων κεφαλῇ, μεθύοντι ἐοικώς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.240}}; μεθύων, opposed to νήφων, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 478, 627, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 4.12, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 128, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1048, “PHal.” 1.193 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; μ. ὑπὸ τοῦ οἴνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.26}}; τὸ μεθύειν **drunkenness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 187.2, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 43; τὸ μ. πημονῆς λυτήριον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 758.}}
**II** _metaphorically_,
**II.1** of things, **to be drenched, steeped** in any liquid, with _dative_, e.g. βοείην.. μεθύουσαν ἀλοιφῇ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.390; μεθύων ἐλαίῳ λύχνος 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 114.1; [χείμαρρος] ὄμβροισι μ. “Anthologia Graeca” 9.277 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.2** of persons, **to be intoxicated** with passion, pride, etc., ὑπὸ τῆς Ἀφροδίτης {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.21; ὑπὸ τρυφῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 121a; ἔρωτι 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 19; τῷ μεγέθει τῶν πεπραγμένων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.49; περὶ τὰς ἡδονάς 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.22.1; οὐ μ. τὴν φρόνησιν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 301; μ. τὸ φίλημα “Anthologia Graeca” 5.304.}}
**II.2.b** **to be stupefied, stunned**, πληγαῖς μεθύων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.98; ἐξ ὀδυνάων 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 5.228, cf. Nonn.[same place]}}

3185:
** μέγᾰς**, μεγάλη [ᾰ], μέγα [ᾰ],
_genitive_ μεγάλου, ης, ου, _dative_ μεγάλῳ, ῃ, ῳ, _accusative_ μέγᾰν, μεγάλην, μέγα [ᾰ]; dual μεγάλω, α, w; _plural_ μεγάλοι, μεγάλαι, μεγάλα, etc.: the stem μεγάλο- is never used in _singular_ _nominative_ and _accusative_ _masculine_ and _neuter_, and only once in _vocative_ _masculine_,
**A** ὦ μεγάλε Ζεῦ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 822 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**A.I** **big**, of bodily size: frequently of stature, εἶδος.. μ. ἦν ὁράασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.4; κεῖτο μ. μεγαλωστί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.776; ἠΰς τε μ. τε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9. 508}}; φῶτα μέγαν καὶ καλόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 513; καλή τε μεγάλη τε 15.418; κάρτα μεγάλη καὶ εὐειδής 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.1}}; φύσιν τίν᾽ εἶχε φράζ; Answ. μέγας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 742.}}
**A.I.b** **full-grown**, of age as shown by stature, νῦν δ᾽ ὅτε δὴ μ. εἰμί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.314; μήτε μέγαν μήτ᾽ οὖν νεαρῶν τινα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 358 (_anapaest meter_)}}; later, **elder** of two persons of the same name, Wilcken {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Chr.” 305 (3rd c.BC)}}; Σκιπίων ὁ μ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.35.9.}}
**A.I.c** of animals, μ. ἵπποι, βοῦς, σῦς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.839, 18.559, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.439; αἰετός 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).50.}}
**A.I.2** generally, **vast, high**, οὐρανός, ὄρος, πύργος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.497, 16.297, 6.386}}; **wide**, πέλαγος, λαῖτμα θαλάσσης, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.179, 5.174}}; **long**, ἠϊών, αἰγιαλός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.31, 2.210: sometimes opposed to ὀλίγος, κῦμα οὔτε μέγ᾽ οὔτ᾽ ὀ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.94}}; but usually opposed to μικρός or σμικρός, πρὸς ἑαυτὸ ἕκαστον καὶ μ. καὶ σμικρόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 3; τὸ ἄπειρον ἐκ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 987b26, etc.}}
**A.II** of quality or degree, **great, mighty**, frequently epithet of gods, ὁ μ. Ζεύς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1052 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; μεγάλα θεά, of Demeter and Persephone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 683 (Lyric poetry)}}; θεοὶ μεγάλοι, of the Cabiri, {{Refs|“IG” 12(8).71 (from Imbros), etc.}}; Μήτηρ μ., of Cybele, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 1014.83 (Erythrae, 3rd c.BC), 1138.3 (Delos, 2nd c.BC)}}; Μήτηρ θεῶν μ. {{Refs|“OGI” 540.6 (from Pessinus), etc.}}; Ἴσιδος μ. μητρὸς θεῶν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PStrassb.” 81.14 (2nd c.BC)}}; μ. ἡ Ἄρτεμις Ἐφεσίων {{NT|NT.Act.19.28}}; τίς θεὸς μ. ὡς ὁ θεὸς ἡμῶ; {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.76(77).13; ὁ μ. θεός NT.Tit.2.13}}; of men, μ. ἠδὲ κραταιός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.382; ὀλίγος καὶ μ. 7th c.BC: Callinus Epicus 1.17, etc.}}; μέγας ηὐξήθη rose **to greatness**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.5; ἤρθη μ. [prev. work]8; βασιλεὺς ὁ μ.}}, i. e. the King of Persia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.188, etc. (θεῶν β. ὁ μ., of Zeus, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.34); βασιλεὺς μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 24 (_anapaest meter_)}}; as a title of special monarchs, Ἀρδιαῖος ὁ μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 615c; ὁ μ. Ἀλέξανδρος 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.3d; ὁ μ. ἐπικληθεὶς Ἀντίοχος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.2.7, etc.; μ. φίλος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 549; πλούτῳ τε κἀνδρείᾳ μ. [prev. author] “Tr.” 674; ἐπὶ μέγα ἦλθεν ἰσχύος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.97.}}
**A.II.2** **strong**, of the elements, etc., ἄνεμος, λαῖλαψ, Ζέφυρος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.200, 12.408, 14.458}}; of properties, passions, qualities, feelings, etc., of men, θάρσος, πένθος, ποθή, etc.,{{NT+8th c.BC+|9.381, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.254, 11.471, etc.; ἀρετή 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.193, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.5; θυμός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.496, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 702; κλέος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.446; ἄχος 9.9; πυρετός NT.Luke.4.38 (incorrect _accusative_ to 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.275)}}; ἡ μ. νοῦσος **epilepsy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.6.5, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(2).341.}}
**A.II.3** of sounds, **great, loud**, ἀλαλητός, ἰαχή, πάταγος, ὀρυμαγδός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.138, 15.384, 21.9, 256}}; θόρυβοι, κωκυτός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 142 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1176; οὐκ ἔστι ὅκως τι νεῖκος ἔσται ἢ μέγα ἢ σμικρόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.62; μὴ φώνει μέγα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 574.}}
**A.II.4** generally, **great, mighty**, ὅρκος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.113}}; ὄλβος, τιμά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.56, “P.” 4.148}}; μ. λόγος, μῦθος, a **great** story, rumour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 732, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 226 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐρώτημα a **big**, i. e. **difficult**, question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 275d, “Hp.Ma.” 287b}}; **weighty, important**, τόδε μεῖζον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.291}}; μέγα ποιέεσθαί τι to esteem **of great importance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, compare 9.111; μέγα γενέσθαι εἴς τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.6; μ. ὑπάρχειν πρός τι [prev. author] “Mem.” 2.3.4; μέγα διαφέρειν εἴς τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 78o c}}; οὐκ ἂν εἴη παρὰ μέγα τὸ δικολογεῖν not of **great importance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.85 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; τὸ δὲ μέγιστον and what is **most important**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.70, compare 1.142}}; οἱ μέγιστοι καιροί the **most pressing** emergencies, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.44}}; μ. ὠνησάμενοι χρημάτων for **large** sums, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.50.3, etc.}}
**A.II.5** with a bad sense, **over-great**, μέγα εἰπεῖν to speak **big**, and so provoke divine wrath, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.288; λίην μέγα εἶπες 3.227, 16.243; μέγα ἔργον 3.261, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.64; ἔργων μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1546 (_anapaest meter_); ὠμὸν τὸ βούλευμα καὶ μ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.36}}; ἔπος μ., μ. λόγοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 423 (Lyric poetry), “Ant.” 1350 (_anapaest meter_); μ. γλῶσσα [prev. work] 127 (_anapaest meter_)}}; μηδὲν μέγ᾽ εἴπῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 386; μὴ μέγα λέγε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95b; μὴ μεγάλα λίαν λέγε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 835; μέγα φρονεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1078, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 6; μεγάλα φρονεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 988}}; μεγάλα, μεῖζον ἢ δικαίως πνεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 189, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 376 (Lyric poetry); μέγα τι παθεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.17; μὴ μέγα λέγων μεῖζον πάθῃς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1244.}}
**A.II.6** of style. **impressive**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 278}}; μεῖζον **more striking**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 103.}}
**A.II.7** of days, **long**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.714.}}
**B** _adverb_ μεγάλως [ᾰ] **greatly, mightily**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.432, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 429, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.16, 30, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.10, 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 28.1, etc.; strengthened, μάλα μ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.723; δμαθέντες μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 907 (Lyric poetry); with _adjectives_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.4, 7.190.}}
**B.II** more frequently _neuter_ _singular_ μέγα as _adverb_, **very much, exceedingly**, μ. χαῖρε **all** hail!, see l. for μάλα in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.402}}; especially with Verbs expressing strong feeling, μ. κεν κεχαροίατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.256; μ. κήδεται 2.27, etc.: with Verbs expressing power, might, μ. πάντων.. κρατέει 1.78; ὃς μ. πάντων.. ἤνασσε 10.32; πατρὸς μ. δυναμένοιο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.276, compare 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “Epigrammata” 15.1, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 950 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1358 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 141, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 366a; μ. δύνασθαι παρά τινι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.29; πλουτέειν μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32}}; or those expressing sound, **loudly**, μ. ἰάχειν, ἀῧσαι, βοῆσαι, εὔξασθαι, ἀμβῶσαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.333, 14.147, 17.334, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.239, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8 (also _plural_, μεγάλ᾽ εὔχετο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.450}}; μ. αὐδήσαντος, μ. ἤπυεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.505, 9.399): strengthened, μάλα μ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.321; μ. δ᾽ ἔβραχε φήγινος ἄξων 5.838, etc.: so in Trag. with all kinds of Verbs, μ. στένειν, σθένειν, χλίειν, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 711 (Lyric poetry), 938, “Ch.” 137}}: also in _plural_, μεγάλα.. δυστυχεῖς {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eu.” 791 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B.II.2** of Space, **far**, μέγα προθορών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.363}}; ἄνευθε μέγα **far** away,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|22.88; οὐκ ἂν μέγα τι τῆς ἀληθείας παρεξέλθοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 66b.}}
**B.II.3** with _adjectives_, as μέγ᾽ ἔξοχος, μέγα νήπιος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.480, 16.46}}; μ. νήπιε Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.85; μ. πλούσιος [prev. author] 1.32, 7.190; ὦ μέγ᾽ εὔδαιμον κόρη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 647}}: with _comparative_ and _superlative_, **by far**, μέγ᾽ ἀμείνονες, ἄριστος, φέρτατος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.405, 2.82, 16.21.}}
**C** degrees of Comparison (regular μεγαλώτερος, -ώτατος late, {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 780.1, 2):}}
**C.1** _comparative_ μείζων, ον, _genitive_ ονος, _Epic dialect_, _Attic dialect_ (also Delph., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 246 H 260 (4th c.BC))}}; _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}}, _Doric dialect_, _Aeolic dialect_ μέζων, ον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 25, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 44, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26, “IG” 7.235.16 (from Oropus), 5(2).3.18 (from Tegea), 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 62}} (also early _Attic dialect_, {{Refs|“IG” 12.22.65, but [με] ίζων [prev. work]6.93, by analogy of ὀλείζων [prev. work]76,95)}}; _dative_ _plural_ μεζόνεσσι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|Diotogenes Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.62: written μέσδων in 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 7.6, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 19}}: compare **μέττον·** μεῖζον, {{NT+5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus (uncertain); later μειζότερος NT.3JOhn.4}} (used as title, **elder,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|POxy. 943.3 (6th c.AD), etc.)}}; μειζονώτερος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 434}}:—**greater, longer, taller**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.168, 9.202, etc.}}; frequently also, **too great**, γέρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 231a}}; Μηνόφιλος μείζων M. **the elder,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Ostr.Bodl. see C 2 (2nd c.AD)}}; as title, μειζων κώμης **headman** of a village, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1626.5 (4th c.AD)}}, etc.: generally, **the higher authority,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PLond. 2.214.22 (3rd c.AD), “POxy.” 1204.17 (_plural_, 3rd c.AD)}}; οὔτε μεῖζον οὔτε ἔλαττον, a strong form of denial, **nothing whatever**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 4; οὐδαμὰ προὔφηνεν οὔτε μείζον᾽ οὔτ᾽ ἐλάττονα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 324}}. _adverb_ μειζόνως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1121, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 13.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.21, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ μεζόνως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.128, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.80, etc.}}: _neuter_ as _adverb_, μεῖζον σθένειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 456, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 216; μ. ἰσχύειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 3.28; ἐπὶ μ. ἔρχεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 259.}}
**C.2** _superlative_ μέγιστος, η, ον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.412, etc.}}: _neuter_ as _adverb_, μέγιστον ἴσχυσε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 502}}; δυνάμενος μ., with _genitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.5, 9.9}}: with another _superlative_, μέγιστον ἐχθίστη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1323: in _plural_, χαῖρ᾽ ὡς μέγιστα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 462; θάλλει μ. [prev. author] “OC” 700 (Lyric poetry); τὰ μέγιστ᾽ ἐτιμάθης [prev. author] “OT” 1203 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐς μέγιστον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 521; ἐς τὰ μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.111}}:—late _superlative_ μεγιστότατος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 1.130.49 (1st-2nd c.AD)}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _majmán_- 'greatness', Latin _magnus_, Gothic _mikils_ 'great'.)

3186:
** μέγᾰς**, μεγάλη [ᾰ], μέγα [ᾰ],
_genitive_ μεγάλου, ης, ου, _dative_ μεγάλῳ, ῃ, ῳ, _accusative_ μέγᾰν, μεγάλην, μέγα [ᾰ]; dual μεγάλω, α, w; _plural_ μεγάλοι, μεγάλαι, μεγάλα, etc.: the stem μεγάλο- is never used in _singular_ _nominative_ and _accusative_ _masculine_ and _neuter_, and only once in _vocative_ _masculine_,
**A** ὦ μεγάλε Ζεῦ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 822 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**A.I** **big**, of bodily size: frequently of stature, εἶδος.. μ. ἦν ὁράασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.4; κεῖτο μ. μεγαλωστί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.776; ἠΰς τε μ. τε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9. 508}}; φῶτα μέγαν καὶ καλόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 513; καλή τε μεγάλη τε 15.418; κάρτα μεγάλη καὶ εὐειδής 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.1}}; φύσιν τίν᾽ εἶχε φράζ; Answ. μέγας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 742.}}
**A.I.b** **full-grown**, of age as shown by stature, νῦν δ᾽ ὅτε δὴ μ. εἰμί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.314; μήτε μέγαν μήτ᾽ οὖν νεαρῶν τινα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 358 (_anapaest meter_)}}; later, **elder** of two persons of the same name, Wilcken {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Chr.” 305 (3rd c.BC)}}; Σκιπίων ὁ μ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.35.9.}}
**A.I.c** of animals, μ. ἵπποι, βοῦς, σῦς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.839, 18.559, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.439; αἰετός 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).50.}}
**A.I.2** generally, **vast, high**, οὐρανός, ὄρος, πύργος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.497, 16.297, 6.386}}; **wide**, πέλαγος, λαῖτμα θαλάσσης, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.179, 5.174}}; **long**, ἠϊών, αἰγιαλός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.31, 2.210: sometimes opposed to ὀλίγος, κῦμα οὔτε μέγ᾽ οὔτ᾽ ὀ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.94}}; but usually opposed to μικρός or σμικρός, πρὸς ἑαυτὸ ἕκαστον καὶ μ. καὶ σμικρόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 3; τὸ ἄπειρον ἐκ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 987b26, etc.}}
**A.II** of quality or degree, **great, mighty**, frequently epithet of gods, ὁ μ. Ζεύς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1052 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; μεγάλα θεά, of Demeter and Persephone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 683 (Lyric poetry)}}; θεοὶ μεγάλοι, of the Cabiri, {{Refs|“IG” 12(8).71 (from Imbros), etc.}}; Μήτηρ μ., of Cybele, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 1014.83 (Erythrae, 3rd c.BC), 1138.3 (Delos, 2nd c.BC)}}; Μήτηρ θεῶν μ. {{Refs|“OGI” 540.6 (from Pessinus), etc.}}; Ἴσιδος μ. μητρὸς θεῶν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PStrassb.” 81.14 (2nd c.BC)}}; μ. ἡ Ἄρτεμις Ἐφεσίων {{NT|NT.Act.19.28}}; τίς θεὸς μ. ὡς ὁ θεὸς ἡμῶ; {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.76(77).13; ὁ μ. θεός NT.Tit.2.13}}; of men, μ. ἠδὲ κραταιός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.382; ὀλίγος καὶ μ. 7th c.BC: Callinus Epicus 1.17, etc.}}; μέγας ηὐξήθη rose **to greatness**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.5; ἤρθη μ. [prev. work]8; βασιλεὺς ὁ μ.}}, i. e. the King of Persia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.188, etc. (θεῶν β. ὁ μ., of Zeus, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.34); βασιλεὺς μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 24 (_anapaest meter_)}}; as a title of special monarchs, Ἀρδιαῖος ὁ μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 615c; ὁ μ. Ἀλέξανδρος 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.3d; ὁ μ. ἐπικληθεὶς Ἀντίοχος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.2.7, etc.; μ. φίλος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 549; πλούτῳ τε κἀνδρείᾳ μ. [prev. author] “Tr.” 674; ἐπὶ μέγα ἦλθεν ἰσχύος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.97.}}
**A.II.2** **strong**, of the elements, etc., ἄνεμος, λαῖλαψ, Ζέφυρος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.200, 12.408, 14.458}}; of properties, passions, qualities, feelings, etc., of men, θάρσος, πένθος, ποθή, etc.,{{NT+8th c.BC+|9.381, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.254, 11.471, etc.; ἀρετή 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.193, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.5; θυμός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.496, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 702; κλέος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.446; ἄχος 9.9; πυρετός NT.Luke.4.38 (incorrect _accusative_ to 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.275)}}; ἡ μ. νοῦσος **epilepsy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.6.5, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(2).341.}}
**A.II.3** of sounds, **great, loud**, ἀλαλητός, ἰαχή, πάταγος, ὀρυμαγδός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.138, 15.384, 21.9, 256}}; θόρυβοι, κωκυτός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 142 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1176; οὐκ ἔστι ὅκως τι νεῖκος ἔσται ἢ μέγα ἢ σμικρόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.62; μὴ φώνει μέγα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 574.}}
**A.II.4** generally, **great, mighty**, ὅρκος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.113}}; ὄλβος, τιμά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.56, “P.” 4.148}}; μ. λόγος, μῦθος, a **great** story, rumour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 732, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 226 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐρώτημα a **big**, i. e. **difficult**, question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 275d, “Hp.Ma.” 287b}}; **weighty, important**, τόδε μεῖζον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.291}}; μέγα ποιέεσθαί τι to esteem **of great importance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, compare 9.111; μέγα γενέσθαι εἴς τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.6; μ. ὑπάρχειν πρός τι [prev. author] “Mem.” 2.3.4; μέγα διαφέρειν εἴς τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 78o c}}; οὐκ ἂν εἴη παρὰ μέγα τὸ δικολογεῖν not of **great importance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.85 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; τὸ δὲ μέγιστον and what is **most important**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.70, compare 1.142}}; οἱ μέγιστοι καιροί the **most pressing** emergencies, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.44}}; μ. ὠνησάμενοι χρημάτων for **large** sums, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.50.3, etc.}}
**A.II.5** with a bad sense, **over-great**, μέγα εἰπεῖν to speak **big**, and so provoke divine wrath, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.288; λίην μέγα εἶπες 3.227, 16.243; μέγα ἔργον 3.261, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.64; ἔργων μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1546 (_anapaest meter_); ὠμὸν τὸ βούλευμα καὶ μ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.36}}; ἔπος μ., μ. λόγοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 423 (Lyric poetry), “Ant.” 1350 (_anapaest meter_); μ. γλῶσσα [prev. work] 127 (_anapaest meter_)}}; μηδὲν μέγ᾽ εἴπῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 386; μὴ μέγα λέγε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95b; μὴ μεγάλα λίαν λέγε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 835; μέγα φρονεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1078, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 6; μεγάλα φρονεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 988}}; μεγάλα, μεῖζον ἢ δικαίως πνεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 189, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 376 (Lyric poetry); μέγα τι παθεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.17; μὴ μέγα λέγων μεῖζον πάθῃς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1244.}}
**A.II.6** of style. **impressive**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 278}}; μεῖζον **more striking**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 103.}}
**A.II.7** of days, **long**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.714.}}
**B** _adverb_ μεγάλως [ᾰ] **greatly, mightily**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.432, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 429, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.16, 30, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.10, 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 28.1, etc.; strengthened, μάλα μ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.723; δμαθέντες μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 907 (Lyric poetry); with _adjectives_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.4, 7.190.}}
**B.II** more frequently _neuter_ _singular_ μέγα as _adverb_, **very much, exceedingly**, μ. χαῖρε **all** hail!, see l. for μάλα in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.402}}; especially with Verbs expressing strong feeling, μ. κεν κεχαροίατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.256; μ. κήδεται 2.27, etc.: with Verbs expressing power, might, μ. πάντων.. κρατέει 1.78; ὃς μ. πάντων.. ἤνασσε 10.32; πατρὸς μ. δυναμένοιο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.276, compare 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “Epigrammata” 15.1, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 950 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1358 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 141, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 366a; μ. δύνασθαι παρά τινι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.29; πλουτέειν μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32}}; or those expressing sound, **loudly**, μ. ἰάχειν, ἀῧσαι, βοῆσαι, εὔξασθαι, ἀμβῶσαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.333, 14.147, 17.334, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.239, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8 (also _plural_, μεγάλ᾽ εὔχετο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.450}}; μ. αὐδήσαντος, μ. ἤπυεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.505, 9.399): strengthened, μάλα μ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.321; μ. δ᾽ ἔβραχε φήγινος ἄξων 5.838, etc.: so in Trag. with all kinds of Verbs, μ. στένειν, σθένειν, χλίειν, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 711 (Lyric poetry), 938, “Ch.” 137}}: also in _plural_, μεγάλα.. δυστυχεῖς {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eu.” 791 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B.II.2** of Space, **far**, μέγα προθορών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.363}}; ἄνευθε μέγα **far** away,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|22.88; οὐκ ἂν μέγα τι τῆς ἀληθείας παρεξέλθοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 66b.}}
**B.II.3** with _adjectives_, as μέγ᾽ ἔξοχος, μέγα νήπιος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.480, 16.46}}; μ. νήπιε Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.85; μ. πλούσιος [prev. author] 1.32, 7.190; ὦ μέγ᾽ εὔδαιμον κόρη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 647}}: with _comparative_ and _superlative_, **by far**, μέγ᾽ ἀμείνονες, ἄριστος, φέρτατος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.405, 2.82, 16.21.}}
**C** degrees of Comparison (regular μεγαλώτερος, -ώτατος late, {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 780.1, 2):}}
**C.1** _comparative_ μείζων, ον, _genitive_ ονος, _Epic dialect_, _Attic dialect_ (also Delph., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 246 H 260 (4th c.BC))}}; _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}}, _Doric dialect_, _Aeolic dialect_ μέζων, ον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 25, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 44, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26, “IG” 7.235.16 (from Oropus), 5(2).3.18 (from Tegea), 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 62}} (also early _Attic dialect_, {{Refs|“IG” 12.22.65, but [με] ίζων [prev. work]6.93, by analogy of ὀλείζων [prev. work]76,95)}}; _dative_ _plural_ μεζόνεσσι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|Diotogenes Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.62: written μέσδων in 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 7.6, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 19}}: compare **μέττον·** μεῖζον, {{NT+5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus (uncertain); later μειζότερος NT.3JOhn.4}} (used as title, **elder,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|POxy. 943.3 (6th c.AD), etc.)}}; μειζονώτερος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 434}}:—**greater, longer, taller**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.168, 9.202, etc.}}; frequently also, **too great**, γέρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 231a}}; Μηνόφιλος μείζων M. **the elder,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Ostr.Bodl. see C 2 (2nd c.AD)}}; as title, μειζων κώμης **headman** of a village, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1626.5 (4th c.AD)}}, etc.: generally, **the higher authority,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PLond. 2.214.22 (3rd c.AD), “POxy.” 1204.17 (_plural_, 3rd c.AD)}}; οὔτε μεῖζον οὔτε ἔλαττον, a strong form of denial, **nothing whatever**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 4; οὐδαμὰ προὔφηνεν οὔτε μείζον᾽ οὔτ᾽ ἐλάττονα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 324}}. _adverb_ μειζόνως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1121, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 13.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.21, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ μεζόνως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.128, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.80, etc.}}: _neuter_ as _adverb_, μεῖζον σθένειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 456, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 216; μ. ἰσχύειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 3.28; ἐπὶ μ. ἔρχεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 259.}}
**C.2** _superlative_ μέγιστος, η, ον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.412, etc.}}: _neuter_ as _adverb_, μέγιστον ἴσχυσε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 502}}; δυνάμενος μ., with _genitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.5, 9.9}}: with another _superlative_, μέγιστον ἐχθίστη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1323: in _plural_, χαῖρ᾽ ὡς μέγιστα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 462; θάλλει μ. [prev. author] “OC” 700 (Lyric poetry); τὰ μέγιστ᾽ ἐτιμάθης [prev. author] “OT” 1203 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐς μέγιστον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 521; ἐς τὰ μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.111}}:—late _superlative_ μεγιστότατος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 1.130.49 (1st-2nd c.AD)}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _majmán_- 'greatness', Latin _magnus_, Gothic _mikils_ 'great'.)

3187:
** μέγᾰς**, μεγάλη [ᾰ], μέγα [ᾰ],
_genitive_ μεγάλου, ης, ου, _dative_ μεγάλῳ, ῃ, ῳ, _accusative_ μέγᾰν, μεγάλην, μέγα [ᾰ]; dual μεγάλω, α, w; _plural_ μεγάλοι, μεγάλαι, μεγάλα, etc.: the stem μεγάλο- is never used in _singular_ _nominative_ and _accusative_ _masculine_ and _neuter_, and only once in _vocative_ _masculine_,
**A** ὦ μεγάλε Ζεῦ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 822 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**A.I** **big**, of bodily size: frequently of stature, εἶδος.. μ. ἦν ὁράασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.4; κεῖτο μ. μεγαλωστί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.776; ἠΰς τε μ. τε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9. 508}}; φῶτα μέγαν καὶ καλόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 513; καλή τε μεγάλη τε 15.418; κάρτα μεγάλη καὶ εὐειδής 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.1}}; φύσιν τίν᾽ εἶχε φράζ; Answ. μέγας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 742.}}
**A.I.b** **full-grown**, of age as shown by stature, νῦν δ᾽ ὅτε δὴ μ. εἰμί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.314; μήτε μέγαν μήτ᾽ οὖν νεαρῶν τινα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 358 (_anapaest meter_)}}; later, **elder** of two persons of the same name, Wilcken {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Chr.” 305 (3rd c.BC)}}; Σκιπίων ὁ μ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.35.9.}}
**A.I.c** of animals, μ. ἵπποι, βοῦς, σῦς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.839, 18.559, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.439; αἰετός 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).50.}}
**A.I.2** generally, **vast, high**, οὐρανός, ὄρος, πύργος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.497, 16.297, 6.386}}; **wide**, πέλαγος, λαῖτμα θαλάσσης, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.179, 5.174}}; **long**, ἠϊών, αἰγιαλός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.31, 2.210: sometimes opposed to ὀλίγος, κῦμα οὔτε μέγ᾽ οὔτ᾽ ὀ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.94}}; but usually opposed to μικρός or σμικρός, πρὸς ἑαυτὸ ἕκαστον καὶ μ. καὶ σμικρόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 3; τὸ ἄπειρον ἐκ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 987b26, etc.}}
**A.II** of quality or degree, **great, mighty**, frequently epithet of gods, ὁ μ. Ζεύς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1052 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; μεγάλα θεά, of Demeter and Persephone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 683 (Lyric poetry)}}; θεοὶ μεγάλοι, of the Cabiri, {{Refs|“IG” 12(8).71 (from Imbros), etc.}}; Μήτηρ μ., of Cybele, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 1014.83 (Erythrae, 3rd c.BC), 1138.3 (Delos, 2nd c.BC)}}; Μήτηρ θεῶν μ. {{Refs|“OGI” 540.6 (from Pessinus), etc.}}; Ἴσιδος μ. μητρὸς θεῶν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PStrassb.” 81.14 (2nd c.BC)}}; μ. ἡ Ἄρτεμις Ἐφεσίων {{NT|NT.Act.19.28}}; τίς θεὸς μ. ὡς ὁ θεὸς ἡμῶ; {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.76(77).13; ὁ μ. θεός NT.Tit.2.13}}; of men, μ. ἠδὲ κραταιός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.382; ὀλίγος καὶ μ. 7th c.BC: Callinus Epicus 1.17, etc.}}; μέγας ηὐξήθη rose **to greatness**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.5; ἤρθη μ. [prev. work]8; βασιλεὺς ὁ μ.}}, i. e. the King of Persia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.188, etc. (θεῶν β. ὁ μ., of Zeus, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.34); βασιλεὺς μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 24 (_anapaest meter_)}}; as a title of special monarchs, Ἀρδιαῖος ὁ μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 615c; ὁ μ. Ἀλέξανδρος 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.3d; ὁ μ. ἐπικληθεὶς Ἀντίοχος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.2.7, etc.; μ. φίλος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 549; πλούτῳ τε κἀνδρείᾳ μ. [prev. author] “Tr.” 674; ἐπὶ μέγα ἦλθεν ἰσχύος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.97.}}
**A.II.2** **strong**, of the elements, etc., ἄνεμος, λαῖλαψ, Ζέφυρος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.200, 12.408, 14.458}}; of properties, passions, qualities, feelings, etc., of men, θάρσος, πένθος, ποθή, etc.,{{NT+8th c.BC+|9.381, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.254, 11.471, etc.; ἀρετή 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.193, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.5; θυμός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.496, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 702; κλέος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.446; ἄχος 9.9; πυρετός NT.Luke.4.38 (incorrect _accusative_ to 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.275)}}; ἡ μ. νοῦσος **epilepsy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.6.5, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(2).341.}}
**A.II.3** of sounds, **great, loud**, ἀλαλητός, ἰαχή, πάταγος, ὀρυμαγδός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.138, 15.384, 21.9, 256}}; θόρυβοι, κωκυτός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 142 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1176; οὐκ ἔστι ὅκως τι νεῖκος ἔσται ἢ μέγα ἢ σμικρόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.62; μὴ φώνει μέγα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 574.}}
**A.II.4** generally, **great, mighty**, ὅρκος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.113}}; ὄλβος, τιμά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.56, “P.” 4.148}}; μ. λόγος, μῦθος, a **great** story, rumour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 732, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 226 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐρώτημα a **big**, i. e. **difficult**, question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 275d, “Hp.Ma.” 287b}}; **weighty, important**, τόδε μεῖζον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.291}}; μέγα ποιέεσθαί τι to esteem **of great importance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, compare 9.111; μέγα γενέσθαι εἴς τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.6; μ. ὑπάρχειν πρός τι [prev. author] “Mem.” 2.3.4; μέγα διαφέρειν εἴς τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 78o c}}; οὐκ ἂν εἴη παρὰ μέγα τὸ δικολογεῖν not of **great importance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.85 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; τὸ δὲ μέγιστον and what is **most important**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.70, compare 1.142}}; οἱ μέγιστοι καιροί the **most pressing** emergencies, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.44}}; μ. ὠνησάμενοι χρημάτων for **large** sums, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.50.3, etc.}}
**A.II.5** with a bad sense, **over-great**, μέγα εἰπεῖν to speak **big**, and so provoke divine wrath, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.288; λίην μέγα εἶπες 3.227, 16.243; μέγα ἔργον 3.261, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.64; ἔργων μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1546 (_anapaest meter_); ὠμὸν τὸ βούλευμα καὶ μ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.36}}; ἔπος μ., μ. λόγοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 423 (Lyric poetry), “Ant.” 1350 (_anapaest meter_); μ. γλῶσσα [prev. work] 127 (_anapaest meter_)}}; μηδὲν μέγ᾽ εἴπῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 386; μὴ μέγα λέγε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95b; μὴ μεγάλα λίαν λέγε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 835; μέγα φρονεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1078, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 6; μεγάλα φρονεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 988}}; μεγάλα, μεῖζον ἢ δικαίως πνεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 189, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 376 (Lyric poetry); μέγα τι παθεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.17; μὴ μέγα λέγων μεῖζον πάθῃς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1244.}}
**A.II.6** of style. **impressive**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 278}}; μεῖζον **more striking**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 103.}}
**A.II.7** of days, **long**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.714.}}
**B** _adverb_ μεγάλως [ᾰ] **greatly, mightily**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.432, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 429, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.16, 30, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.10, 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 28.1, etc.; strengthened, μάλα μ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.723; δμαθέντες μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 907 (Lyric poetry); with _adjectives_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.4, 7.190.}}
**B.II** more frequently _neuter_ _singular_ μέγα as _adverb_, **very much, exceedingly**, μ. χαῖρε **all** hail!, see l. for μάλα in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.402}}; especially with Verbs expressing strong feeling, μ. κεν κεχαροίατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.256; μ. κήδεται 2.27, etc.: with Verbs expressing power, might, μ. πάντων.. κρατέει 1.78; ὃς μ. πάντων.. ἤνασσε 10.32; πατρὸς μ. δυναμένοιο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.276, compare 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “Epigrammata” 15.1, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 950 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1358 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 141, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 366a; μ. δύνασθαι παρά τινι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.29; πλουτέειν μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32}}; or those expressing sound, **loudly**, μ. ἰάχειν, ἀῧσαι, βοῆσαι, εὔξασθαι, ἀμβῶσαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.333, 14.147, 17.334, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.239, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8 (also _plural_, μεγάλ᾽ εὔχετο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.450}}; μ. αὐδήσαντος, μ. ἤπυεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.505, 9.399): strengthened, μάλα μ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.321; μ. δ᾽ ἔβραχε φήγινος ἄξων 5.838, etc.: so in Trag. with all kinds of Verbs, μ. στένειν, σθένειν, χλίειν, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 711 (Lyric poetry), 938, “Ch.” 137}}: also in _plural_, μεγάλα.. δυστυχεῖς {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eu.” 791 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B.II.2** of Space, **far**, μέγα προθορών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.363}}; ἄνευθε μέγα **far** away,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|22.88; οὐκ ἂν μέγα τι τῆς ἀληθείας παρεξέλθοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 66b.}}
**B.II.3** with _adjectives_, as μέγ᾽ ἔξοχος, μέγα νήπιος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.480, 16.46}}; μ. νήπιε Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.85; μ. πλούσιος [prev. author] 1.32, 7.190; ὦ μέγ᾽ εὔδαιμον κόρη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 647}}: with _comparative_ and _superlative_, **by far**, μέγ᾽ ἀμείνονες, ἄριστος, φέρτατος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.405, 2.82, 16.21.}}
**C** degrees of Comparison (regular μεγαλώτερος, -ώτατος late, {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 780.1, 2):}}
**C.1** _comparative_ μείζων, ον, _genitive_ ονος, _Epic dialect_, _Attic dialect_ (also Delph., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 246 H 260 (4th c.BC))}}; _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}}, _Doric dialect_, _Aeolic dialect_ μέζων, ον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 25, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 44, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26, “IG” 7.235.16 (from Oropus), 5(2).3.18 (from Tegea), 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 62}} (also early _Attic dialect_, {{Refs|“IG” 12.22.65, but [με] ίζων [prev. work]6.93, by analogy of ὀλείζων [prev. work]76,95)}}; _dative_ _plural_ μεζόνεσσι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|Diotogenes Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.62: written μέσδων in 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 7.6, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 19}}: compare **μέττον·** μεῖζον, {{NT+5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus (uncertain); later μειζότερος NT.3JOhn.4}} (used as title, **elder,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|POxy. 943.3 (6th c.AD), etc.)}}; μειζονώτερος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 434}}:—**greater, longer, taller**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.168, 9.202, etc.}}; frequently also, **too great**, γέρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 231a}}; Μηνόφιλος μείζων M. **the elder,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Ostr.Bodl. see C 2 (2nd c.AD)}}; as title, μειζων κώμης **headman** of a village, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1626.5 (4th c.AD)}}, etc.: generally, **the higher authority,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PLond. 2.214.22 (3rd c.AD), “POxy.” 1204.17 (_plural_, 3rd c.AD)}}; οὔτε μεῖζον οὔτε ἔλαττον, a strong form of denial, **nothing whatever**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 4; οὐδαμὰ προὔφηνεν οὔτε μείζον᾽ οὔτ᾽ ἐλάττονα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 324}}. _adverb_ μειζόνως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1121, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 13.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.21, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ μεζόνως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.128, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.80, etc.}}: _neuter_ as _adverb_, μεῖζον σθένειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 456, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 216; μ. ἰσχύειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 3.28; ἐπὶ μ. ἔρχεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 259.}}
**C.2** _superlative_ μέγιστος, η, ον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.412, etc.}}: _neuter_ as _adverb_, μέγιστον ἴσχυσε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 502}}; δυνάμενος μ., with _genitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.5, 9.9}}: with another _superlative_, μέγιστον ἐχθίστη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1323: in _plural_, χαῖρ᾽ ὡς μέγιστα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 462; θάλλει μ. [prev. author] “OC” 700 (Lyric poetry); τὰ μέγιστ᾽ ἐτιμάθης [prev. author] “OT” 1203 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐς μέγιστον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 521; ἐς τὰ μ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.111}}:—late _superlative_ μεγιστότατος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 1.130.49 (1st-2nd c.AD)}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _majmán_- 'greatness', Latin _magnus_, Gothic _mikils_ 'great'.)

3188:
** μέλᾰν**, ᾰνος, τό,
(_neuter_ of μέλας) **ink**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 276c; τὸ μ. τρίβων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.258, compare 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.66, etc.; μ. γραφικόν 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.69}}; used of a drawing material capable of erasure, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 3.72.}}
**2** μ. Ἰνδικόν **indigo,** {{Refs|Peripl.M.Rubr. 39.}}
**II** **iris** of the eye, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 491b21.}}
**II.b** **cornea**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.772.}}
**II.2** = {αἰδοῖον}, τὸ μ. τῷ μ. συναρμόσαι {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.403.}}

3189:
** μέλᾱς**
(μέλᾰς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhianus Epicus 58, where μέγας is corrupt for μέλας), μέλαινα, μέλᾰν}}; _genitive_ μέλᾰνος, μελαίνης, μέλᾰνος, etc.; _Epic dialect_ _dative_ μείλανι (for the sake of meter) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.79}}: _Aeolic dialect_ _nominative_ **μέλαις**, from *μέλανς, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 1.123, Greg.Cor. p.599 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, hence restored in 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 57}}:—**black, dark**: in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} generally, **dark** in colour, οἶνος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.265}}; μέλαν αἷμα, κῦμα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.149, 23.693; γαῖα μέλαινα 2.699, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 5.2; ὕδωρ μέλαν Αἰσήποιο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.825, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.359, νηῦς μ.}}, from its being **pitched over**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.300, +others}}; of men, **dark, swarthy**, μέλανας δὲ ἀνδρικοὺς ἰδεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 474e; ἰσχυρός τις ἦν, μ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.71}}; τὰ μ. **black marks** about the ears of dogs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.23.}}
**II** **dark, murky**, ἕσπερος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.423; εὐφρόνα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.3.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, **black, dark**, θάνατος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.834, etc.; κήρ [prev. work] 859, etc.}}; ὀδύναι {{Refs|4.117, etc. (the origin of the metaphor is seen in the phrases θανάτου μ. νέφος, ἄχεος νεφέλη μ., 16. 350, 18.22)}}; μ. τύχα, ἀρά, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 89 (Lyric poetry), “Th.” 832 (Lyric poetry); Ἐρινύς [prev. work] 993 (Lyric poetry),compare “Eu.” 52; ἄτα [prev. author] “Ag.” 769 (Lyric poetry); Ἄρης [prev. work] 1511 (Lyric poetry)}}; Ἅιδης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 29; Ἅιδου μ. ἀνάγκα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1388 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; ἡμέραι μέλαιναι, = Latin _dies atri_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 27.}}
**III.2** of the voice, **indistinct**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 106a25, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 4.44}}; βραχὺ καὶ μ. φώνημα, of Nero, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 61.20.}}
**III.3** **dark, obscure, enigmatical**, ἱστορίη {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.347 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus).}}
**III.4** of character, **dark, malignant**, μ. φρήν, καρδία, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 42.4, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 123.4; μ. ἄνθρωποι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.12d}}; μ. ἦθος M. Ant.4.28.
**IV** _comparative_ μελάντερος, α, ον, **blacker, very black**, τοῦ δ᾽ οὔ τι μελάντερον ἔπλετο ἔσθος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.94: proverbial of thick darkness, [νέφος] μελάντερον ἠΰτε πίσσα (see. ἠΰτε) 4.277}}: _superlative_ μελάντατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 14, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 580, etc.}}:— _comparative_ also μελανώτερος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.4.12}}: _superlative_ μελαινότατος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.68 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus), “epigram Gr.” 320.4 (from Thyatira).}}
**V** **μέλαινα** (i.e. νοῦσος), ἡ, in Medicine texts, of diseases causing black secretions (such as **melaena**), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.73.}}
**VI** μέλαν, τό, see entry (Cf. Sanskrit _málam_ 'dirt', **malinás** 'dirty', Lett. **melns** 'black'.)

3191:
** μελετ-άω**,
_future_ -ήσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.80, etc., -ήσομαι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 6, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.24.2:—post- 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} Verb, **take thought** or **care for**, with _genitive_, βίου, ἔργου, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 316, 443: with _accusative_ _of things_, δόξαν ἀρετῆς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.11}}; of a physician, **treat** a case, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 27, etc.:}} —_passive_ {{Refs|of the patient, [prev. work] 26, etc.}}
**2** **attend to, study**, οὐ δύναμαι ἀκοῦσαι, τοῦτο μελετῶν (i.e. τὸ ἀκοῦσαι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.115; πλήθους δόξας μεμελετηκώς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 260c.}}
**II** **pursue, exercise**, [μαντείην] {{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 557}}; μ. τοῦτο (i.e. ἡμεροδρόμην εἶναι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.105}}: frequently in _Attic dialect_, μ. σοφίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 511}}; τέχνας, ῥητορικήν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 511b, 511c, 448d}}; **practise**, ἤθη, γαστριμαργίας, ὕβρεις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 81e; ὄρχησιν [prev. author] “Lg.” 813e}}; [νόμους] {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 892 (Lyric poetry); ἀστοῖς ἴσα χρὴ μελετᾶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 171 (_anapaest meter_); τὴν τῶν πολεμικῶν ἄσκησιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1333b39: generally, μ. ἄδικα LXX.Job.27.4; ταῦτα μελέτα NT.1Tim.4.15}}; especially **practise** speaking, **con over** a speech **in one's mind**, λογάρια δύστηνα μελετήσας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.255; ἀπολογίαν [prev. author] 46.1}}; also, **deliver, declaim**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|(compare 11.5 b), λόγους 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 40.54:}} —_passive_, τὸ ναυτικὸν οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἐκ παρέργου μελετᾶσθαι naval warfare cannot **be practised** 'en amateur', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142}}; εὐταξία μετὰ κινδύνων μελετωμένη discipline **put in practice** on the battle-field, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.72, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 455c.}}
**II.2** with _infinitive_, μετρίως ἀλγεῖν μελετᾷ σοφία **practises** moderation in grief, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 46; λαλεῖν μεμελετήκασί που 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 119; also μ. τοξεύειν καὶ ἀκοντίζειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.12, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.3; μ. ποιεῖν καὶ λέγειν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.9}}; μ. ἀποθνῄσκειν, τεθνάναι, **practise** dying, death, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 67e, 81a, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 470.}}
**II.3** less frequently with _participle_, μ. κυβερνῶντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 1.20}}; with ὡς and _participle_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 5.5.47.}}
**II.4** with _preposition_, μ. ἔν τινι {{LXX|LXX.Josh.1.8, +others}}
**II.5** _absolutely_, **study, train oneself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 164, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.80, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.16; ἦν τὸ ἱππικὸν μεμελετηκός [prev. work] 6.4.10}}: with _dative_ modi, τόξῳ μ. καὶ ἀκοντίῳ {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 2.1.21}}; ἐν τῷ μὴ μελετῶντι by want of **practice**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142.}}
**II.5.b** especially **practise oratory, declaim**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228b}}; ἐπὶ τῶν καιρῶν μ. **extemporize a speech**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.43; Ἑλληνιστὶ μ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 4, compare 2.131a, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 6, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.24.2, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.145}}; of actors, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 904b3.}}
**II.6** in Medicine texts, of disease, **threaten**, μ. τὴν τοῦ καρκινώματος γένεσιν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Leonidas Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 16.43: _absolutely_, ἢν κρύβδην μελετήσῃ.. ἡ νοῦσος 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 2.12}}:—_passive_, ἀπειλᾷ καὶ μελετᾶται μανία {{Refs 4th c.BC+|7th c.AD: Stephanus Medicus “in Hp.” 1.99 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, compare 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 16.63.}}
**II.7** with _accusative_ _person_, **exercise, train** persons, ἐμελέτησεν [αὐτοὺς] ὡς εἶεν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.42}}: with _infinitive_, οὓς ἀναβαίνειν ἐπὶ τοὺς ἵππους μελετᾷ Φείδων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Mnesimachus Comicus 4.7:}} —_passive_, μελετώμενοι ὑπ᾽ αὐτῶν τὴν πτῆσιν, of eaglets, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.7.}}
**III** Grammars, **to be accustomed**, with _infinitive_, μεμελέτηκε τὸ τ ¯ εἰς θ ¯ τρέπεσθαι {{Refs 9th c.AD+|“Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 1.66; τὰ μὴ μελετήσανταπάσχεινσυναίρεσιν 9th c.AD: Theognostus Grammaticus “Can.” 145.25.}}
**III.b** in Medicine texts, **acquire a habit**, μελετησάντων ἐκπίπτειν βραχιόνων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.782}}:— _passive_, **become chronic**, μελετηθὲν τὸ πάθος {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 16.67.}}

3192:
** μέλῐ**, τό,
_genitive_ ῐτος, etc.; _dative_ μέλι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Philoxenus Epigrammaticus 3.17; _genitive_ _plural_ μελίτων 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 128.7 (nisi to be read μελιτῶν, compare μελιτόν)}}:—**honey**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.69, etc.; μ. χλωρόν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.631, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.234, 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 38.1; ξανθόν 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 47; παμφαές 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 612; τὸ μέλι τἀττικόν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 252, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 708; various kinds, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 190}}; said to be made from the palm (φοῖνιξ), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.193, compare 4.194; μ. θανάτου σύμβολον 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Antro Nympharum - Porphyrii Opuscula” 18.}}
**2** in comparisons, of anything sweet, especially of eloquence, μέλιτος γλυκίων ῥέεν αὐδή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.249, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10 (11). 98; Σοφοκλέους τοῦ μέλιτι κεχριμένου 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 581; ὕπνος γλυκίων μέλιτος 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 2.3; ἡ τῶν ἀνδρῶν [χολή] ἐστι πρὸς ἐκείνην μέλι 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 146.6: proverbial μήτε μοι μ. μήτε μέλισσα}}, of those who refuse to take 'the rough with the smooth', {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 113.}}
**II** **sweet gum** collected from certain trees, **manna**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 831b23; τὸ ὕον μ. 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 4.3.32}}; μ. ἄγριον, μαινόμενον, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.94, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 12.3.18}}. (Cf. Gothic _milip_, Latin _mel._)

3193:
** μελίσσ-ειος**, α, ον,
= {μελισσαῖος}, κηρίον μ. **honey**comb, [{{NT|NT.Luke.24.42 (variant{μελίσσιον})}}]; μελίσσειον or μελίσσιον alone, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus s. vv. νύμφη, σής, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus, Scholia 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 547}}; also μελίσσιον· σμῆνος, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II** μελίσσειον, τό, **beehive,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 467 (3rd c.BC).}}

3195:
** μέλλω**,
_imperfect_ ἔμελλον and ἤμελλον (see. below), _Epic dialect_ μέλλον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.278, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.232, 9.378, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.164}}; _Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_ μέλλεσκον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.240, 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 2.109}}: _future_ μελλήσω {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.15, NT.Matt.24.6}}: _aorist_ ἐμέλλησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.55, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.65, etc., and ἠμ- (see. below)}}:—_passive_ and _middle_, see below see.—Only _present_ and _imperfect_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}}, Lyric poetry, and Trag.: _aorist_ only in Prose (except {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus, see below): the}}_imperfect_ ἤμελλον with long augment is established by the metre in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 898, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 906, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 597, “Ra.” 1038 (both _anapaest meter_), 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1309 (cf. Scholia (at [prev. work])), 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 58}}: _aorist 1_ ἠμέλλησα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 259}}; ἤμελλον is not found in earlier _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., but occurs in Papyrus, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.146 (3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.145 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus (but ἔμελλον 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 7, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 25.3).}}
**I** **to be destined** or **likely to**, indicating an estimated certainty or strong probability in the present, past, or future (cf. Aristonic. cited in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.326, 11.817, 16.46, +others): a. with}}_present_ _infinitive_ (or its equivalent), of a probability in the present, ὅθι που μέλλουσιν ἄριστοι βουλὰς βουλεύειν where **belike** the best are holding counsel, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.326}}; ᾧ μέλλεις εὔχεσθαι to whom thou **doubtless** prayest, {{Refs|11.364}}; μέλλεις δὲ σὺ ἴδμεναι **doubtless** thou knowest, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.200}}; τὰ δὲ μέλλετ᾽ ἀκουέμεν **belike** you have heard it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.125, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.94; οὕτω που Διὶ μέλλει ὑπερμενέϊ φίλον εἶναι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.116}}; ὄλβον δὲ θεοὶ μέλλουσιν ὀπάζειν **methinks** it is the gods who give wealth, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.19}}; εἰ δ᾽ οὕτω τοῦτ᾽ ἐστίν, ἐμοὶ μέλλει φίλον εἶναι **you may be sure** it is my good pleasure, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.564. b. with}}_aorist_ _infinitive_, of a probability in the past, μέλλω που ἀπεχθέσθαι Διὶ πατρί I **must have** become hateful to father Zeus, {{Refs|21.83}}; κελευσέμεναι δέ σ᾽ ἔμελλε δαίμων a god **must surely have** bidden thee, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.274}}; πολλάκι που μέλλεις ἀρήμεναι you **must** often **have** prayed, {{Refs|22.322}}; μέλλω ἀθανάτους ἀλιτέσθαι I **must have** sinned against the immortals, {{Refs|4.377}}; ἄλλοτε δή ποτε μᾶλλον ἐρωῆσαι πολέμοιο μέλλω at any other time rather than this I **may have** drawn back.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.777}}; μέλλει μέν πού τις καὶ φίλτερον ἄλλον ὀλέσσαι before now, **no doubt**, a man **has** lost.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|24.46, compare 18.362; τοῦ δ᾽ ἤδη μέλλουσι κύνες ταχέες τ᾽ οἰωνοὶ ῥινὸν ἀπ᾽ ὀστεόφιν ἐρύσαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.133}}; of a destiny in the past, ἔμελλεν οἷ αὐτῷ θάνατον.. λιτέσθαι he **was fated to have** been praying for his own death, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.46}}; ἐπεὶ οὐκ ἄρ᾽ ἔμελλον ἑταίρῳ κτεινομένῳ ἐπαμῦναι since I **was** (i.e. **am**) not **destined to have** succoured my comrade when they were slaying him, {{Refs|18.98: with}}_present_ _infinitive_, οὐκ ἄρ᾽ ἔμελλες ἀνάλκιδος ἀνδρὸς ἑταίρους ἔδμεναι he **was to turn out** no helpless man whose comrades you ate, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.475. with with}}_future_ _infinitive_, of a destin y or probability in the future, ἅ οὐ τελέεσθαι ἔμελλον which **were** not **to be** accomplished, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.36}}; τάχα δ᾽ ἀνστήσεσθαι ἔμελλεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 694; ἐπεὶ οὐκ ἄρ᾽ ἔμελλον ἔγωγε νοστήσας οἶκόνδε.. εὐφρανέειν ἄλοχον 5.686, compare 12.113, 22.356, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.293, 384; μέλλον ἔτι ξυνέσεσθαι ὀϊζυῖ πολλῇ 7.270}}; περὶ τρίποδος γὰρ ἔμελλον θεύσεσθαι they **were to have** run.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.700, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 463; χρόνῳ ἔμελλέ σ᾽ Ἕκτωρ.. ἀποφθίσειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1027; ἔμελλον ἄρα παύσειν ποθ᾽ ὑμᾶς τοῦ κοάξ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 268; φεύγει; ἔμελλόν σ᾽ ἆρα κινήσειν ἐγώ [prev. author] “Nu.” 1301, compare “V.” 460, “Pl.” 103, “Ach.” 347: with}}_present_ _infinitive_, καὶ γὰρ ἐγώ ποτ᾽ ἔμελλον ἐν ἀνδράσιν ὄλβιος εἶναι I **had a chance** of being, **might have** been.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.138; μέλλεν ποτὲ οἶκος ὅδ᾽ ἀφνειὸς καὶ ἀμύμων ἔμμεναι, ὄφρ᾽ ἔτι κεῖνος ἀνὴρ ἐπιδήμιος ἦεν 1.232}}: with _aorist_ _infinitive_ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare(below 11), οὐδεὶς ἂν οὐδὲ μελλήσειε γενέσθαι ἀγαθός 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1105b11: with}}_infinitive_ understood, [τὰ μὲν] πάσχουσι, τὰ δὲ μέλλουσι [πάσχειν] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 814}}; ἀλλ᾽ οὐχ οὑμὸς τοῦτο πέπονθεν βίος οὐ μὰ Δί᾽ οὐδέ γε μέλλει no, not **likely**! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 551; οὐδὲν.. οὔτε ἐπάθετε οὔτε ἐμελλήσατε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.55; οὔτ᾽ ἐμὲ ἀπέφηνεν ἡ βουλὴ οὔτ᾽ ἐμέλλησεν 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.49.}}
**I.d** in εἰ clauses, εἰ μέλλει πόλις εἶναι if it **is to** be a city, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 324e: with}}_future_ _infinitive_, εἰ ἐμέλλομεν.. ἀνοίσειν if we **were to** refer.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 75b}}: with _aorist_ _infinitive_, εἰ μέλλομεν.. δηλῶσαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 713a, compare “Smp.” 184d, “Plt.” 268d, +others}}: so in _participle_, τὴν μέλλουσαν οἰκήσεσθαι πόλιν καλῶς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1261a3, etc.}}
**I.e** in final clauses, ξυνεπιμέλεσθαι ᾗ μέλλει ἄριστα ἕξειν, = {ᾗ ἄριστα ἕξει}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.39; εἴχομεν ἂν.. ἐπιστάτην λαβεῖν.. ὃς ἔμελλεν.. ποιήσειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 20b, compare 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Συριακή” 46, etc.}}
**I.f** in questions, the _infinitive_ being understood, τί οὐ μέλλω (μέλλεις, etc.); why **shouldn't** I? why **is it** not **likely** that I should?, i. e. **yes, of course**, τὸν υἱὸν ἑόρακας αὐτο; Answ. τί δ᾽ οὐ μέλλω (i.e. ἑορακέναι); **of course** I have, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.6}}; τί δ᾽ οὐ μέλλει, εἴπερ γε δρᾷ αὐτ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 605c}}; πῶς γὰρ οὐ μέλλε; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 78b, etc.}}; ἀλλὰ τί μέλλε; what (else) **would you expect**? i. e. **yes, of course**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 349d, “Hp.Mi.” 373d.}}
**II** **to be about to**, in purely temporal sense, with _future_ _infinitive_, Ἕκτορα δῖον ἔτετμεν ἀδελφεόν, εὖτ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἔμελλε στρέψεσθ᾽ ἐκ χώρης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.515}}; ὁ μέν μιν ἔμελλε γενείου.. ἁψάμενος λίσσεσθαι (perhaps _present_ _infinitive_), ὁ δ᾽ αὐχένα μέσσον ἔλασσε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|10.454; ἄλεισον ἀναιρήσεσθαι ἔμελλε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.9, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.544, 2.39, 6.52, 393}}; δειπνήσειν μέλλομεν, ἢ τ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 464, compare “Eq.” 931 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.8, etc.: with}}_present_ _infinitive_, τί μέλλεις δρᾶ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1379, “Th.” 215, compare “Ec.” 760, “Ach.” 493, “Av.” 498, +others; μέλλω μαίνεσθαι “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 1.23}}: more rarely with _aorist_ _infinitive_, παθεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 625; κτανεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 967 (nisi to be read κτενεῖν)}}; ἀναλαβεῖν, λιπεῖν, θανεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 292, “Heracl.” 709, “Med.” 393}}; ἀπολέσαι, λαβεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 366, “Ach.” 1159 (Lyric poetry); προσθεῖναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.92; οὐδὲ ἐμέλλησαν οὐδὲ διενοήθησαν ἐνθέσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.19: 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 316 wrongly condemns this _construction_}} —The _infinitive_ is sometimes omitted, τὸ μέλλειν ἀγαθά (i.e. πράσσειν or πράξειν) the **expectation** of good things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1182, compare “IA” 1118.}}
**III** **to be always going to do** without ever doing: hence, **delay, put off**, frequently in Trag. (also in _middle_ μέλλομαι, see infr. IV near the end): in this _signification_ usually followed by _present_ _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 678 (Lyric poetry), “OC” 1627, etc.}}; τοὺς ξυμμάχους.. οὐ μελλήσομεν τιμωρεῖν· οἱ δ᾽ οὐκέτι μέλλουσι κακῶς πάσχειν we **shall** not **delay** to succour our allies, for their sufferings **are** not **being delayed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.86: frequently with μὴ οὐ, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 627, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 540: with μή, τί μέλλομεν.. μὴ πράσσειν κακ; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1242}}: rarely followed by _aorist_ _infinitive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 299 (Lyric poetry), “Rh.” 673:}}_infinitive_ is frequently omitted, τί μέλλει; why **delayest thou**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 36, compare “Pers.” 407, “Ag.” 908, 1353, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 917, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.78, etc.; μακρὰ μ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 219 (Lyric poetry); Ἄρης στυγεῖ μέλλοντας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 723; ἴωμεν καὶ μὴ μέλλωμεν ἔτι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 712b}}; μέλλον τι.. ἔπος a **hesitating** word, which one hesitates to speak, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1002}}; μέλλων σφυγμός a **hesitating** pulse, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.653.}}
**IV** _participle_ μέλλων is used quasi-adjectivally, ὁ μ. χρόνος the **future** time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).7, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 839, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 111b28: Grammars, ὁ μέλλων}} **the future tense**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 638.23, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 69.28, etc.}}; ἡ μ. αὐτοῦ δύναμις his **future** power, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 494c; μ. φυλάξασθαι χρέος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.40}}; τὸν μ. βλαστόν (καρπόν codices) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.15.1: especially in _neuter_, τὸ μέλλον, τὰ μέλλοντα}} **things to come, the future**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.56, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 102, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138, 4.71, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 178e, etc.}}; opposed to to what is simply future (τὸ ἐσόμενον), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Divinatione per Somnia” 463b29, compare “GC” 337b4}}; εἰς τὸ μέλλον (i.e. ἔτος) {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Luke.13.9, compare “PLond.” 3.1231.4 (2nd c.AD), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 14:—also in}}_middle_, τὰ ἰσχυρότατα ἐλπιζόμενα μέλλεται your strongest pleas **are** hopes **in futurity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.111:}} — but
**V** _passive_ μέλλομαι, ὡς μὴ μέλλοιτο τὰ δέοντα that the necessary steps **might** not **be delayed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.47}}; ἐν ὅσῳ ταῦτα μέλλεται while these **delays are going on**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.37:}}_future_ μελλήσομαι uncertain reading in {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Bello Gothico” 2.30:}}_perfect_ _participle_ μεμελλημένος, = {μέλλων, σφυγμός} {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 9.308.}}

3196:
** μέλος**, εος, τό,
**limb**, in early writers always in _plural_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.131, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.47, etc. (κατὰ μέλος is corrupt for κατὰ μέρος in “hymnus ad Mercurium” 419)}}; μελέων ἔντοσθε within my **bodily frame**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 991 (Lyric poetry), compare “Eu.” 265 (Lyric poetry)}}; κατὰ μέλη (-εα) **limb by limb**, like{μελεϊστί}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.49, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.119; τὰ τοῦ σώματος μέλη καὶ μέρη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 795e}}; μέλη ποιεῖν **dismember**, {{LXX|LXX.2Mac.1.16}}: later in _singular_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.141, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 12.3, +others; ἡ κατὰ μέλος τομή 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.1.30.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, ἐσμὲν.. ἀλλήλων μέλη {{NT|NT.Rom.12.5, cf. NT.1Cor.6.15.}}
**3** **features, form**, οὐκέτ᾽ ἐγὼ.. γονέων μ. ὄψομαι {{Refs|“BMus.Inscription” 1077 (from Sudan).}}
**B** especially **musical member, phrase**: hence, **song, strain**, first in {{Refs|“Hymni Homerici” 19.16 (_plural_)}}, of the nightingale (the {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus word being μολπή),compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 761, etc.; μέλη βοῶν ἄναυλα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 699}}; especially of **lyric poetry**, τὸ Ἀρχιλόχου μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.1}}; ἐν μέλεϊ ποιέειν to write in **lyric strain**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.95, compare 2.135; ἐν μέλει ἤ τινι ἄλλῳ μέτρῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 607d, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 11; Ἀδμήτου μ. 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 236}}; μέλη, τά, **lyric poetry, choral songs**, opposed to Epic or Dramatic verse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 379a, 607a, +others; [μ.] ἐκ τριῶν συγκείμενον, λόγου τε καὶ ἁρμονίας καὶ ῥυθμοῦ [prev. work] 398d.}}
**B.b** **lyric portion** of the Comic παράβασις, {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus “Poëm.” 8.2.}}
**B.2** **music to which a song is set, tune**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1450a14}}; opposed to ῥυθμός, μέτρον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 502c}}; opposed to ῥυθμός, ῥῆμα, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 656c}}; Κρητικόν, Καρικόν, Ἰωνικὸν μ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 222, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 69.12, 14: _metaphorically_, ἐν μέλει}} **properly, correctly**, ἐν μ. φθέγγεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 227d}}; παρὰ μέλος **incorrectly, inopportunely**, πὰρ μ. ἔρχομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.69; παρὰ μ. φθέγξασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 28b, “Lg.” 696d; παρὰ μέλος λαμπρύνεσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1123a22, compare “EE” 1233a39.}}
**B.3** **melody** of an instrument, φόρμιγξ δ᾽ αὖ φθέγγοιθ᾽ ἱερὸν μ. ἠδὲ καὶ αὐλός {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 761; αὐλῶν πάμφωνον μ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 12.19; πηκτίδων μέλη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 241}}: generally, **tone**, μ. βοῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 756. [In “hymnus ad Mercurium” 502 θεὸς δ᾽ ὑπὸ καλὸν ἄεισεν must be read for θεὸς δ᾽ ὑπὸ μέλος ἄεισεν, and Ἕλλησιν δ᾽ ᾄδων μέλεα καὶ ἐλέγους}} is corrupt in epigram cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.7.6.]}}

3199:
** μέλω**,
_middle_ **μέλομαι**, used in both voices, either in _neuter_ sense, **to be an object of care** or **thought**, or in _active_ sense, **care for, take an interest in.**
**A** _present_ μέλω: _imperfect_ ἔμελον, _Epic dialect_ μέλον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.6:}}_future_ μελήσω, _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ μελησέμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.51:}}_aorist_ ἐμέλησα: _perfect_ μεμέληκ; also _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry μέμηλα, _Doric dialect_ _participle_ μεμᾱλώς uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.89 (for _Epic dialect_ forms of _middle_ see.below 111.2): almost always _3rd.pers.??}}_singular_ and _plural_, except in _present_ (see. below):—**to be an object of care** or **thought**, sometimes with a personal subject (not in _Attic dialect_ Prose):
**A.I** πᾶσι δόλοισιν ἀνθρώποισι μέλω by all manner of wiles **am I in** men's **thoughts**, i. e. **am well known** to them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.20; Ἀργὼ πᾶσι μέλουσα 12.70; μελήσεις ἄφθιτον ἀνθρώποις αἰὲν ἔχων ὄνομα 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 245; Εὐθυμίᾳ μέλων εἴην 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 155; μέλει σφισὶ Καλλιόπα [prev. author] “O.” 10(11).14; ἵνα θανοῦσα νερτέροισιν μέλω 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 850 (Lyric poetry); Ἔρως.. οὐρανίδαισι μέλων [prev. author] “Tr.” 842; μέλων πολλοῖσι “Anthologia Graeca” 5.121 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Diodorus Epigrammaticus); ἡ μέλουσα ἀγέλη 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 1.10a:}}_perfect_ _participle_, ἀρεταῖσι μεμαλότας **dear** to virtue, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.89 (uncertain)}}; μέλεγάρ οἱ [Ὀδυσσεύς] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.6; τὸν ξεῖνον δὲ ἐῶμεν... Τηλεμάχῳ μελέμεν 18.420: but more frequently of things, μή τοι ταῦτα... μελόντων}} **let** not these things **weigh on thy soul**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.463, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.362; μηδέ τί οἱ θάνατος μελέτω φρεσί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.152}}; σοὶ χρὴ τάδε πάντα μέλειν 'tis good these things **should be a care** to thee,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.490; πόλεμος δ᾽ ἄνδρεσσι μελήσει 6.492; μελήσουσιν δ᾽ ἐμοὶ ἵπποι 5.228; ᾧ τόσσα μέμηλε 2.25; οἷς ὕβρις μέμηλε κακή 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 238; τοῖσιν.. ἐνὶ φρεσὶν ἄλλα μεμήλει 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.151, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.614; ὅσα φημὶ μελησέμεν Ἀργείοισι 10.51; ἔλεγε.. κομιδῆς πέρι τὴν ὥρην αὐτῷ μελήσειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.19; μέλει γὰρ ἀνδρὶ.. τἄξωθεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 200; σοὶ χρὴ μέλειν ἐπιστολάς [prev. author] “Pr.” 3; οὗτος.. δμωσὶν ἂν μέλοι πόνος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 939; ἃ τοῖσιν ἀστοῖς ἔμελεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 459; τοῖσδε μελήσει γάμος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1342 (_anapaest meter_); τοῦτο ἴσασιν ἐμοὶ μεμεληκός 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 20.}}
**I.2** _impersonal_ with _infinitive_, οὐκ ἔμελέν μοι ταῦτα μεταλλῆσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.465; so in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1250, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.141, etc.; also, μοι ἐμέλησεν ὥστε εἰδέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.3.19}}: united with the personal construction, οὗτος μητρὶ κηδεύειν μέλει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 983.}}
**I.3** less frequently with a _conjunction_, οὐ μέλειν οἱ ὅτι ἀποθνῄσκει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.72}}; σοὶ μελέτω ὅκως.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.13, etc.; ὡς δὲ καλῶς ἕξει.., ἐμοὶ μελήσει [prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.2.13}}; ἐμοὶ τοῦτο μέλει, μὴ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1121 (Lyric poetry)}}; οὐ τοσοῦτόν μοι μέλει εἰ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.12.}}
**I.4** _3rd.pers._ _singular_ is frequently used _impersonal_ with the object in _genitive_, and _person_ in _dative_, ᾧ μέλει μάχας to whom **there is care** for the battle, who **careth** for it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 946 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ag.” 974; ἐμοὶ δ᾽ ἔλασσον Ζηνὸς ἢ μηδὲν μέλει [prev. author] “Pr.” 938; θεοῖσιν εἰ δίκης μέλει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1036; Ζηνὶ τῶν σῶν μέλει πόνων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 717; πάνυ μοι τυγχάνει μεμεληκὸς τοῦ ᾄσματος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 339b; also μέλει μοι περί τινος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 780, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 502, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.150d; μεμέληκέ μοι περὶ αὐτῶν [prev. author] “Cra.” 428b: less frequently with ὑπέρ, εἴπερ ὑπὲρ τοῦ κοινῇ βελτίστου δεῖ μέλειν ὑμῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.37.}}
**I.5** _absolutely_, μηδέ σοι μελησάτω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 334}}; οἶμαι θεοῖς τοῖς κάτω μέλειν, οἳ (nisi to be read οἷς) ἠδίκηνται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.31.}}
**I.6** frequently with a _negative_, οὐδέν μοι μέλει I **care** not, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 655; μή νυν μελέτω σοι μηδέν [prev. author] “Pl.” 208; τῷ δ᾽ οὐδὲν μ. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 178.2}}; so τί δέ σοι μέλε; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 73.10.}}
**A.II** μέλον ἔστι _periphrastic_ for μέλει, as τοῖσδ᾽ ἔσται μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 653, compare 1433.}}
**II.2** _neuter_ _participle_ used _absolutely_, οὐδὲν ἄρ᾽ ἐμοῦ μέλον for **they took** no **thought** of me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1288}}; δῆλον ὅτι οἶσθα, μέλον γέ σοι **since you care about it**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 24d; οὐδὲν αὐτῷ μ. τοῦ τοιούτου [prev. author] “Phdr.” 235a; μ. αὐτοῖς ἰσχυρῶς ὅπῃ τὸ μέλλον ἀποβήσοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.24; οὔτε σκοπούμεναι οὔτε μ. αὐταῖς ἄλλο ἢ χαρίζεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 501b.}}
**A.III** _middle_ is used by Poets and in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus like}}_active_, μελόμεθα, -ησόμεθα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 27}}; **to be an object of care**, Ἄρτεμιν ᾇ μελόμεσθα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 60}}: mostly in _3rd.pers._ _singular_, ἐμοὶ δέ κε ταῦτα μελήσεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.523}}; μή τί τοι ἡγεμόνος γε ποθὴ μελέσθω **let** it not **weigh on** thy **mind**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.505}}; τἀντεῦθεν.. αὐτῷ μελέσθω Λοξίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 61; τἀνθάδ᾽ ἂν μέλοιτ᾽ ἐμοί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1436; γάμους.. σοὶ χρὴ μέλεσθαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 759, etc.; ἰαχὰν μελομέναν νεκροῖς [prev. work] 1302}}: rarely _impersonal_, σοὶ.. μελέσθω φρουρῆσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 74; μέλεταί τινί τινος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.53, Oracle texts cited in 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 24.}}
**III.2** _Epic dialect_ _perfect_ and _pluperfect_ _passive_ **μέμβλεται**, **μέμβλετο** (from μέ-μλ-εται, μέ-μλ-ετο), with _present_ and _imperfect_ sense, ἦ νύ τοι οὐκέτι πάγχυ μετὰ φρεσὶ μέμβλετ᾽ Ἀχιλλεύς (for μέλει); {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.343}}; μέμβλετο γάρ οἱ τεῖχος (for ἔμελε){{Refs 8th c.BC+|21.516; φόνος δέ οἱ οὐκ ἐνὶ θυμῷ μέμβλετο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.12; ᾗσιν ἀοιδὴ μέμβλεται ἐν στήθεσσιν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 61: hence later _Epic dialect_ formed a}}_present_ μέμβλομαι, _2nd pers._ _plural_ μέμβλεσθε {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.217}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ μέμβλονται, in _active_ sense {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11 below), μ. πόνοισι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 4.77: the regular}}_perfect_ and _pluperfect_ (with _present_ and _imperfect_ sense) also occur in later Poets, μεμέληται {{Refs 1st c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.436; Φοίβῳ μεμελήμεθα “Anthologia Graeca” 10.17 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus); μεμέληνται Callias Epigrammaticus “anonymous fragment” 119, 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.349: 2 and 3}}_pluperfect_ μεμέλησο, -το, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.219 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 17.46}}; _participle_ μεμελημένος, α, ον, **cared for**, πολλοῖς μεμελημέναι ἡρωῖναι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] 26.36, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 7.199 (3rd c.BC: Tymnes Epigrammaticus):}}_aorist_ _participle_ _passive_ μεληθέν{{Refs|[prev. work] 5.200}}; compare βέβλεσθαι.
**B** with an object, **care for, take an interest in** a thing, with _genitive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in}}_perfect_ _participle_, μέγα πλούτοιο μεμηλώς **busied with, attending to**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.708; μέγα πτολέμοιο μεμηλώς 13.297}}: later in _present_, οὐκ ἔφα τις θεοὺς βροτῶν ἀξιοῦσθαι μέλειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 370 (Lyric poetry); μέλειν μὲν ἡμῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 689; δεινόν σε.. τικτούσης μέλειν [prev. author] “El.” 342}}: later with _dative_, **care for**, μέλω κύρτοις {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 10.10 (1st c.BC: Archias Jun.); θεοῖς μέλοντες 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 7}}: _absolutely_, **to be anxious**, μέλει.. κέαρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 288, compare “Pers.” 1049 (both Lyric poetry); μελούσῃ καρδίᾳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 770.}}
**B.2** rarely with _accusative_, πεντήκοντα βοῶν ἀντάξια ταῦτα μέμηλας **thou hastinvented,** {{Refs|hymnus ad Mercurium 437}} (perhaps μέμηδας).
**B.3** with _infinitive_, θεοὶ τῶν ἀδίκων μέλουσι (μέλλουσι codices _optative_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|καὶ τῶν ὁσίων ἐπᾴειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 773 (assuming variant).}}
**B.II** _middle_ μέλομαι, **care for, take care of**, with _genitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 177 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1466, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 109, “Heracl.” 354 (Lyric poetry), 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.967}}; τὰ λοιπά μου μέλου (where τὰ λ. is adverbial) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1138; μεμελημένοι ἀέθλων 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 4.101: with _dative_, ἐτητυμίῃ μεμελημένος 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.76; ἱππασίῃ μεμελημένον ἦτορ 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 4.500: with _accusative_, μέλομαι ῥόδον (probable reading for μέλπομαι) “Anacreontea” 53.2: with Preps., μέλεσθαι ἀμφί τι or τινος, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.376, 4.491; ἀμφ᾽ αἰγῶν μεμελημένοι “Anthologia Graeca” 6.221 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus); ἐμέλοντο περὶ σφίσιν 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1172}}: with _infinitive_, μέλομαι.. ἀείδειν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 65; μελέσθω λαὸς ἐκπονεῖν ἄκη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 367, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 96 (Lyric poetry)}}: _aorist_ in same sense, with _genitive_, τάφου μεληθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1184.}}
